{"version":"6.9","hitCount":1179,"nextCursorMark":"AoIIQJQROyg1NDYxNDY1Mg==","nextPageUrl":"https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=((LLMOps OR \"LLM observability\" OR \"large language model monitoring\" OR guardrails) AND (governance OR compliance OR audit)) AND FIRST_PDATE:[1900-01-01 TO 2026-07-31]&cursorMark=AoIIQJQROyg1NDYxNDY1Mg==&resultType=core&pageSize=100&format=json","request":{"queryString":"((LLMOps OR \"LLM observability\" OR \"large language model monitoring\" OR guardrails) AND (governance OR compliance OR audit)) AND FIRST_PDATE:[1900-01-01 TO 2026-07-31]","resultType":"core","cursorMark":"*","pageSize":100,"sort":"","synonym":false},"resultList":{"result":[{"id":"42333365","source":"MED","pmid":"42333365","pmcid":"PMC13283513","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13283513"]},"doi":"10.7759/cureus.109393","title":"Artificial Intelligence and Machine Learning in Hospital Quality Management, Patient Safety, and Accreditation Readiness: A Systematic Review and Narrative Synthesis.","authorString":"Marathe N, Ranjan M, Singh MP, Trigunait P, Prakash A, Kaushal N, Pawar A, Kshatriya GS, Dubey R.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Marathe N","firstName":"Nitin","lastName":"Marathe","initials":"N","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Hospital Administration, All India Institute of Medical Sciences, Nagpur, Nagpur, IND."}]}},{"fullName":"Ranjan M","firstName":"Manish","lastName":"Ranjan","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Microbiology, All India Institute of Medical Sciences, Jammu, Jammu, IND."}]}},{"fullName":"Singh MP","firstName":"Madhusudan P","lastName":"Singh","initials":"MP","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Pharmacology and Therapeutics, All India Institute of Medical Sciences, Deoghar, Deoghar, IND."}]}},{"fullName":"Trigunait P","firstName":"Pragati","lastName":"Trigunait","initials":"P","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology, All India Institute of Medical Sciences, Nagpur, Nagpur, IND."}]}},{"fullName":"Prakash A","firstName":"Avinash","lastName":"Prakash","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Anaesthesiology, All India Institute of Medical Sciences, Nagpur, Nagpur, IND."}]}},{"fullName":"Kaushal N","firstName":"Nitin","lastName":"Kaushal","initials":"N","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Community Medicine and Public Health, Postgraduate Institute of Medical Education and Research, Chandigarh, IND."}]}},{"fullName":"Pawar A","firstName":"Amit","lastName":"Pawar","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Transfusion Medicine, Military Hospital Doda, Jammu, IND."}]}},{"fullName":"Kshatriya GS","firstName":"Girish Singh","lastName":"Kshatriya","initials":"GS","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Transfusion Medicine, All India Institute of Medical Sciences, Deoghar, Deoghar, IND."}]}},{"fullName":"Dubey R","firstName":"Rounak","lastName":"Dubey","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Transfusion Medicine, All India Institute of Medical Sciences, Nagpur, Nagpur, IND."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0295-3262"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"5","volume":"18","journalIssueId":4188949,"dateOfPublication":"2026 May","monthOfPublication":5,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-05-01","journal":{"title":"Cureus","medlineAbbreviation":"Cureus","isoabbreviation":"Cureus","nlmid":"101596737","essn":"2168-8184","issn":"2168-8184"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e109393","abstractText":"As healthcare systems become increasingly data-driven, artificial intelligence (AI) and machine learning (ML) are being explored as tools to support quality management, patient safety, and accreditation. This systematic review synthesizes the available evidence across these domains through a structured literature search of major biomedical databases, encompassing studies published over the past decade and narratively synthesized to capture implementation context alongside technical performance. Three electronic databases were searched, and 23 studies were included. The available empirical evidence suggests that these technologies are most useful for repetitive, operationally well-defined, and digitally captured tasks, while local validation, acceptance testing, and continuous monitoring consistently emerge as prerequisites for safe institutional deployment. Governance capacity and organizational readiness are identified as equally determinative of outcomes as algorithmic performance itself. Collectively, the evidence supports positioning AI and ML as augmentative instruments within human-led quality governance structures, rather than as autonomous operational systems. Evidence was heterogeneous, with most empirical studies being single-center, retrospective, or implementation-focused. The risk-of-bias assessment indicated limited high-quality comparative evidence, limiting the strength of the pooled conclusions.","affiliation":"Hospital Administration, All India Institute of Medical Sciences, Nagpur, Nagpur, IND.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Quality assurance","Accreditation","Patient Safety","Machine Learning","Natural Language Processing","Deep Learning","Healthcare Governance","Hospital Quality Management"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.7759/cureus.109393"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13283513"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13283513?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-23","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-23","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-24","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-26","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-05-21","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-21"},{"id":"42349995","source":"MED","pmid":"42349995","pmcid":"PMC13311569","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13311569"]},"doi":"10.1136/bmjoq-2025-004073","title":"Value operating system: a practical framework to measure outcomes-per-cost at the episode level.","authorString":"Fuchs B, Vogel B, Studer G, Heesen P.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Fuchs B","firstName":"Bruno","lastName":"Fuchs","initials":"B","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6453-3947"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Health Sciences and Medicine, University of Lucerne, Lucerne, Switzerland fuchs@sarcoma.surgery."},{"affiliation":"Swiss Sarcoma Network SSN, University Teaching Hospital LUKS, Lucerne, Switzerland."}]}},{"fullName":"Vogel B","firstName":"Bettina","lastName":"Vogel","initials":"B","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Swiss Sarcoma Network SSN, University Teaching Hospital LUKS, Lucerne, Switzerland."}]}},{"fullName":"Studer G","firstName":"Gabriela","lastName":"Studer","initials":"G","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Health Sciences and Medicine, University of Lucerne, Lucerne, Switzerland."},{"affiliation":"Swiss Sarcoma Network SSN, University Teaching Hospital LUKS, Lucerne, Switzerland."}]}},{"fullName":"Heesen P","firstName":"Philip","lastName":"Heesen","initials":"P","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Medicine, University of Zurich, Zürich, Switzerland."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6453-3947"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"2","volume":"15","journalIssueId":4204777,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-06-01","journal":{"title":"BMJ open quality","medlineAbbreviation":"BMJ Open Qual","isoabbreviation":"BMJ Open Qual","nlmid":"101710381","essn":"2399-6641","issn":"2399-6641"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e004073","abstractText":"<h4>Objective</h4>To specify a value operating system (VOS) and its executable metric-the Value Index (VI)-that expresses risk-adjusted outcomes-per-episode-cost on a 0-100 scale with 95% CIs, including transparency guardrails and equity checks, and to outline two prospective exemplars for clinical deployment.<h4>Design</h4>Methods framework with prospective service evaluation and a predefined sensitivity plan (weight-elicitation variants; alternative risk models; cost-driver decomposition and subgroup equity).<h4>Setting</h4>Hospital-based integrated practice unit with hub-and-spoke pathways for sarcoma care; real-world-time data flows.<h4>Participants</h4>All consecutive episodes within the sarcoma care cycle eligible for routine audit (diagnosis through follow-up), without additional sampling beyond standard care.<h4>Interventions</h4>(1) Cross-cycle optimisation versus intensification of preoperative therapy using VI to compare alternative pathways; (2) causal machine learning-assisted targeting of radiotherapy, using VI to evaluate net value in treated patients compared with candidates identified through the benefit model.<h4>Main outcome measures</h4>Primary: VI (0-100) with 95% CIs. Secondary: component outcome and cost scores; cost-driver decomposition; data completeness/latency; subgroup equity.<h4>Results</h4>We define the VOS architecture, VI formulation, uncertainty treatment and governance guardrails (prospective registration, transparency of component scores/weights, equity checks and antigaming rules). We provide operational playbooks for both exemplars, including reporting templates and a priori sensitivity analyses to ensure interpretability and comparability over time and across subgroups.<h4>Conclusions</h4>VOS/VI deliver an auditable, episode-level value signal that links outcomes meaningful to patients with the full episode cost, enabling clinical teams to iteratively redesign pathways while maintaining stewardship. The prospective evaluations will test usability, fairness and impact on cost-drivers, supporting scale-up and value-aligned contracting.","affiliation":"Faculty of Health Sciences and Medicine, University of Lucerne, Lucerne, Switzerland fuchs@sarcoma.surgery.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Sarcoma","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"EC","qualifierName":"economics","majorTopic_YN":"N"},{"abbreviation":"TH","qualifierName":"therapy","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Prospective Studies"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Cost-Benefit Analysis","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Outcome Assessment, Health Care","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods","majorTopic_YN":"N"},{"abbreviation":"SN","qualifierName":"statistics & numerical data","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Value-Based Health Care"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Cost-effectiveness","Audit And Feedback","Healthcare Quality Improvement","Decision Support, Clinical","Outcome Assessment, Health Care"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1136/bmjoq-2025-004073"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13311569"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13311569?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by-nc","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["nct","doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-26","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-25","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-26","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-08-19","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-25","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-25"},{"id":"42308509","source":"MED","pmid":"42308509","pmcid":"PMC13324317","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13324317"]},"doi":"10.2196/92240","title":"Ethical Considerations in Personal Health Large Language Models.","authorString":"Liu J, Liu S.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Liu J","firstName":"Jialin","lastName":"Liu","initials":"J","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1369-4625"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Information Center, West China Hospital of Sichuan University, Chengdu, Sichuan, China."},{"affiliation":"Department of Otolaryngology-Head and Neck Surgery, West China Hospital of Sichuan University, Chengdu, Sichuan, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Liu S","firstName":"Siru","lastName":"Liu","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5003-5354"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biomedical Informatics, Vanderbilt University Medical Center, Nashville, TN, United States."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1369-4625"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5003-5354"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"28","journalIssueId":4204496,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-06-01","journal":{"title":"Journal of medical Internet research","medlineAbbreviation":"J Med Internet Res","isoabbreviation":"J Med Internet Res","nlmid":"100959882","essn":"1438-8871","issn":"1439-4456"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e92240","abstractText":"Personal health large language models (PH-LLMs) have rapidly evolved from research prototypes into consumer-facing, data-linked systems that support symptom triage, medication questions, mental health check-ins, and longitudinal self-management. Their direct-to-consumer use without clinical oversight creates a distinct ethical risk profile that general artificial intelligence governance frameworks do not fully address. This viewpoint focuses on text-based, platform-mediated PH-LLMs and synthesizes PH-LLM-specific challenges across 6 domains: privacy, accuracy, equity, transparency, human-artificial intelligence interaction, and regulatory governance. These risks may be amplified by health literacy gaps, longitudinal data aggregation, persuasive conversational design, and fragmented oversight across the consumer-clinical boundary. Grounded in the 4 principles of biomedical ethics, we propose a governance framework that operationalizes beneficence, nonmaleficence, autonomy, and justice through design and deployment controls, including health literacy-aligned communication, crisis and pharmacological safeguards, hallucination mitigation, role disclosure, granular consent, fairness auditing, and accessible design. We further outline implementation mechanisms, including risk-tiered certification, tiered accountability, and postdeployment oversight through adverse-event reporting, transparency reporting, and independent safety evaluation. This framework is intended as an evidence-informed but partly anticipatory approach to governing PH-LLMs in personal health management.","affiliation":"Information Center, West China Hospital of Sichuan University, Chengdu, Sichuan, China.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Artificial Intelligence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Health Literacy"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Large Language Models"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Ethics","Patient Safety","Health Literacy","Privacy","Governance","Health Equity","Digital Health","Generative Artificial Intelligence","Health Ai","Personal Health Large Language Model"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2196/92240"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13324317"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13324317?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-17","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-17","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-18","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-08-19","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-17","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-17"},{"id":"42272633","source":"MED","pmid":"42272633","pmcid":"PMC13216892","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13216892"]},"doi":"10.63116/ahisp-26-003","title":"Revolutionizing Health Care Analytics Through Data Democratization at America's Largest Municipal Health Care Delivery System.","authorString":"Sharma B, Abdul A, Phyu H, Sarao M, Nunez M.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Sharma B","firstName":"Bharti","lastName":"Sharma","initials":"B","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0003-8076-6128"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Data and AI, New York City Health + Hospitals."}]}},{"fullName":"Abdul A","firstName":"Adnan","lastName":"Abdul","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Data and AI, New York City Health + Hospitals."}]}},{"fullName":"Phyu H","firstName":"Hnin","lastName":"Phyu","initials":"H","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Data and AI, New York City Health + Hospitals."}]}},{"fullName":"Sarao M","firstName":"Maninder","lastName":"Sarao","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Data and AI, New York City Health + Hospitals."}]}},{"fullName":"Nunez M","firstName":"Maryam","lastName":"Nunez","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Data and AI, New York City Health + Hospitals."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0003-8076-6128"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"2","volume":"2","journalIssueId":4214783,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Advances in health information science and practice","medlineAbbreviation":"Adv Health Inf Sci Pract","isoabbreviation":"Adv Health Inf Sci Pract","nlmid":"9919050935306676","essn":"3066-5825","issn":"3066-5825"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"eahisp-26-003","abstractText":"In the rapidly evolving landscape of health care data utilization, achieving data democracy is crucial for enhancing organizational agility and decision-making effectiveness. In this article, the authors document the journey toward data democracy at America's largest public hospital system, highlighting the strategic alignment among leadership and stakeholders to secure buy-in and commitment. Beginning with early adopters and an initial roadmap, the initiative progressed through iterative updates informed by evolving strategies and stakeholder input. A crucial element of this journey was the establishment of <i>data champions</i>. These champions were thoughtfully engaged and diligently trained by the data and analytics team through persistent efforts. In this article, the authors explore key areas of focus for data champions, including the establishment of a data academy, development of support mechanisms, formulation of governance policies and technical guardrails, curation of structured data assets, and facilitation of access to tools and technology. Challenges encountered in data integration, accessibility, and quality underscored the critical role of robust governance structures and policy frameworks in sustaining data-driven decision-making. The insights shared in this article aim to guide other organizations in fostering a data-informed culture, learning from both challenges and successes.","affiliation":"Department of Data and AI, New York City Health + Hospitals.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Leadership"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Democracy"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Delivery of Health Care","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"OG","qualifierName":"organization & administration","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"United States"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Data Analytics"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Data Governance","Data Democratization","Data Academy/Literacy","Data Champions"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.63116/ahisp-26-003"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13216892"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13216892?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-12","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-11","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-11","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-10","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-05-28","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-28"},{"id":"41942581","source":"MED","pmid":"41942581","pmcid":"PMC13053716","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13053716"]},"doi":"10.1038/s41746-026-02621-6","title":"Guardrails for GenAI drafted replies in patient portal messaging.","authorString":"Sakr J.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Sakr J","firstName":"Johnny","lastName":"Sakr","initials":"J","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0009-0732-6373"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"College of Law, Prince Sultan University, Riyadh, Saudi Arabia. jsakr@psu.edu.sa."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0009-0732-6373"}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"9","journalIssueId":4171893,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Apr","monthOfPublication":4,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-04-01","journal":{"title":"NPJ digital medicine","medlineAbbreviation":"NPJ Digit Med","isoabbreviation":"NPJ Digit Med","nlmid":"101731738","essn":"2398-6352","issn":"2398-6352"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"283","affiliation":"College of Law, Prince Sultan University, Riyadh, Saudi Arabia. jsakr@psu.edu.sa.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Letter"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"Prince Sultan University","orderIn":0}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41746-026-02621-6"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13053716"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13053716?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by-nc-nd","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-04-06","dateOfCreation":"2026-04-06","firstIndexDate":"2026-04-07","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-04-08","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-04-06","firstPublicationDate":"2026-04-06"},{"id":"41837238","source":"MED","pmid":"41837238","pmcid":"PMC12979488","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12979488"]},"doi":"10.3389/frai.2026.1759211","title":"Audit-as-code: a policy-as-code framework for continuous AI assurance.","authorString":"Muhammad AE, Yow KC, Alsenan S.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Muhammad AE","firstName":"Aoun E","lastName":"Muhammad","initials":"AE","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0007-4127-1260"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Engineering and Applied Science, University of Regina, Regina, SK, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Yow KC","firstName":"Kin-Choong","lastName":"Yow","initials":"KC","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Engineering and Applied Science, University of Regina, Regina, SK, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Alsenan S","firstName":"Shrooq","lastName":"Alsenan","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Information Systems, College of Computer and Information Sciences, Princess Nourah Bint Abdurahman University, Riyadh, Saudi Arabia."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0007-4127-1260"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"9","journalIssueId":4117457,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Frontiers in artificial intelligence","medlineAbbreviation":"Front Artif Intell","isoabbreviation":"Front Artif Intell","nlmid":"101770551","essn":"2624-8212","issn":"2624-8212"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1759211","abstractText":"<h4>Introduction</h4>Existing AI assurance and governance frameworks rely heavily on documented written policies and manual reviews of the implementation. The primary challenge is not the length of these documents, but to operationalize the gap from transforming qualitative requirements into verifiable controls. This approach makes ensuring continuous compliance through the development life cycle hard to enforce, scale, and reproduce.<h4>Methods</h4>This study presents a continuous assurance framework called Audit-as-Code that maps governance requirements to technically-auditable rules, that can be a combination of versioned policy specification and executable checks for evidence artifacts, linked to structured evidence regarding data, models, provenance, performance, decisions, and explanations regarding the decisions being made. While the framework addresses the governance and regulatory mapping requirements, the primary focus of this study is MLOps/CI-CD (continuous integration/continuous delivery) operationalization, and turning these requirements into deterministic checks and gate decisions integrated in operational workflows. In this study, we introduce an assured readiness score that integrates the governance risk with other key responsible AI principles, such as traceability and explainability. This approach helps in aligning deployment decisions with predefined risk tiers, and the framework automates decisions on whether a system can proceed, requires remediation and fixes, or should be blocked. It also provides targeted suggestions for improvement and compliance for the lags identified.<h4>Results</h4>We evaluated this framework on representative AI systems and demonstrated how a single evidence bundle can be used to support assessment across different governance regulations.<h4>Discussion</h4>In doing so, Audit-as-Code ensures AI assurance transforms from a documentation-driven policy module to a quantitative, auditable, reproducible, and operationally practical module to ensure compliance.","affiliation":"Faculty of Engineering and Applied Science, University of Regina, Regina, SK, Canada.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Reproducibility","Compliance","Traceability","Governance","Explainability","Ai Assurance","Ci/Cd","Policy-As-Code"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/frai.2026.1759211"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12979488"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12979488?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-03-16","dateOfCreation":"2026-03-16","firstIndexDate":"2026-03-17","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-03-14","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-02-26","firstPublicationDate":"2026-02-26"},{"id":"42091170","source":"MED","pmid":"42091170","pmcid":"PMC13150866","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13150866"]},"doi":"10.1136/bmjhci-2025-101921","title":"Self-regulating the use of large language models in clinical practice: a risk-stratified approach.","authorString":"Mohammad M, Jimenez-Solem E, Hejmadi M, Pihl A.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Mohammad M","firstName":"Milan","lastName":"Mohammad","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2227-9375"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Centre for Physical Activity Research, Copenhagen University Hospital - Rigshospitalet, Copenhagen, Denmark milanmohammad@hotmail.com."},{"affiliation":"Department of Cellular and Molecular Medicine, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, University of Copenhagen, Copenhagen, Denmark."},{"affiliation":"Department of Plastic and Breast Surgery, Zealand University Hospital, Roskilde, Denmark."}]}},{"fullName":"Jimenez-Solem E","firstName":"Espen","lastName":"Jimenez-Solem","initials":"E","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3777-147X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Clinical Pharmacology, Bispebjerg and Frederiksberg Hospital, Copenhagen, Denmark."},{"affiliation":"Department of Clinical Medicine, University of Copenhagen, Copenhagen, Denmark."},{"affiliation":"Copenhagen Phase IV Unit (Phase4CPH), Department of Clinical Pharmacology, Center for Clinical Research and Prevention, Bispebjerg and Frederiksberg Hospital, Copenhagen, Denmark."}]}},{"fullName":"Hejmadi M","firstName":"Michael","lastName":"Hejmadi","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Lægehuset Ferritslev, Funen, Denmark."},{"affiliation":"Research Unit for General Practice and Centre for Digital Health Research, University of Southern Denmark, Odense, Denmark."}]}},{"fullName":"Pihl A","firstName":"Andreas","lastName":"Pihl","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Roche Diagnostics AS, Copenhagen, Denmark."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3777-147X"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2227-9375"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"33","journalIssueId":4190954,"dateOfPublication":"2026 May","monthOfPublication":5,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-05-01","journal":{"title":"BMJ health & care informatics","medlineAbbreviation":"BMJ Health Care Inform","isoabbreviation":"BMJ Health Care Inform","nlmid":"101745500","essn":"2632-1009","issn":"2632-1009"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e101921","abstractText":"The rapid integration of large language models (LLMs) into clinical practice offers promising benefits, including assistance with documentation, decision support and patient communication. However, these advantages are tempered by concerns around model accuracy, data privacy, clinician trust and regulatory responsibility. In this comment, we propose a clinician-led, risk-stratified framework to guide responsible adoption of LLMs in healthcare. The framework categorises applications into four risk tiers: low risk, moderate risk, high risk and critical risk. Each tier demands tailored oversight, validation and governance, with increasing levels of statutory regulatory scrutiny (eg., European Union Artificial Intelligence Act and U.S. Food and Drug Administration) and clinical supervision. We argue that proactive self-regulation combined with ongoing quality management and clinician education is essential to safely integrate LLMs into ongoing care.","affiliation":"Centre for Physical Activity Research, Copenhagen University Hospital - Rigshospitalet, Copenhagen, Denmark milanmohammad@hotmail.com.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["discussion","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Artificial Intelligence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Decision Support Systems, Clinical"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Large Language Models"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","information technology","Health Information Management","Decision Support Systems, Clinical","Implementation Science"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1136/bmjhci-2025-101921"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13150866"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13150866?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":1,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"N","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by-nc","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-07-16","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-06","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-13","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-20","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-05-06","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-06"},{"id":"42205846","source":"MED","pmid":"42205846","pmcid":"PMC13186148","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13186148"]},"doi":"10.30953/bhty.v9.430","title":"Zero-Knowledge Process Verification: A Comprehensive Framework for a Distributed Healthcare System.","authorString":"Krishnasamy S.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Krishnasamy S","firstName":"Sathya","lastName":"Krishnasamy","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0000-1420-1098"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"President and Principal, ChainAim, Newington, Connecticut, USA."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0000-1420-1098"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"9","journalIssueId":4202830,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Blockchain in healthcare today","medlineAbbreviation":"Blockchain Healthc Today","isoabbreviation":"Blockchain Healthc Today","nlmid":"101752951","essn":"2573-8240","issn":"2573-8240"}},"pubYear":"2026","abstractText":"<h4>Objective</h4>This article introduces the ZK-PRET Business Process Prover framework that integrates Object Management Group (OMG) business process standards with zero-knowledge cryptographic verification to enable privacy-preserving healthcare process compliance across distributed systems.<h4>Methods</h4>We developed a multilayer architecture combining formal business process modeling, zero-knowledge proof generation, and regulatory compliance verification. The framework extends established OMG standards with cryptographic verification capabilities to achieve verifiable compliance, privacy preservation, and regulatory accountability. Implementation testing were conducted in synthetic data environments designed to represent real-world healthcare scenarios.<sup>1</sup> These environments enable comprehensive modeling and testing of multi-entity process orchestration patterns while maintaining privacy protections essential for healthcare research and development. All scenarios, clinical examples, and process expressions presented in this article utilize synthetic data to ensure no real patient data, clinical records, or identifiable health information were used.<h4>Results</h4>The ZK-PRET Business Process Prover framework demonstrates practical applicability across many healthcare domains, including treatment planning, telemedicine coordination, healthcare administration, consumer health services, multientity clinical trials, and supply chain management. Implementation results demonstrate cryptographic verification capabilities that enable mathematical prevention of regulatory violations rather than post hoc detection. The results demonstrate configurable privacy preservation through zero-knowledge verification and consistent proof sizes suitable for modeling complex orchestrations, while leveraging already widely used Web 2 process models suitable for multiple runtime deployment topologies.<h4>Conclusions</h4>Zero-knowledge healthcare process verification represents a foundational technology for regulatory compliance in distributed healthcare systems. While agentic AI systems present important opportunities for automation, the underlying requirement for verifiable process compliance through cryptographic means brings broader challenges. ZK-PRET Business Process Prover addresses these challenges in healthcare transformative flows, enabling safer deployment of autonomous systems while maintaining regulatory standards.","affiliation":"President and Principal, ChainAim, Newington, Connecticut, USA.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Healthcare Compliance","Privacy Preservation","Distributed Ledger Technology","Healthcare Interoperability","Zero-knowledge Proofs","Ai Agents","Bpmn 2.0","Regulatory Verification"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.30953/bhty.v9.430"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13186148"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13186148?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by-nc","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-05-28","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-28","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-28","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-27","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-05-02","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-02"},{"id":"42341297","source":"MED","pmid":"42341297","pmcid":"PMC13293561","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13293561"]},"doi":"10.2196/88058","title":"Enhancing Physician Resilience to Generative AI: Multilevel Framework for Shared Authority, Verification, and Skill Preservation.","authorString":"Pan H, Liu J, Liu S.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Pan H","firstName":"Hongxia","lastName":"Pan","initials":"H","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2215-525X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Rehabilitation Medicine Center and Institute of Rehabilitation Medicine, West China Hospital of Sichuan University, Chengdu, Sichuan, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Liu J","firstName":"Jialin","lastName":"Liu","initials":"J","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1369-4625"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medical Informatics, West China Hospital of Sichuan University, Guoxue Xiang Street, Chengdu, Sichuan, 610041, China, 86 28-85422416."},{"affiliation":"Department of Otolaryngology-Head and Neck Surgery, West China Hospital of Sichuan University, Chengdu, Sichuan, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Liu S","firstName":"Siru","lastName":"Liu","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5003-5354"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biomedical Informatics, Vanderbilt University Medical Center, Nashville, TN, United States."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1369-4625"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5003-5354"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2215-525X"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"28","journalIssueId":4204496,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-06-01","journal":{"title":"Journal of medical Internet research","medlineAbbreviation":"J Med Internet Res","isoabbreviation":"J Med Internet Res","nlmid":"100959882","essn":"1438-8871","issn":"1439-4456"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e88058","abstractText":"<h4>Unlabelled</h4>As generative artificial intelligence (AI), particularly large language model-based tools, is increasingly integrated into diagnosis, triage, decision support, and treatment planning, it offers potential gains in efficiency and information access. However, real-world deployment also introduces important risks, including hallucinations, miscalibrated confidence, automation bias, and increased verification burden on physicians. This burden may divert attention from independent clinical reasoning, contribute to deskilling, and increase vulnerability when models fail silently or perform poorly in unfamiliar clinical contexts. Existing AI governance frameworks emphasize data quality, transparency, accountability, and ethical deployment, but pay less attention to physician-facing resilience, defined in this paper as the capacity to sustain independent and safe clinical judgment when collaborating with generative AI. In this viewpoint, we propose a multilevel governance framework organized around 3 coordinated domains: cognitive workload shaping, clinical authority governance and allocation, and organizational safety governance and accountability. Together, these domains aim to reduce verification burden, preserve physician decisional authority, and align institutional oversight with safe and context-sensitive AI use. The framework includes mechanisms such as risk-sensitive verification triggers, bounded delegation, structured interprofessional review, and organizational monitoring to support safe clinical integration while minimizing avoidable workflow disruption. At the same time, implementation may be limited by workflow friction, alert fatigue, variable institutional resources, and the need for ongoing monitoring and recalibration to ensure that safeguards remain clinically useful rather than burdensome. Accordingly, this paper outlines a structured governance framework to guide safer integration of generative AI into clinical care and inform future evaluation across specialties, workflows, and institutional settings.","affiliation":"Rehabilitation Medicine Center and Institute of Rehabilitation Medicine, West China Hospital of Sichuan University, Chengdu, Sichuan, China.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Clinical Competence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Artificial Intelligence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Physicians","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"PX","qualifierName":"psychology","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Generative Artificial Intelligence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Large Language Models"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","AI","Physician","Accountability","Cognitive Load","Authority","Clinical Safety","Generative Artificial Intelligence","Generative Ai"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2196/88058"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13293561"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13293561?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-24","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-24","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-25","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-26","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-24","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-24"},{"id":"PPR1244975","source":"PPR","doi":"10.21203/rs.3.rs-9890341/v1","title":"A Layered Guardrail Architecture for Agentic AI in Cloud-Native DevOps Pipelines: Design, Implementation, and Empirical Evaluation on Live AWS Infrastructure","authorString":"POTLURI M.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"POTLURI M","firstName":"MANVITHA","lastName":"POTLURI","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0006-3209-7857"}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0006-3209-7857"}]},"pubYear":"2026","abstractText":"<title>Abstract</title>  <p>Autonomous AI agents capable of executing cloud 2 infrastructure operations introduce a new class of risk that 3 existing safety mechanisms address incompletely. LLM output 4 filters govern what the model says but not what the agent does; 5 static admission controllers enforce rules at deployment time 6 but not at agent runtime; human oversight mechanisms are 7 decoupled from real-time execution pipelines. This paper presents 8 a five-layer guardrail architecture intercepting at the tool-call 9 execution boundary, combining: (1) AWS Bedrock Guardrails 10 for LLM output interception; (2) Open Policy Agent (OPA) 11 evaluation against live infrastructure state; (3) confidence-delta 12 scoring via A/B model comparison; (4) human-in-the-loop (HITL) 13 approval gating with DynamoDB time-to-live auto-rejection; 14 and (5) CloudWatch audit telemetry with S3 rollback. Em15 pirical evaluation on live AWS (us-east-1) across 100 prompts 16 spanning five risk categories including 20 adversarial jailbreak 17 variants demonstrates 96% overall accuracy, 1% false positive 18 rate, 0% false negative rate on destructive operations, and 19 a 22× latency reduction for intercepted actions (347 ms vs. 20 8,099 ms). An iterative calibration study across four tuning cycles 21 documents convergence from 60% to 96% accuracy, providing 22 empirical evidence that multi-layer defense-in-depth is necessary 23 rather than merely sufficient for production-grade DevOps agent 24 governance. Total evaluation cost: $0.0017 USD. Implementation: 25 https://github.com/ManvithaP-hub/agentic-devops-guardrails</p>","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Preprint"]},"bookOrReportDetails":{"publisher":"Research Square","yearOfPublication":2026},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.21203/rs.3.rs-9890341/v1"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","versionList":{"version":[{"id":"PPR1244975","source":"PPR","firstPublishDate":"2026-06-03","versionNumber":1,"pubTypeList":{"pubType":["preprint"]},"hasEvaluations":"N"}]},"versionNumber":1,"hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-04","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-04","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-03"},{"id":"42080195","source":"MED","pmid":"42080195","pmcid":"PMC13129269","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13129269"]},"doi":"10.1177/17562848261441693","title":"Responsible use of large language models in gastroenterology and hepatology.","authorString":"Khanna S.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Khanna S","firstName":"Sahil","lastName":"Khanna","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7619-8338"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Gastroenterology and Hepatology, Mayo Clinic, 200 1st Street SW, Rochester, MN 55905, USA."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7619-8338"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"19","journalIssueId":4085889,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Therapeutic advances in gastroenterology","medlineAbbreviation":"Ther Adv Gastroenterol","isoabbreviation":"Ther Adv Gastroenterol","nlmid":"101478893","essn":"1756-2848","issn":"1756-283X"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"17562848261441693","abstractText":"Large language models (LLMs) and related generative artificial intelligence (AI) systems are rapidly entering clinical workflows, including in gastroenterology and hepatology, where text-heavy documentation, guideline-driven care, and high-volume patient messaging create a strong demand for decision support and automation. Deployment raises distinctive risks: hallucinations and unsafe recommendations, automation bias, privacy and confidentiality threats, inequitable care, intellectual property and licensing uncertainty, and unclear allocation of responsibility across clinicians, institutions, and vendors. Regulators are increasingly emphasizing a lifecycle approach to trustworthy AI, including validation, risk management, human oversight, cybersecurity, monitoring, and transparency. Frameworks for healthcare protection regulate how health information may be shared with external model providers and how outputs should be logged, audited, and retained. This review synthesizes practical guidance for responsible LLM use, organized around (1) tiered use cases and risk stratification; (2) ethical principles (beneficence, non-maleficence, autonomy, justice, and accountability); (3) core legal and regulatory considerations; and (4) operational governance, evaluation, and monitoring strategies. Actionable checklists to support institutional adoption, use, and transparency are provided. Responsible use requires aligning LLM capabilities with risk, preventing inappropriate data sharing, validating performance in representative populations, ensuring human oversight and clear accountability, mitigating harm, and maintaining an evidence-based, continuously monitored deployment lifecycle.","affiliation":"Division of Gastroenterology and Hepatology, Mayo Clinic, 200 1st Street SW, Rochester, MN 55905, USA.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Regulation","Ethics","Gastroenterology","Hepatology","Law","Privacy","Clinical Decision Support","Governance","Generative Artificial Intelligence","Large Language Models"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1177/17562848261441693"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13129269"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13129269?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by-nc","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-05-04","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-04","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-13","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-19","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-04-26","firstPublicationDate":"2026-04-26"},{"id":"42049553","source":"MED","pmid":"42049553","doi":"10.1016/j.jnma.2026.03.009","title":"The governance gap no one is solving.","authorString":"DeShazer C.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"DeShazer C","firstName":"Charles","lastName":"DeShazer","initials":"C","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Managing Partner, Nuvanta Consulting Group, 6800 Wisconsin Ave Suite #1230 Chevy Chase, MD 20815. Electronic address: charles@nuvantacg.com."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"3","volume":"118","journalIssueId":4208782,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-06-01","journal":{"title":"Journal of the National Medical Association","medlineAbbreviation":"J Natl Med Assoc","isoabbreviation":"J Natl Med Assoc","nlmid":"7503090","essn":"1943-4693","issn":"0027-9684"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"458-462","abstractText":"Artificial intelligence (AI) is rapidly entering clinical practice, yet the governance models needed to ensure its safe, ethical, and equitable use have not kept pace-particularly in community and safety-net settings. Existing frameworks, designed for large academic systems, are often impractical for frontline physicians, creating a dangerous gap between AI adoption and oversight. This article reframes AI governance as a clinician-centered enabler rather than a compliance burden. We propose a pragmatic model built on clear accountability, defined use guardrails, basic safety and bias checks, transparency, and lightweight workflows-supported by a scalable hub-and-spoke approach leveraging trusted professional organizations. Without intentional governance, AI risks amplifying disparities and eroding trust. Done well, it becomes a force multiplier-extending high-quality, equitable care into the communities that need it most. For community physicians, AI governance is not optional; it is essential to protecting patients, preserving clinical judgment, and ensuring that innovation advances equity rather than harm.","affiliation":"Managing Partner, Nuvanta Consulting Group, 6800 Wisconsin Ave Suite #1230 Chevy Chase, MD 20815. Electronic address: charles@nuvantacg.com.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Medicine"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Artificial Intelligence","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"LJ","qualifierName":"legislation & jurisprudence","majorTopic_YN":"N"},{"abbreviation":"ES","qualifierName":"ethics","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Health Equity","Clinical Safety","Community Practice","Ai Governance"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jnma.2026.03.009"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"N","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-08","dateOfCreation":"2026-04-28","firstIndexDate":"2026-04-30","dateOfRevision":"2026-06-09","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-04-27","firstPublicationDate":"2026-04-27"},{"id":"42079468","source":"MED","pmid":"42079468","pmcid":"PMC13132845","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13132845"]},"doi":"10.3389/frhs.2026.1771744","title":"Trust and trade: patient perspectives on the ethics of real-world data monetisation.","authorString":"Sagkriotis A.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Sagkriotis A","firstName":"Alexandros","lastName":"Sagkriotis","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Independent Consultant in Real-World Evidence and Health Data Science, Helios Academy Limited | Where Science Meets Compassion, London, United Kingdom."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"6","journalIssueId":4117504,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Frontiers in health services","medlineAbbreviation":"Front Health Serv","isoabbreviation":"Front Health Serv","nlmid":"9918334887706676","essn":"2813-0146","issn":"2813-0146"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1771744","abstractText":"Real-world data (RWD) and real-world evidence (RWE) are increasingly used to inform regulatory decisions, health technology assessment, and health system planning. However, patients whose data underpin these activities often experience limited transparency or benefit when their information is monetised. While regulatory and HTA frameworks emphasise methodological rigor and analytical transparency, they provide limited guidance on fairness, reciprocity, and legitimacy from a patient perspective. This Policy Brief examines this governance gap and argues that evidence integrity must extend beyond technical standards to include ethical stewardship and public trust. Drawing on policy contexts from UK, EU, and North America, it proposes five pragmatic safeguards to strengthen transparency, accountability, and patient-centred governance in secondary data use, supporting the sustainability and legitimacy of RWE infrastructures as data initiatives expand.","affiliation":"Independent Consultant in Real-World Evidence and Health Data Science, Helios Academy Limited | Where Science Meets Compassion, London, United Kingdom.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["brief-report","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Ethics","Consent","Governance","Patient Trust","Real-world Evidence (Rwe)","Real-world Data (Rwd)","Data Monetisation","Evidence Integrity"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/frhs.2026.1771744"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13132845"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13132845?pdf=render"}]},"commentCorrectionList":{"commentCorrection":[{"id":"PPR1121897","source":"PPR","type":"Preprint in","note":"Link created based on a title-first author match","orderIn":10002}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":1,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-05-04","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-04","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-13","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-19","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-04-17","firstPublicationDate":"2026-04-17"},{"id":"41975498","source":"MED","pmid":"41975498","pmcid":"PMC13192042","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13192042"]},"doi":"10.1186/s13012-026-01503-5","title":"AI Methods for Implementation Science (AIM-IS): developing a framework, toolkit, and reporting standard for the responsible use of AI in implementation practice and research.","authorString":"Fontaine G, Michie S, Beidas RS, Geng E, Fahim C, Powell BJ, Welch V, Thomas J, Chan J, Abbasgholizadeh-Rahimi S, Légaré F, Hastings J, Lambert SD, Presseau J, Straus SE, An R, Saran A, Taylor N.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Fontaine G","firstName":"Guillaume","lastName":"Fontaine","initials":"G","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Ingram School of Nursing, Faculty of Medicine and Health Sciences, McGill University, 680 Sherbrooke West, #1812, Montréal, QC, H3A 2M7, Canada. guil.fontaine@mcgill.ca."},{"affiliation":"Centre for Clinical Epidemiology, Lady Davis Institute for Medical Research, Montréal, QC, Canada. guil.fontaine@mcgill.ca."},{"affiliation":"Methodological and Implementation Research Program, Ottawa Hospital Research Institute, Ottawa, ON, Canada. guil.fontaine@mcgill.ca."},{"affiliation":"Kirby Institute, UNSW Sydney, Sydney, NSW, Australia. guil.fontaine@mcgill.ca."}]}},{"fullName":"Michie S","firstName":"Susan","lastName":"Michie","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Centre for Behaviour Change, University College London, London, UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Beidas RS","firstName":"Rinad S","lastName":"Beidas","initials":"RS","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medical Social Sciences, Feinberg School of Medicine, Northwestern University, Chicago, IL, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Geng E","firstName":"Elvin","lastName":"Geng","initials":"E","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Infectious Diseases, School of Medicine, Washington University in St. Louis, St. Louis, MO, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Fahim C","firstName":"Christine","lastName":"Fahim","initials":"C","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Li Ka Shing Knowledge Institute, Unity Health Toronto, Toronto, ON, Canada."},{"affiliation":"Institute of Health Policy, Management, and Evaluation, University of Toronto, Toronto, ON, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Powell BJ","firstName":"Byron J","lastName":"Powell","initials":"BJ","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Brown School, Washington University in St. Louis, St. Louis, MO, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Welch V","firstName":"Vivian","lastName":"Welch","initials":"V","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Epidemiology and Public Health, University of Ottawa, Ottawa, ON, Canada."},{"affiliation":"Bruyère Health Research Institute, Ottawa, ON, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Thomas J","firstName":"James","lastName":"Thomas","initials":"J","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4805-4190"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"EPPI Centre, Social Research Institute, University College London, London, UK."},{"affiliation":"Institute of Education, University College London, London, UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Chan J","firstName":"Jeffery","lastName":"Chan","initials":"J","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7521-9504"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Population Health, UNSW Sydney, Sydney, NSW, Australia."}]}},{"fullName":"Abbasgholizadeh-Rahimi S","firstName":"Samira","lastName":"Abbasgholizadeh-Rahimi","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Family Medicine, McGill University, Montreal, QC, Canada."},{"affiliation":"Mila - Quebec Artificial Intelligence Institute, Montréal, QC, Canada."},{"affiliation":"Faculty of Dental Medicine and Oral Health Sciences, McGill University, Montreal, QC, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Légaré F","firstName":"France","lastName":"Légaré","initials":"F","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Family and Emergency Medicine, Université Laval, Québec City, QC, Canada."},{"affiliation":"VITAM Research Centre for Sustainable Health, Québec City, QC, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Hastings J","firstName":"Janna","lastName":"Hastings","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Idiap Research Institute, Martigny, Switzerland."},{"affiliation":"Swiss Institute of Bioinformatics, Lausanne, Switzerland."}]}},{"fullName":"Lambert SD","firstName":"Sylvie D","lastName":"Lambert","initials":"SD","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Ingram School of Nursing, Faculty of Medicine and Health Sciences, McGill University, 680 Sherbrooke West, #1812, Montréal, QC, H3A 2M7, Canada."},{"affiliation":"St. Mary's Research Centre, CIUSSS West-Montreal, Montréal, QC, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Presseau J","firstName":"Justin","lastName":"Presseau","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Methodological and Implementation Research Program, Ottawa Hospital Research Institute, Ottawa, ON, Canada."},{"affiliation":"School of Epidemiology and Public Health, University of Ottawa, Ottawa, ON, Canada."},{"affiliation":"School of Psychology, University of Ottawa, Ottawa, ON, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Straus SE","firstName":"Sharon E","lastName":"Straus","initials":"SE","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6106-832X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Li Ka Shing Knowledge Institute, Unity Health Toronto, Toronto, ON, Canada."},{"affiliation":"Institute of Health Policy, Management, and Evaluation, University of Toronto, Toronto, ON, Canada."},{"affiliation":"Department of Medicine, University of Toronto, Toronto, ON, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"An R","firstName":"Ruopeng","lastName":"An","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Silver School of Social Work, New York University, New York, NY, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Saran A","firstName":"Ashrita","lastName":"Saran","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Global Development Network, New Delhi, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Taylor N","firstName":"Natalie","lastName":"Taylor","initials":"N","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0280-0883"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Population Health, UNSW Sydney, Sydney, NSW, Australia."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0280-0883"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6106-832X"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7521-9504"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4805-4190"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"21","journalIssueId":4192846,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Apr","monthOfPublication":4,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-04-01","journal":{"title":"Implementation science : IS","medlineAbbreviation":"Implement Sci","isoabbreviation":"Implement Sci","nlmid":"101258411","essn":"1748-5908","issn":"1748-5908"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"37","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Artificial intelligence (AI), including machine learning, natural language processing, and large language models, may support implementation practice and research in tasks such as evidence synthesis, determinant assessment, strategy selection, monitoring, adaptation, and theory development. However, these applications of AI do not form a single, uniform category. They span a continuum from practice-facing applications that support local implementation work to research- and methods-facing applications that support evidence generation and synthesis. The guidance on how to classify, evaluate, and report these uses of AI remains limited. The AI Methods for Implementation Science (AIM-IS) program aims to develop, validate, and maintain a suite of products to guide the responsible use of AI across implementation practice, implementation research, and bridging use cases.<h4>Methods</h4>AIM-IS is a multi-phase, multi-method methodological development program. The unit of analysis is the AI-for-implementation use case: a specific AI capability supporting a defined implementation practice or research task within a workflow, decision point, and governance context. Phase 1 is a living scoping review mapping published AI use cases in implementation science, including how they are evaluated and what risks they raise. Phase 2 is a qualitative interview study with implementation researchers, practitioners, AI experts, community members, and data infrastructure and governance experts to refine use cases and identify feasibility constraints, outcome priorities, and reporting needs. Phase 3 will integrate findings from Phases 1 and 2 to develop the draft AIM-IS products, including a framework, a taxonomy of use cases, guardrails for responsible use, a practical guide, outcome domains, and reporting items. Phase 4 will use an eDelphi process and consensus meeting to refine and finalize these products. Phase 5 will conduct usability testing to improve clarity and ease of use, resulting in the finalized AIM-IS products. AIM-IS is informed by implementation science, sociotechnical systems, equity, and responsible AI frameworks, and includes a living-update approach to support ongoing refinement.<h4>Discussion</h4>The AIM-IS program will deliver a suite of products, including a framework, toolkit and reporting standard, to support the specification, governance, evaluation, and reporting of AI in implementation science. Together, these products aim to strengthen transparency, comparability, accountability, and attention to equity in how AI is used by implementation practitioners and researchers over time.<h4>Registration</h4>Open Science Framework, March 15, 2026: https://doi.org/10.17605/OSF.IO/BX35K.","affiliation":"Ingram School of Nursing, Faculty of Medicine and Health Sciences, McGill University, 680 Sherbrooke West, #1812, Montréal, QC, H3A 2M7, Canada. guil.fontaine@mcgill.ca.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Artificial Intelligence","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"ST","qualifierName":"standards","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Implementation Science"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Methodology","Machine Learning","Reporting Guideline","Implementation Research","Implementation Practice","Generative Ai","Large Language Models"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s13012-026-01503-5"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13192042"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13192042?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":1,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by-nc-nd","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-07-15","dateOfCreation":"2026-04-14","firstIndexDate":"2026-04-14","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-22","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-04-14","firstPublicationDate":"2026-04-14"},{"id":"42418625","source":"MED","pmid":"42418625","doi":"10.1056/cat.25.0377","title":"Blueprint for Safety: Implementing a Clinically Governed AI Digital Assistant for Patient Guidance.","authorString":"Boogaard CO, Marshall J, Shah A, Razmpour O, Pastor Larrieta H, Parekh A.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Boogaard CO","firstName":"Claire O'Connell","lastName":"Boogaard","initials":"CO","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Patient Safety Officer, Included Health, San Francisco, CA, USA."},{"affiliation":"Adjunct Associate Professor, The George Washington University School of Medicine and Health Sciences, Washington, DC, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Marshall J","firstName":"Jaclyn","lastName":"Marshall","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Senior Health Economics and Outcomes Research Director, Included Health, San Francisco, CA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Shah A","firstName":"Ankoor","lastName":"Shah","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Vice President, Clinical Excellence, Included Health, San Francisco, CA, USA."},{"affiliation":"Adjunct Assistant Professor, Department of Pediatrics, Morehouse School of Medicine, Atlanta, GA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Razmpour O","firstName":"Omid","lastName":"Razmpour","initials":"O","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Visiting Ph.D. Student, Sloan School of Management, Massacusetts Institute of Technology, Cambridge, MA, USA."},{"affiliation":"Former Nurse and Ph.D/M.B.A. Candidate, Nell Hodgson Woodruff School of Nursing, Emory University, Atlanta, GA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Pastor Larrieta H","firstName":"Hasier","lastName":"Pastor Larrieta","initials":"H","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Senior Software Engineer, Included Health, San Francisco, CA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Parekh A","firstName":"Ami","lastName":"Parekh","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Chief Health Officer, Included Health, San Francisco, CA, USA."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"7","volume":"7","journalIssueId":4228482,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jul","monthOfPublication":7,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-07-01","journal":{"title":"NEJM catalyst innovations in care delivery","medlineAbbreviation":"NEJM Catal Innov Care Deliv","isoabbreviation":"NEJM Catal Innov Care Deliv","nlmid":"101760264","essn":"2642-0007","issn":"2642-0007"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"CAT250377","abstractText":"Patients navigating a fragmented health care system may feel increasingly tempted to turn to publicly available large language models for quick answers to clinical questions; however, these tools were not built with patient safety, risk stratification, or escalation pathways in mind. In this case study, the authors describe how Included Health designed, piloted, and clinically governed a risk-stratified artificial intelligence (AI) digital assistant that offered generalized health guidance while reliably routing higher-risk situations to human clinicians. Building on OpenAI's generative pretrained transformer 4 (GPT-4) model, the team created a multitier risk classification engine that separated emergency, high-risk, and standard-risk patient inquiries; developed conservative safety guardrails that blocked AI advice and triggered escalation for concerning symptoms; and ran a continuous human-in-the-loop audit program that reviewed 100% of clinical interactions during the pilot. Using a randomized rollout to half of the patient population, the authors found that the risk-stratified assistant maintained a high level of clinical safety (96% accurate guidance, 0% critical safety events, and no AI-generated diagnoses) while reducing standard-risk queries routed to human support by 65%, shortening average human response times from 9.6 to 3.6 minutes, and improving resolution of health inquiries without additional visits. This blueprint illustrates how health care organizations can pair proactive risk analysis, adversarial testing, and ongoing governance to deploy patient-facing generative AI that is explicitly designed to put safety ahead of convenience and still meet patients' expectations for timely, trustworthy guidance.","affiliation":"Patient Safety Officer, Included Health, San Francisco, CA, USA.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Artificial Intelligence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Patient Safety"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Digital Health"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Generative Artificial Intelligence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Intelligent Systems"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1056/CAT.25.0377"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-07-08","dateOfCreation":"2026-07-08","firstIndexDate":"2026-07-09","dateOfRevision":"2026-07-08","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-17","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-17"},{"id":"42041331","source":"MED","pmid":"42041331","pmcid":"PMC13113701","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13113701"]},"doi":"10.3390/antibiotics15040368","title":"Large Language Model Recommendations for Empiric Antibiotics Versus Clinician Prescribing: A Non-Interventional Paired Retrospective Antimicrobial Stewardship Analysis.","authorString":"Antonie NI, Ionescu VA, Gheorghe G, Tiucă LC, Diaconu CC.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Antonie NI","firstName":"Ninel Iacobus","lastName":"Antonie","initials":"NI","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Medicine, University of Medicine and Pharmacy Carol Davila Bucharest, 050474 Bucharest, Romania."},{"affiliation":"Internal Medicine Department, Clinical Emergency Hospital of Bucharest, 105402 Bucharest, Romania."}]}},{"fullName":"Ionescu VA","firstName":"Vlad Alexandru","lastName":"Ionescu","initials":"VA","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Medicine, University of Medicine and Pharmacy Carol Davila Bucharest, 050474 Bucharest, Romania."},{"affiliation":"Internal Medicine Department, Clinical Emergency Hospital of Bucharest, 105402 Bucharest, Romania."}]}},{"fullName":"Gheorghe G","firstName":"Gina","lastName":"Gheorghe","initials":"G","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Medicine, University of Medicine and Pharmacy Carol Davila Bucharest, 050474 Bucharest, Romania."},{"affiliation":"Internal Medicine Department, Clinical Emergency Hospital of Bucharest, 105402 Bucharest, Romania."}]}},{"fullName":"Tiucă LC","firstName":"Loredana-Crista","lastName":"Tiucă","initials":"LC","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Medicine, University of Medicine and Pharmacy Carol Davila Bucharest, 050474 Bucharest, Romania."},{"affiliation":"Internal Medicine Department, Clinical Emergency Hospital of Bucharest, 105402 Bucharest, Romania."}]}},{"fullName":"Diaconu CC","firstName":"Camelia Cristina","lastName":"Diaconu","initials":"CC","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6837-260X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Medicine, University of Medicine and Pharmacy Carol Davila Bucharest, 050474 Bucharest, Romania."},{"affiliation":"Internal Medicine Department, Clinical Emergency Hospital of Bucharest, 105402 Bucharest, Romania."},{"affiliation":"Section of Medical Sciences, Academy of Romanian Scientists, 050045 Bucharest, Romania."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6837-260X"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"4","volume":"15","journalIssueId":4186027,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Apr","monthOfPublication":4,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-04-01","journal":{"title":"Antibiotics (Basel, Switzerland)","medlineAbbreviation":"Antibiotics (Basel)","isoabbreviation":"Antibiotics (Basel)","nlmid":"101637404","essn":"2079-6382","issn":"2079-6382"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"368","abstractText":"<b>Background/Objectives</b>: Antimicrobial resistance (AMR) remains a major global health threat, strengthening the case for antimicrobial stewardship strategies that limit unnecessary broad-spectrum empiric therapy while preserving timely escalation when clinically warranted. Before any clinical deployment of large language model (LLM)-based antibiotic decision support can be considered, structured offline evaluation is needed to assess whether model outputs align with auditable stewardship constraints under real-world admission contexts. We therefore evaluated whether post hoc LLM-generated empiric antibiotic recommendations showed greater concordance with a pre-specified stewardship benchmarking framework than clinician-initiated regimens in a retrospective shadow-mode setting. <b>Methods</b>: Single-center retrospective paired evaluation at Clinical Emergency Hospital of Bucharest (Internal Medicine, 2020-2024). The unit of analysis was the admission (N = 493), with paired 24 h empiric regimens (clinician-prescribed vs. post hoc LLM-recommended via OpenAI API; not visible to clinicians; no influence on care). Local laboratory-derived epidemiology was precomputed from microbiology exports and provided as structured prompt context to approximate information parity with clinicians' implicit local ecology knowledge. Primary (prespecified) endpoint: any contextual guardrail violation (unjustified carbapenem/antipseudomonal/anti-MRSA under prespecified structured severity/MDR-risk rules), exact McNemar. Key secondary (prespecified): Δ contextual guardrail penalty (LLM - Clin), sign test and Wilcoxon signed-rank (ties reported). Ethics committee approval was obtained. <b>Results</b>: Guardrail violations occurred in 17.0% of clinician regimens vs. 4.9% of LLM regimens (paired RD -12.2%; matched OR 0.216, 95% CI 0.127-0.367; McNemar exact <i>p</i> = 1.60 × 10<sup>-10</sup>). Δ penalty had median 0 with 398/493 ties; among non-ties, improvements (Δ < 0) exceeded adverse shifts (79 vs. 16; sign-test <i>p</i> = 3.47 × 10<sup>-11</sup>). <b>Conclusions</b>: In this offline, non-interventional paired evaluation, LLM-generated empiric regimens showed greater concordance with a pre-specified stewardship benchmarking framework than clinician empiric regimens for the same admissions. These findings should not be interpreted as evidence of clinical superiority, patient safety, or causal effectiveness, but rather as process-level benchmarking within a rule-based stewardship construct. As such, reproducible guardrail-based benchmarking may serve as an early pre-implementation step to identify alignment and potential failure modes before prospective, safety-governed evaluation.","affiliation":"Faculty of Medicine, University of Medicine and Pharmacy Carol Davila Bucharest, 050474 Bucharest, Romania.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Antimicrobial resistance","Antimicrobial Stewardship","Large Language Model","Openai Api","Paired Retrospective Study"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/antibiotics15040368"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13113701"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13113701?pdf=render"}]},"commentCorrectionList":{"commentCorrection":[{"id":"PPR1163822","source":"PPR","type":"Preprint in","note":"CrossRef Pre-print loader","orderIn":10002}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":2,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-04-27","dateOfCreation":"2026-04-27","firstIndexDate":"2026-04-27","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-18","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-04-02","firstPublicationDate":"2026-04-02"},{"id":"41534067","source":"MED","pmid":"41534067","pmcid":"PMC12853083","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12853083"]},"doi":"10.2196/86550","title":"From Agents to Governance: Essential AI Skills for Clinicians in the Large Language Model Era.","authorString":"Cao W, Zhang Q, Liu J, Liu S.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Cao W","firstName":"Weiping","lastName":"Cao","initials":"W","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0000-9117-6367"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Cardiology, West China Hospital, Sichuan University, Chengdu, Sichuan, China."},{"affiliation":"Department of Cardiology, The People's Hospital of Leshan, Leshan, Sichuan, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Zhang Q","firstName":"Qing","lastName":"Zhang","initials":"Q","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5652-4036"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Cardiology, West China Hospital, Sichuan University, Chengdu, Sichuan, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Liu J","firstName":"Jialin","lastName":"Liu","initials":"J","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1369-4625"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Information Center, West China Hospital, Sichuan University, Chengdu, China."},{"affiliation":"Department of Medical Informatics, West China Medical School, Sichuan University, Chengdu, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Liu S","firstName":"Siru","lastName":"Liu","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5003-5354"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biomedical Informatics, Vanderbilt University Medical Center, Nashville, TN, United States."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1369-4625"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5003-5354"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5652-4036"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0000-9117-6367"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"28","journalIssueId":4082344,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jan","monthOfPublication":1,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Journal of medical Internet research","medlineAbbreviation":"J Med Internet Res","isoabbreviation":"J Med Internet Res","nlmid":"100959882","essn":"1438-8871","issn":"1439-4456"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e86550","abstractText":"Large language models are rapidly transitioning from pilot schemes to routine clinical practice. This creates an urgent need for clinicians to develop the necessary skills to strike the right balance between seizing opportunities and taking accountability. We propose a 3-tier competency framework to support clinicians' evolution from cautious users to responsible stewards of artificial intelligence (AI). Tier 1 (foundational skills) defines the minimum competencies for safe use, including prompt engineering, human-AI agent interaction, security and privacy awareness, and the clinician-patient interface (transparency and consent). Tier 2 (intermediate skills) emphasizes evaluative expertise, including bias detection and mitigation, interpretation of explainability outputs, and the effective clinical integration of AI-generated workflows. Tier 3 (advanced skills) establishes leadership capabilities, mandating competencies in ethical governance (delineating accountability and liability boundaries), regulatory strategy, and model life cycle management-specifically, the ability to govern algorithmic adaptation and change protocols. Integrating this framework into continuing medical education programs and role-specific job descriptions could enhance clinicians' ability to use AI safely and responsibly. This could standardize deployment and support safer clinical practice, with the potential to improve patient outcomes.","affiliation":"Department of Cardiology, West China Hospital, Sichuan University, Chengdu, Sichuan, China.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Artificial Intelligence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Large Language Models"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Agent","Education","Continuing Medical Education","Competency","Clinician","Large Language Model"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2196/86550"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12853083"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12853083?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":2,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-26","dateOfCreation":"2026-01-14","firstIndexDate":"2026-01-15","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-01-30","dateOfRevision":"2026-06-26","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-01-14","firstPublicationDate":"2026-01-14"},{"id":"42211738","source":"MED","pmid":"42211738","pmcid":"PMC13214549","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13214549"]},"doi":"10.1016/j.xjmad.2026.100181","title":"Current themes of AI in mental health: Actionable evidence and guardrails for mood and anxiety care.","authorString":"Paulus MP.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Paulus MP","firstName":"Martin P","lastName":"Paulus","initials":"MP","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0825-3606"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Laureate Institute for Brain Research, Tulsa, OK, USA."},{"affiliation":"Oxley College of Health and Natural Sciences, University of Tulsa, Tulsa, OK, USA."},{"affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry, University of California, San Diego, La Jolla, CA, USA."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0825-3606"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"14","journalIssueId":4182059,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-06-01","journal":{"title":"Journal of mood and anxiety disorders","medlineAbbreviation":"J Mood Anxiety Disord","isoabbreviation":"J Mood Anxiety Disord","nlmid":"9918646056406676","essn":"2950-0044","issn":"2950-0044"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"100181","abstractText":"Currently, artificial intelligence (AI) is clinically relevant to mood and anxiety care, but the evidence base is uneven across use cases. This narrative review synthesizes recent literature most relevant to clinicians and investigators. Five themes dominate the current field: patient-facing adjunctive tools, failure modes and safety risks, clinician-facing decision support, passive sensing and measurement infrastructure, and governance. Recent randomized evidence supports a narrow efficacy claim for structured chatbot interventions, with small improvements in depressive and anxiety symptoms and more consistent effects on engagement than on symptom superiority. These studies do not support autonomous psychotherapy, and they do not establish a therapeutic advantage for open-ended large language model systems over more constrained designs. Safety studies, by contrast, identify active concerns: harmful endorsement, weak youth risk assessment, inconsistent crisis handling, and anxiety/OCD reassurance loops. The strongest current clinical signal lies in supervised clinician-facing decision support, where recent trials of AI-assisted antidepressant selection improved treatment persistence and some downstream symptom outcomes. Passive sensing detects behaviorally meaningful signals, but evidence that alert-driven deployment improves care remains insufficient for routine practice. Across stakeholder and policy sources, the most defensible deployment model is human-in-the-loop, stepped, and bounded by explicit handoff rules.","affiliation":"Laureate Institute for Brain Research, Tulsa, OK, USA.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"NIGMS","orderIn":0}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Depression","Phenotyping","Implementation","Anxiety","Governance","Chatbot"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.xjmad.2026.100181"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13214549"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13214549?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by-nc-nd","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-05-29","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-29","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-31","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-02","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-05-14","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-14"},{"id":"42101962","source":"MED","pmid":"42101962","pmcid":"PMC13155343","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13155343"]},"doi":"10.2196/91940","title":"Governing Patient-Facing AI-Generated Video in Digital Health: A Risk-and-Ethics Matrix for Deployment, Monitoring, and Change Control.","authorString":"Hu Y, Jiang W.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Hu Y","firstName":"Yongzheng","lastName":"Hu","initials":"Y","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-3064-6294"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Nephrology, Affiliated Hospital of Qingdao University, 16 Jiangsu Road, Shinan District, Qingdao, Shandong, 266000, China, 86 13044087725."},{"affiliation":"Department of Nephrology, Qingdao University, Qingdao, Shandong, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Jiang W","firstName":"Wei","lastName":"Jiang","initials":"W","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0003-1965-3650"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Nephrology, Affiliated Hospital of Qingdao University, 16 Jiangsu Road, Shinan District, Qingdao, Shandong, 266000, China, 86 13044087725."},{"affiliation":"Department of Nephrology, Qingdao University, Qingdao, Shandong, China."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-3064-6294"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0003-1965-3650"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"28","journalIssueId":4187347,"dateOfPublication":"2026 May","monthOfPublication":5,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-05-01","journal":{"title":"Journal of medical Internet research","medlineAbbreviation":"J Med Internet Res","isoabbreviation":"J Med Internet Res","nlmid":"100959882","essn":"1438-8871","issn":"1439-4456"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e91940","abstractText":"<h4>Unlabelled</h4>In this Viewpoint, we argue that patient-facing high-fidelity artificial intelligence (AI)-generated video requires governance that is operational, life cycle based, and embedded in existing institutional review pathways rather than limited to predeployment checks alone. Patient-facing high-fidelity AI-generated video-synthetic or substantially AI-mediated video that presents realistic human likeness, voices, or clinical communication cues-is rapidly entering patient education and clinical communication. We propose a risk-and-ethics matrix that combines residual clinical risk (likelihood × severity after mitigations) with an ethical alignment score that operationalizes autonomy, beneficence, nonmaleficence, and justice to yield actionable dispositions (encourage, permit with oversight, restrict or redesign, or prohibit). The framework links each disposition to dossier-based review, minimum controls, and postdeployment monitoring triggers-focused on measurable outcomes (eg, comprehension, content-attributable follow-up burden, incidents and complaints, and equity gaps) as well as provenance and change control-to support auditable, revisitable decisions over the system life cycle.","affiliation":"Department of Nephrology, Affiliated Hospital of Qingdao University, 16 Jiangsu Road, Shinan District, Qingdao, Shandong, 266000, China, 86 13044087725.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Artificial Intelligence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Video Recording"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Digital Health"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","AI","risk management","Digital Health","Deepfakes","Postdeployment Monitoring"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2196/91940"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13155343"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13155343?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-07-16","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-08","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-13","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-20","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-05-08","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-08"},{"id":"41749896","source":"MED","pmid":"41749896","pmcid":"PMC12939146","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12939146"]},"doi":"10.3390/cancers18040643","title":"From Network Governance to Real-World-Time Learning: A High-Reliability Operating Model for Rare Cancers.","authorString":"Fuchs B, Falkowski AL, Jaeger R, Kopf B, Rothermundt C, van Oudenaarde K, Zacchariah R, Heesen P, Schelling G, Studer G, Swiss Sarcoma Network.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Fuchs B","firstName":"Bruno","lastName":"Fuchs","initials":"B","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6453-3947"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Health Sciences & Medicine, University of Lucerne, Frohburgstrasse 3, 6002 Luzern, Switzerland."},{"affiliation":"Swiss Sarcoma Network SSN, LUKS Sarcoma-IPU, University Teaching Hospital LUKS, Spitalstrasse, 6000 Luzern, Switzerland."},{"affiliation":"Department of Orthopaedics and Trauma, LUKS Sarcoma-IPU, University Teaching Hospital LUKS, Spitalstrasse, 6000 Lucerne, Switzerland."},{"affiliation":"Department of Orthopaedics and Trauma, Kantonsspital Winterthur, KSW Sarcoma Center, Brauerstrasse 15, 8401 Winterthur, Switzerland."}]}},{"fullName":"Falkowski AL","firstName":"Anna L","lastName":"Falkowski","initials":"AL","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4293-0911"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Swiss Sarcoma Network SSN, LUKS Sarcoma-IPU, University Teaching Hospital LUKS, Spitalstrasse, 6000 Luzern, Switzerland."}]}},{"fullName":"Jaeger R","firstName":"Ruben","lastName":"Jaeger","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Swiss Sarcoma Network SSN, LUKS Sarcoma-IPU, University Teaching Hospital LUKS, Spitalstrasse, 6000 Luzern, Switzerland."},{"affiliation":"Department of Orthopaedics and Trauma, LUKS Sarcoma-IPU, University Teaching Hospital LUKS, Spitalstrasse, 6000 Lucerne, Switzerland."}]}},{"fullName":"Kopf B","firstName":"Barbara","lastName":"Kopf","initials":"B","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Istituto Oncologico della Svizzera Italiana, Ente Ospedaliero Cantonale, Ospedale Regionale di Locarno, La Carità, Viale Officina 3, 6500 Bellinzona, Switzerland."}]}},{"fullName":"Rothermundt C","firstName":"Christian","lastName":"Rothermundt","initials":"C","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Swiss Sarcoma Network SSN, LUKS Sarcoma-IPU, University Teaching Hospital LUKS, Spitalstrasse, 6000 Luzern, Switzerland."},{"affiliation":"Department of Medical Oncology, LUKS Sarcoma-IPU, University Teaching Hospital LUKS, Spitalstrasse, 6000 Lucerne, Switzerland."}]}},{"fullName":"van Oudenaarde K","firstName":"Kim","lastName":"van Oudenaarde","initials":"K","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0003-6191-3778"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Swiss Sarcoma Network SSN, LUKS Sarcoma-IPU, University Teaching Hospital LUKS, Spitalstrasse, 6000 Luzern, Switzerland."},{"affiliation":"Department of Radiology, LUKS Sarcoma-IPU, University Teaching Hospital LUKS, Spitalstrasse, 6000 Lucerne, Switzerland."}]}},{"fullName":"Zacchariah R","firstName":"Ralph","lastName":"Zacchariah","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medical Oncology, Kantonsspital Winterthur, KSW Sarcoma Center, Brauerstrasse 15, 8401 Winterthur, Switzerland."}]}},{"fullName":"Heesen P","firstName":"Philip","lastName":"Heesen","initials":"P","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5090-4935"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Medicine, University of Zurich, Raemistrasse 71, 8006 Zurich, Switzerland."}]}},{"fullName":"Schelling G","firstName":"Georg","lastName":"Schelling","initials":"G","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Swiss Sarcoma Network SSN, LUKS Sarcoma-IPU, University Teaching Hospital LUKS, Spitalstrasse, 6000 Luzern, Switzerland."},{"affiliation":"Department of Orthopaedics and Trauma, LUKS Sarcoma-IPU, University Teaching Hospital LUKS, Spitalstrasse, 6000 Lucerne, Switzerland."}]}},{"fullName":"Studer G","firstName":"Gabriela","lastName":"Studer","initials":"G","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8780-7701"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Health Sciences & Medicine, University of Lucerne, Frohburgstrasse 3, 6002 Luzern, Switzerland."},{"affiliation":"Swiss Sarcoma Network SSN, LUKS Sarcoma-IPU, University Teaching Hospital LUKS, Spitalstrasse, 6000 Luzern, Switzerland."},{"affiliation":"Department of Radiation Oncology, LUKS Sarcoma-IPU, University Teaching Hospital LUKS, Spitalstrasse, 6000 Lucerne, Switzerland."}]}},{"collectiveName":"Swiss Sarcoma Network"}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6453-3947"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8780-7701"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5090-4935"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4293-0911"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0003-6191-3778"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"4","volume":"18","journalIssueId":4132784,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Feb","monthOfPublication":2,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-02-01","journal":{"title":"Cancers","medlineAbbreviation":"Cancers (Basel)","isoabbreviation":"Cancers (Basel)","nlmid":"101526829","essn":"2072-6694","issn":"2072-6694"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"643","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Rare cancers combine low incidence with high biological heterogeneity and multi-institutional care trajectories. These features make single-center learning structurally incomplete and render pathway fragmentation a dominant driver of preventable harm, variability, and waste. In this context, care quality is best understood as a property of pathway integrity across routing, diagnostics (imaging/biopsy planning), multidisciplinary intent-setting, definitive treatment, and surveillance-rather than as a department-level attribute.<h4>Objective</h4>To define a pragmatic, transferable operating blueprint for a rare-cancer Learning Health System (LHS) that turns routine care into continuous, auditable learning under explicit governance, while maintaining claims discipline and protecting measurement validity.<h4>Approach</h4>We synthesize an implementation-oriented operating model using the Swiss Sarcoma Network (SSN) as an exemplar. The blueprint couples clinical governance (Integrated Practice Unit logic, hub-and-spoke routing, auditable multidisciplinary team decision systems) with an interoperable real-world-time data backbone designed for benchmarking, pathway mapping, and feedback. The operating logic is expressed as a closed-loop control cycle: capture → harmonize → benchmark → learn → implement → re-measure, with explicit owners, minimum requirements, and failure modes. Results/Blueprint: (i) The model specifies a minimal set of data primitives-time-stamped and traceable decision points covering baseline and tumor characteristics, pathway timing, treatment exposure, outcomes and complications, and feasible longitudinal PROMs and PREMs; (ii) a VBHC-ready, multi-domain measurement backbone spanning outcomes, harms, timeliness, function, process fidelity, and resource stewardship; and (iii) two non-negotiable validity guardrails: explicit applicability (\"N/A\") rules and mandatory case-mix/complexity stratification. Implementation is treated as a governed step with defined workflow levers, fidelity criteria, balancing measures, and escalation thresholds to prevent \"dashboard medicine\" and surrogate-driven optimization.<h4>Conclusions</h4>This perspective contributes an operating model-not a platform or single intervention-that enables credible improvement science and establishes prerequisites for downstream causal learning and minimum viable digital twins. By distinguishing enabling infrastructure from the governed clinical system as the primary intervention, the blueprint supports scalable, learnable excellence in rare-cancer care while protecting against gaming, inequity, and inference drift. Distinct from generic LHS or VBHC frameworks, this blueprint specifies validity gates required for rare-cancer benchmarking-explicit applicability (\"N/A\") rules, denominator integrity/capture completeness disclosure, anti-gaming safeguards, and escalation governance. These elements are critical in rare cancers because small denominators, high heterogeneity, and multi-institutional pathways otherwise make benchmarking prone to artifacts and unsafe inferences.","affiliation":"Faculty of Health Sciences & Medicine, University of Lucerne, Frohburgstrasse 3, 6002 Luzern, Switzerland.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["other","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Sarcoma","Rare Cancers","Learning Health System","Value-based Healthcare","Hub-and-spoke","Real-world-time Data","Multidisciplinary Tumor Board/Mdt"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/cancers18040643"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12939146"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12939146?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-02-27","dateOfCreation":"2026-02-27","firstIndexDate":"2026-02-27","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-02-27","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-02-16","firstPublicationDate":"2026-02-16"},{"id":"42078947","source":"MED","pmid":"42078947","pmcid":"PMC13134441","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13134441"]},"doi":"10.1093/nsr/nwag204","title":"Towards China-initiated actions on AI safety and governance.","authorString":"Zeng Y, Huang T, Jiang Y, Poo MM.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Zeng Y","firstName":"Yi","lastName":"Zeng","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Beijing Institute of AI Safety and Governances, China."},{"affiliation":"Beijing Key Laboratory of Safe AI and Superalignment, Institute of Automation, Chinese Academy of Sciences, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Huang T","firstName":"Tiejun","lastName":"Huang","initials":"T","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Beijing Academy of Artificial Intelligence, China."},{"affiliation":"State Key Laboratory of Multimedia Information Processing, Peking University, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Jiang Y","firstName":"Yugang","lastName":"Jiang","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Fudan University, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Poo MM","firstName":"Mu-Ming","lastName":"Poo","initials":"MM","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"CAS Center for Excellence for Brain Science and Intelligence Technology, China."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"8","volume":"13","journalIssueId":4186533,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Apr","monthOfPublication":4,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-04-01","journal":{"title":"National science review","medlineAbbreviation":"Natl Sci Rev","isoabbreviation":"Natl Sci Rev","nlmid":"101633095","essn":"2053-714X","issn":"2095-5138"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"nwag204","affiliation":"Beijing Institute of AI Safety and Governances, China.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Editorial"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/nsr/nwag204"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13134441"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13134441?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"N","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-05-04","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-04","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-13","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-20","dateOfRevision":"2026-05-04","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-04-08","firstPublicationDate":"2026-04-08"},{"id":"41606555","source":"MED","pmid":"41606555","pmcid":"PMC12924558","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12924558"]},"doi":"10.1186/s12889-025-26148-9","title":"Generative AI and health misinformation: production, propagation, and mitigation-a systematic review.","authorString":"Saeidnia HR, Jahani S, Ghiasi N, Keshavarz H.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Saeidnia HR","firstName":"Hamid Reza","lastName":"Saeidnia","initials":"HR","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Knowledge and Information Science, Tarbiat Modares University, (TMU), Tehran, Iran."}]}},{"fullName":"Jahani S","firstName":"Shamim","lastName":"Jahani","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Research Committee, Babol University of Medical Sciences, Babol, Iran."}]}},{"fullName":"Ghiasi N","firstName":"Nasrin","lastName":"Ghiasi","initials":"N","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Public Health, School of Health, Ilam University of Medical Sciences, Ilam, Iran. ghnazi93@gmail.com."}]}},{"fullName":"Keshavarz H","firstName":"Hamid","lastName":"Keshavarz","initials":"H","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Knowledge and Information Science, Shahid Beheshti University, Tehran, Iran."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"26","journalIssueId":4082921,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jan","monthOfPublication":1,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"BMC public health","medlineAbbreviation":"BMC Public Health","isoabbreviation":"BMC Public Health","nlmid":"100968562","essn":"1471-2458","issn":"1471-2458"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"693","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Generative artificial intelligence technologies have disrupted information ecosystems, posing new threats to public health by enabling rapid, scalable manufacture of convincing but false health stories. This systematic review synthesizes evidence on how generative AI reconfigures health misinformation creation, dissemination, and moderation.<h4>Methods</h4>In line with PRISMA 2020, 15 empirical studies published between January 2023 and August 2025 were included. Databases consulted were MEDLINE (via PubMed), Embase, Scopus, Web of Science Core Collection, ACM Digital Library, IEEE Xplore, PsycINFO, Communication & Mass Media Complete, arXiv, and medRxiv/SSRN. Studies were contrasted on the basis of production capacity, propagation dynamics, and efficacy of mitigation at technical, sociotechnical, and governance layers.<h4>Results</h4>The synthesis indicates that generative AI substantially increases the volume, speed, and perceived credibility of health disinformation production, while altering its propagation dynamics. Users often struggle to distinguish AI‑generated from human‑authored health misinformation, and their sharing intentions are not tightly coupled with perceived accuracy. Existing detection systems show limited performance against AI‑generated content, and while labeling interventions can reduce perceived accuracy, their effects are context‑dependent.<h4>Conclusion</h4>Generative AI transforms the health misinformation landscape by lowering barriers to creation and exploiting platform and behavioral dynamics. Current mitigation strategies-spanning technical, sociotechnical, and governance layers-are promising but remain nascent and unevenly evaluated. Future work must prioritize multimodal, multilingual, and health‑specific verification, as well as real‑world testing of interventions, to build equitable and resilient health information ecosystems.","affiliation":"Department of Knowledge and Information Science, Tarbiat Modares University, (TMU), Tehran, Iran.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Systematic Review","review-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Communication"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Information Dissemination","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Health Communication"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Generative Artificial Intelligence"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Systematic review","Mitigation Strategies","Health Misinformation","Deepfakes","Generative Ai"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s12889-025-26148-9"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12924558"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12924558?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":2,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by-nc-nd","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-07-01","dateOfCreation":"2026-01-28","firstIndexDate":"2026-02-06","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-02-22","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-01-29","firstPublicationDate":"2026-01-29"},{"id":"41993038","source":"MED","pmid":"41993038","pmcid":"PMC13079118","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13079118"]},"doi":"10.3389/fdgth.2026.1778786","title":"A review for navigating the trade-offs: evaluating open-source and proprietary large language models for clinical and biomedical information extraction.","authorString":"Sugihara Y, Milosavljevic A, Jankovskaja S, Falk M.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Sugihara Y","firstName":"Yutaka","lastName":"Sugihara","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biomedical Science, Faculty of Health and Society, Malmö University, Malmö, Sweden."},{"affiliation":"Biofilms-Research Center for Biointerfaces, Malmö University, Malmö, Sweden."}]}},{"fullName":"Milosavljevic A","firstName":"Aleksandar","lastName":"Milosavljevic","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Periodontology, Faculty of Odontology, Malmö University, Malmö, Sweden."}]}},{"fullName":"Jankovskaja S","firstName":"Skaidre","lastName":"Jankovskaja","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biomedical Science, Faculty of Health and Society, Malmö University, Malmö, Sweden."},{"affiliation":"Biofilms-Research Center for Biointerfaces, Malmö University, Malmö, Sweden."}]}},{"fullName":"Falk M","firstName":"Magnus","lastName":"Falk","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biomedical Science, Faculty of Health and Society, Malmö University, Malmö, Sweden."},{"affiliation":"Biofilms-Research Center for Biointerfaces, Malmö University, Malmö, Sweden."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"8","journalIssueId":4117475,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Frontiers in digital health","medlineAbbreviation":"Front Digit Health","isoabbreviation":"Front Digit Health","nlmid":"101771889","essn":"2673-253X","issn":"2673-253X"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1778786","abstractText":"The exponential growth of biomedical data necessitates advanced tools for efficient information extraction (IE) to support clinical decision-making and research. Large language models (LLMs) have emerged as transformative solutions, yet their application in healthcare raises critical trade-offs between open-source (OSS) and proprietary models. This review evaluates IE workflows such as named entity recognition, relation extraction, and terminology normalization, through five axes: performance (including schema fidelity), reproducibility, cost, transparency & auditability, and patient-centric governance. While proprietary models excel in schema compliance and complex reasoning, OSS models offer advantages in auditability, local control, and cost-effectiveness. Challenges such as schema fidelity, reproducibility, and ethical considerations like algorithmic fairness and data sovereignty are emphasized. The analysis highlights that OSS models, though requiring domain-specific adaptation, enable greater transparency and customization for privacy-sensitive tasks, whereas proprietary systems face limitations in bias mitigation and regulatory alignment. By addressing technical, ethical, and operational challenges, this work underscores the importance of context-aware model selection to balance innovation with accountability in clinical AI deployment. The findings advocate for hybrid approaches that integrate OSS flexibility with proprietary capabilities, ensuring equitable, reliable, and compliant healthcare solutions.","affiliation":"Department of Biomedical Science, Faculty of Health and Society, Malmö University, Malmö, Sweden.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Information Extraction","Healthcare Ai","Large Language Models","Proprietary Models","Open-source Models"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fdgth.2026.1778786"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13079118"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13079118?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-04-17","dateOfCreation":"2026-04-17","firstIndexDate":"2026-04-18","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-04-20","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-04-01","firstPublicationDate":"2026-04-01"},{"id":"PPR1292229","source":"PPR","doi":"10.20944/preprints202607.2395.v1","title":"From Data Quality to Quality of Agentic Data Use: A Conceptual Framework for Agentic Data Engineering","authorString":"Cravero A, Díaz J.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Cravero A","firstName":"Ania","lastName":"Cravero","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0883-7254"}},{"fullName":"Díaz J","firstName":"Jorge","lastName":"Díaz","initials":"J"}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0883-7254"}]},"pubYear":"2026","abstractText":"The integration of large language models and autonomous agents into data engineering is shifting data systems from human-specified pipelines to processes in which agents interpret requirements, select sources, generate transformations, invoke tools, and communicate analytical results. This shift introduces a governance gap: a dataset may satisfy established quality requirements and a generated query may execute successfully while the agent applies an incorrect metric, combines incompatible grains, uses unauthorized sources, or produces conclusions without evidential support. We develop a conceptual framework for Agentic Data Engineering, the engineering domain concerned with specifying, validating, governing, and auditing data processes in which AI agents operate under delegated authority. An evidence-informed analysis of six foundations—Data Contracts, Semantic Layers, Data Quality, Guardrails, AI Governance, and Data Provenance—shows that existing controls are essential but fragmented. The framework integrates them through four artifacts: Agentic Data Contracts, Agentic Expectations, Agentic Data Provenance, and Agentic Data Governance, and defines Quality of Agentic Data Use as the central evaluative construct. A lifecycle model, a reference architecture, a failure taxonomy, a multidimensional evaluation scheme, and an illustrative governed sales-analysis scenario operationalize the framework and provide a foundation for the empirical validation of trustworthy agentic data systems.","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Preprint"]},"bookOrReportDetails":{"publisher":"Preprints.org","yearOfPublication":2026},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.20944/preprints202607.2395.v1"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","versionList":{"version":[{"id":"PPR1292229","source":"PPR","firstPublishDate":"2026-07-31","versionNumber":1,"pubTypeList":{"pubType":["preprint"]},"hasEvaluations":"N"}]},"versionNumber":1,"hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-08-07","firstIndexDate":"2026-08-07","firstPublicationDate":"2026-07-31"},{"id":"42053543","source":"MED","pmid":"42053543","doi":"10.1080/15265161.2026.2645617","title":"Personal Utility Without Clinical Guardrails: Toward a Governance Framework for Direct-to-Consumer Biological Futures.","authorString":"Greenbaum D.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Greenbaum D","firstName":"Dov","lastName":"Greenbaum","initials":"D","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4440-3090"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Reichman University."},{"affiliation":"Yale University."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4440-3090"}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"5","volume":"26","journalIssueId":4186359,"dateOfPublication":"2026 May","monthOfPublication":5,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-05-01","journal":{"title":"The American journal of bioethics : AJOB","medlineAbbreviation":"Am J Bioeth","isoabbreviation":"Am J Bioeth","nlmid":"100898738","essn":"1536-0075","issn":"1526-5161"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"46-50","affiliation":"Reichman University.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Comment","Journal Article"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1080/15265161.2026.2645617"}]},"commentCorrectionList":{"commentCorrection":[{"id":"40100620","source":"MED","reference":"Am J Bioeth. 2026 May;26(5):6-17. doi: 10.1080/15265161.2025.2475015.","type":"Comment on","orderIn":1}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"N","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-04-29","firstIndexDate":"2026-04-30","dateOfRevision":"2026-07-31","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-04-29","firstPublicationDate":"2026-04-29"},{"id":"42464875","source":"MED","pmid":"42464875","doi":"10.1093/cid/ciag445","title":"Artificial Intelligence Across the Vaccine Clinical Trial Lifecycle: Evidence, Readiness, and Guardrails.","authorString":"Idriss J, Kalash S, Faraj JA, Nolan L, Bechnak K, Rouphael N.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Idriss J","firstName":"Jad","lastName":"Idriss","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Hope Clinic, Infectious Diseases Division, Department of Medicine, School of Medicine, Emory University, Decatur, GA 30030, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Kalash S","firstName":"Suha","lastName":"Kalash","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0005-3755-7771"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Hope Clinic, Infectious Diseases Division, Department of Medicine, School of Medicine, Emory University, Decatur, GA 30030, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Faraj JA","firstName":"Jana Abu","lastName":"Faraj","initials":"JA","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Hope Clinic, Infectious Diseases Division, Department of Medicine, School of Medicine, Emory University, Decatur, GA 30030, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Nolan L","firstName":"Lauren","lastName":"Nolan","initials":"L","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0004-1489-513X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Emory Children's Center Vaccine Research Clinic, Infectious Diseases Division, Department of Pediatrics, School of Medicine, Emory University, Atlanta, GA 30324, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Bechnak K","firstName":"Kareem","lastName":"Bechnak","initials":"K","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Hope Clinic, Infectious Diseases Division, Department of Medicine, School of Medicine, Emory University, Decatur, GA 30030, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Rouphael N","firstName":"Nadine","lastName":"Rouphael","initials":"N","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2512-7919"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Hope Clinic, Infectious Diseases Division, Department of Medicine, School of Medicine, Emory University, Decatur, GA 30030, USA."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2512-7919"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0004-1489-513X"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0005-3755-7771"}]},"journalInfo":{"journalIssueId":4223619,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jul","monthOfPublication":7,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-07-01","journal":{"title":"Clinical infectious diseases : an official publication of the Infectious Diseases Society of America","medlineAbbreviation":"Clin Infect Dis","isoabbreviation":"Clin Infect Dis","nlmid":"9203213","essn":"1537-6591","issn":"1058-4838"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"ciag445","abstractText":"Artificial intelligence (AI) is increasingly being used to support clinical research, but its value in vaccine clinical trials requires careful evidence-based assessment. Vaccine trials pose distinctive challenges, including high safety expectations in healthy participants, evolving pathogen exposure and baseline immunity, incomplete correlates of protection, applicability of findings to intended-use populations, and intense public scrutiny. We conducted a structured, vaccine-focused narrative review of AI applications across the vaccine trial lifecycle, supplemented by targeted clinical trial and vaccine pharmacovigilance studies with directly transferable methods. In the combined evidence base, evidence is strongest for operational uses, particularly recruitment, eligibility screening, trial matching, and risk-based monitoring. Applications to immune-response interpretation, correlates of protection, and vaccine safety surveillance are promising but remain less prospectively validated. Responsible adoption should be guided by intended tool use, evidence of strength, data governance, regulatory expectations, and preservation of human scientific and safety judgment.","affiliation":"Hope Clinic, Infectious Diseases Division, Department of Medicine, School of Medicine, Emory University, Decatur, GA 30030, USA.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Immunogenicity","Recruitment","Pharmacovigilance","Machine Learning","Vaccine Clinical Trials","Clinical Trial Monitoring"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/cid/ciag445"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-07-17","firstIndexDate":"2026-07-18","dateOfRevision":"2026-07-17","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-07-17","firstPublicationDate":"2026-07-17"},{"id":"42039682","source":"MED","pmid":"42039682","pmcid":"PMC13104593","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13104593"]},"doi":"10.3389/frai.2025.1496945","title":"Population health management fit lifecycles in analytics.","authorString":"Henry JA.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Henry JA","firstName":"James Andrew","lastName":"Henry","initials":"JA","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Institute of Biomedical Sciences, London, United Kingdom."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"8","journalIssueId":3897209,"dateOfPublication":"2025 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2025,"printPublicationDate":"2025-01-01","journal":{"title":"Frontiers in artificial intelligence","medlineAbbreviation":"Front Artif Intell","isoabbreviation":"Front Artif Intell","nlmid":"101770551","essn":"2624-8212","issn":"2624-8212"}},"pubYear":"2025","pageInfo":"1496945","abstractText":"<h4>Introduction</h4>\"Population Health Management (PHM), Fit Lifecycles in Analytics\" examines the policy and practice of AI-driven methodologies to enhance public health and patient safety in the context of the Human Phenotype Ontology (HPO). It aims for personalized healthcare delivery through the risk stratification of predictors and pathology segmentation for intercepts. This manuscript aimed to introduce the Five-Point PHM strategy as a mission for public trust and governance. Scientific and technological advancements address public genomic inclusiveness and engage biobanks and life sciences for national public health and patient safety oversight.<h4>Methods</h4>The study assesses genome and socio-environmental health factor variables that segment and image disease through stakeholder engagement in real-world settings such as HPO neighborhood trials. It assesses practices for data training, emphasizing data alliances, scientific themes, and data structure. The manuscript evaluates data preprocessing and prioritizes open-source frameworks to ensure data balance and bias mitigation.<h4>Actions</h4>The recommendation for a PHM infrastructure ensures personal classification under the national authority with a comparative analysis of AI architectures that highlights trade-offs in AI modes for HPO. Structural and continuous control monitoring, explainability, and model performance metrics are emphasized. The actions transform the vision and language for HPO, advocating for a national generative classification for genome predictor pre-eXam and eXam intercepts. Actions on guardrails and ethics address a secure and safe national program.<h4>Discussion</h4>The governance of fit lifecycles in analytics discusses improvement with research science integration and accountability for HPO as primary care. The PHM mission and ten-year infrastructure plan addresses implementation challenges through government principles for an adoption mission with AISI/AIDRS authority. Diligent PHM through HPO policy action GPT-5, with federated data learning and quantum computing as emerging technologies that synergize for BM of predictors and intercepts.<h4>Conclusion</h4>The manuscript concludes with the potential of the proposed PHM mission to support the UK AI Action Plan and principles outlined in the UK Government AI Playbook. By integrating research science into HPO evidence-based primary care practice, this paper drives progress in public health and patient safety for national well-being and growth. The study advocates for the ethical and secure implementation of AI-driven PHM with public science and technology trustworthiness.","affiliation":"Institute of Biomedical Sciences, London, United Kingdom.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["other","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Biological Modeling","Population Health Management","Human Phenotype Ontology","Ai Digital Regulation Service","Classify Predictors And Intercepts"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/frai.2025.1496945"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13104593"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13104593?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":1,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-04-27","dateOfCreation":"2026-04-27","firstIndexDate":"2026-04-27","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-04-24","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-04-09","firstPublicationDate":"2026-04-09"},{"id":"PPR1123006","source":"PPR","doi":"10.20944/preprints202511.1554.v1","title":"From Static Prediction to Mindful Machines: A Paradigm Shift in Distributed AI Systems","authorString":"Mikkilineni R, Kelly WP.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Mikkilineni R","firstName":"Rao","lastName":"Mikkilineni","initials":"R"},{"fullName":"Kelly WP","firstName":"Patrick","lastName":"Kelly","initials":"WP"}]},"pubYear":"2025","abstractText":"A special class of complex adaptive systems—biological, and social—thrive not by passively accumulating patterns, but by engineering coherence: the deliberate alignment of prior knowledge, real-time updates, and teleonomic purposes. By contrast, today’s AI stacks—Large Language Models (LLMs) wrapped in agentic toolchains—remain rooted in a Turing-paradigm architecture: statistical world models (opaque weights) bolted onto brittle, imperative workflows. They excel at pattern completion, but they externalize governance, memory, and purpose, thereby accumulating coherence debt—a structural fragility manifested as hallucinations, shallow and siloed memory, ad hoc guardrails, and costly human oversight. The shortcoming of current AI relative to human-like intelligence is therefore less about raw performance or scaling, and more about an architectural limitation: knowledge is treated as an after-the-fact annotation on computation, rather than as an organizing substrate that shapes computation. This paper introduces Mindful Machines, a computational paradigm that operationalizes coherence as an architectural property rather than an emergent afterthought. A Mindful Machine is specified by a Digital Genome (encoding purposes, constraints, and knowledge structures) and orchestrated by an Autopoietic and Meta-Cognitive Operating System (AMOS) that runs a continuous Discover–Reflect–Apply–Share (D-R-A-S) loop. Instead of a static model embedded in a one-shot ML pipeline or deep learning neural network, the architecture separates: (1) a structural knowledge layer (Digital Genome and knowledge graphs), (2) an autopoietic control plane (health checks, rollback, and self-repair), and (3) meta-cognitive governance (critique-then-commit gates, audit trails, and policy enforcement). We validate this approach on the classic Credit Default Prediction problem by comparing a traditional, static Logistic Regression pipeline (monolithic training, fixed features, external scripting for deployment) with a distributed Mindful Machine implementation whose components can reconfigure logic, update rules, and migrate workloads at runtime. The Mindful Machine not only matches the predictive task, but also achieves autopoiesis (self-healing services and live schema evolution), explainability (causal, event-driven audit trails), and dynamic adaptation (real-time logic and threshold switching driven by knowledge constraints), thereby paying down the coherence debt that characterizes contemporary ML- and LLM-centric AI architectures.","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Preprint"]},"bookOrReportDetails":{"publisher":"Preprints.org","yearOfPublication":2025},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.20944/preprints202511.1554.v1"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","versionList":{"version":[{"id":"PPR1123006","source":"PPR","firstPublishDate":"2025-11-20","versionNumber":1,"pubTypeList":{"pubType":["preprint"]},"hasEvaluations":"N"}]},"versionNumber":1,"hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2025-11-21","firstIndexDate":"2025-11-21","firstPublicationDate":"2025-11-20"},{"id":"41551635","source":"MED","pmid":"41551635","pmcid":"PMC12811553","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12811553"]},"doi":"10.1177/20552076261416808","title":"Artificial intelligence in occupational therapy documentation: Chatbot versus Occupational Therapists: A Letter to the Editor.","authorString":"Wang SP, Liu YL.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Wang SP","firstName":"San-Ping","lastName":"Wang","initials":"SP","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5883-250X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Occupational Therapy, Taoyuan Psychiatric Center, Ministry of Health and Welfare, Taoyuan."},{"affiliation":"Department of Occupational Therapy, College of Medicine, Chang Gung University, Taoyuan."}]}},{"fullName":"Liu YL","firstName":"Yun-Ling","lastName":"Liu","initials":"YL","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychology, Taoyuan Psychiatric Center, Ministry of Health and Welfare, Taoyuan."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5883-250X"}]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"12","journalIssueId":4085000,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jan-Dec","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Digital health","medlineAbbreviation":"Digit Health","isoabbreviation":"Digit Health","nlmid":"101690863","essn":"2055-2076","issn":"2055-2076"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"20552076261416808","affiliation":"Department of Occupational Therapy, Taoyuan Psychiatric Center, Ministry of Health and Welfare, Taoyuan.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Letter"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1177/20552076261416808"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12811553"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12811553?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"N","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by-nc","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-01-19","dateOfCreation":"2026-01-19","firstIndexDate":"2026-01-19","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-01-22","dateOfRevision":"2026-01-21","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-01-16","firstPublicationDate":"2026-01-16"},{"id":"41971288","source":"MED","pmid":"41971288","pmcid":"PMC13062171","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13062171"]},"doi":"10.3389/fpubh.2026.1752484","title":"AI-driven high-risk pregnancy prediction: balancing early detection, anxiety, and discrimination in digital public health.","authorString":"Ji Q, Wang M.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Ji Q","firstName":"Qingqing","lastName":"Ji","initials":"Q","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"West China Second University Hospital, Sichuan University, Chengdu, Sichuan, China."},{"affiliation":"Key Laboratory of Birth Defects and Related Diseases of Women and Children (Sichuan University), Ministry of Education, Chengdu, Sichuan, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Wang M","firstName":"Mengyi","lastName":"Wang","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"West China Second University Hospital, Sichuan University, Chengdu, Sichuan, China."},{"affiliation":"Key Laboratory of Birth Defects and Related Diseases of Women and Children (Sichuan University), Ministry of Education, Chengdu, Sichuan, China."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"14","journalIssueId":4110762,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Frontiers in public health","medlineAbbreviation":"Front Public Health","isoabbreviation":"Front Public Health","nlmid":"101616579","essn":"2296-2565","issn":"2296-2565"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1752484","abstractText":"Over the past five years, perinatal risk prediction using artificial intelligence has expanded rapidly, drawing on routine clinical records, ultrasound findings, and continuous physiologic signals to generate dynamic high-risk scores across pregnancy. These tools promise earlier identification of complications, more precise monitoring, and better targeting of preventive resources, but their net benefit will hinge on how risk labels shape care and lived experience. In this Perspective, we conducted a targeted, non-systematic narrative synthesis integrating (i) evidence on AI-based obstetric risk prediction, (ii) lessons from prenatal screening and high-risk labeling, and (iii) principles and guidance on trustworthy digital health, equity/fairness, risk communication, and reproductive-data governance to examine how probabilistic outputs can unintentionally increase distress and inequity. We argue that risk labeling may fuel predictive anxiety when probabilities are interpreted deterministically, and secondary anxiety when intensified surveillance is experienced as confirmation of danger. We also outline discrimination pathways, including biased data and labels that over-flag socially disadvantaged groups, defensive clinical escalation that drives over-medicalization, and social or employment harms when sensitive pregnancy data are reused beyond care. To balance benefit and harm, we propose integrated safeguards: transparent model documentation, local and subgroup calibration, continuous fairness monitoring, structured and patient-centered risk communication with meaningful choice, strict privacy and purpose-limitation protections, and tiered psychological support embedded in clinical pathways. Future deployments should proceed as monitored pilots that jointly track clinical outcomes, equity, and perinatal mental health before scale-up.","affiliation":"West China Second University Hospital, Sichuan University, Chengdu, Sichuan, China.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["brief-report","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Risk Assessment","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Anxiety"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Public Health"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Pregnancy"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Pregnancy, High-Risk"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Artificial Intelligence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Female"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Digital Health"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Prediction Algorithms"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Data Privacy","High-risk Pregnancy","Health Equity","Algorithmic Bias","Patient-Centered Risk Communication","Perinatal Risk Prediction","Predictive Anxiety"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fpubh.2026.1752484"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13062171"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13062171?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":2,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-07-14","dateOfCreation":"2026-04-13","firstIndexDate":"2026-04-13","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-04-10","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-03-26","firstPublicationDate":"2026-03-26"},{"id":"42318585","source":"MED","pmid":"42318585","pmcid":"PMC13272403","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13272403"]},"doi":"10.3389/frai.2026.1818128","title":"Evidence-based AI: from trailblazer to trustblazer?","authorString":"Luechtefeld T, Hartung T.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Luechtefeld T","firstName":"Thomas","lastName":"Luechtefeld","initials":"T","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Insilica Inc., Rockville, MD, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Hartung T","firstName":"Thomas","lastName":"Hartung","initials":"T","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Center for Alternatives to Animal Testing (CAAT), Johns Hopkins Bloomberg School of Public Health, Baltimore, MD, United States."},{"affiliation":"CAAT-Europe, University of Konstanz, Konstanz, Germany."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"9","journalIssueId":4117457,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Frontiers in artificial intelligence","medlineAbbreviation":"Front Artif Intell","isoabbreviation":"Front Artif Intell","nlmid":"101770551","essn":"2624-8212","issn":"2624-8212"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1818128","abstractText":"Agentic AI systems can plan, call tools, and coordinate specialized sub-agents, enabling multi-step scientific workflows that exceed what single-model text generation can reliably deliver. Yet in high-stakes domains such as regulatory science and toxicology, fluent outputs are not sufficient: adoption hinges on traceability, reproducibility, context-of-use validity, and explicit uncertainty communication. This perspective argues that evidence-based medicine and evidence-based toxicology provide a mature epistemic scaffold for making agentic AI trustworthy by design. We propose an Evidence-based Agent Stack that decomposes end-to-end tasks into protocolized roles (question framing, retrieval, screening, extraction, risk-of-bias appraisal, synthesis, mechanistic/causal integration, uncertainty assessment, and evidence-to-decision translation) with mandatory provenance and versioning. Anchoring agentic workflows in systematic review practice, risk-of-bias frameworks, and emerging regulatory principles (e.g., TREAT and e-validation) can turn \"<i>trailblazing</i>\" AI into \"<i>trustblazing</i>\" AI: systems whose outputs are auditable, updateable, and aligned with decision accountability.","affiliation":"Insilica Inc., Rockville, MD, United States.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["brief-report","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Systematic review","Regulatory Science","Evidence-based Toxicology","Risk Of Bias","Retrieval-augmented Generation","Agentic Ai","E-validation","Evidence-based Edicine"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/frai.2026.1818128"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13272403"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13272403?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-19","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-19","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-20","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-24","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-03","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-03"},{"id":"PPR1245533","source":"PPR","doi":"10.21203/rs.3.rs-9857965/v1","title":"Governing Autonomous Enterprises: A Transaction Cost Perspective on Multi-Agent AI Systems in Supply Chain Operations","authorString":"Sharma G.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Sharma G","firstName":"Garv","lastName":"Sharma","initials":"G"}]},"pubYear":"2026","abstractText":"<title>Abstract</title>  <p>  <bold>Problem definition</bold>  : The rapid proliferation of autonomous multi-agent AI systems (MAS) in enterprise environments creates novel governance challenges that existing business frameworks have not addressed.  <bold>Relevance</bold>  : As organizations deploy agent-to-agent transaction networks across B2B supply chain operations, the absence of theoretically grounded governance models exposes firms to systemic algorithmic risk, cascade failures, and runaway operational costs.  <bold>Methodology</bold>  : Anchored in Transaction Cost Economics (TCE), this paper identifies three novel algorithmic transaction cost dimensions—semantic blind spots, black-box drift, and log decryption costs—and applies Design Science Research (DSR) principles to instantiate a computational governance artifact.  <bold>Results</bold>  : The proposed Three-Layered Governance Framework, comprising Deterministic Financial Guardrails, Semantic Communication Logs, and Dynamic Human-in-the-Loop Triggers, demonstrably prevents cascade failures across four simulated supply shock scenarios, preserving up to $40,000 in operational capital per autonomous transaction loop.  <bold>Managerial implications</bold>  : Supply chain executives and enterprise architects can deploy this modular, layer-by-layer governance model to safely scale autonomous procurement agents without sacrificing operational velocity or corporate accountability.  </p>","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Preprint"]},"bookOrReportDetails":{"publisher":"Research Square","yearOfPublication":2026},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.21203/rs.3.rs-9857965/v1"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","versionList":{"version":[{"id":"PPR1245533","source":"PPR","firstPublishDate":"2026-06-04","versionNumber":1,"pubTypeList":{"pubType":["preprint"]},"hasEvaluations":"N"}]},"versionNumber":1,"hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-05","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-05","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-04"},{"id":"42224752","source":"MED","pmid":"42224752","doi":"10.1016/j.ijmedinf.2026.106513","title":"Safety guardrails in patient-facing large language model systems for chronic disease self-management: a realist review.","authorString":"Zhao Y, Miao Y, Luo Y, Guo R, Wang H, Wu Y.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Zhao Y","firstName":"Yuhan","lastName":"Zhao","initials":"Y","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0008-0461-1913"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Nursing, Capital Medical University, No. 10 Xitoutiao, Youanmenwai, Fengtai District, Beijing 100069, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Miao Y","firstName":"Yiqun","lastName":"Miao","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Nursing, Capital Medical University, No. 10 Xitoutiao, Youanmenwai, Fengtai District, Beijing 100069, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Luo Y","firstName":"Yuan","lastName":"Luo","initials":"Y","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1198-3877"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Nursing, Capital Medical University, No. 10 Xitoutiao, Youanmenwai, Fengtai District, Beijing 100069, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Guo R","firstName":"Rongrong","lastName":"Guo","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Nursing, Capital Medical University, No. 10 Xitoutiao, Youanmenwai, Fengtai District, Beijing 100069, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Wang H","firstName":"Huiying","lastName":"Wang","initials":"H","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Nursing, Capital Medical University, No. 10 Xitoutiao, Youanmenwai, Fengtai District, Beijing 100069, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Wu Y","firstName":"Ying","lastName":"Wu","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Nursing, Capital Medical University, No. 10 Xitoutiao, Youanmenwai, Fengtai District, Beijing 100069, China. Electronic address: helenywu@vip.163.com."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1198-3877"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0008-0461-1913"}]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"218","journalIssueId":4222291,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Sep","monthOfPublication":9,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-09-01","journal":{"title":"International journal of medical informatics","medlineAbbreviation":"Int J Med Inform","isoabbreviation":"Int J Med Inform","nlmid":"9711057","essn":"1872-8243","issn":"1386-5056"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"106513","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Patient-facing large language model (LLM) systems are increasingly proposed as scalable tools for chronic disease self-management support. In this setting, safety depends not only on factual accuracy but also on whether outputs are interpretable, trusted, and used safely over time.<h4>Objective</h4>To explain how safety guardrails shape outcomes in patient-facing LLM-supported self-management across different task-risk, user, and interaction contexts.<h4>Methods</h4>We conducted a realist review of LLM-based or LLM-enabled generative conversational systems used for self-management of long-term physical health conditions. Searches of PubMed, Web of Science Core Collection, IEEE Xplore, ACM Digital Library, and arXiv covered all indexed years to 1 April 2026 and used terms for chronic disease or self-management, patient-facing conversational systems, and LLMs or generative AI, identifying 1,154 records before deduplication. The core evidence base comprised 21 studies and 38 context-mechanism-outcome configurations (CMOCs). The patient-facing self-management task, rather than disease label, was the unit of synthesis.<h4>Results</h4>At the study level, the dominant patient-facing task was coded as low risk in 6 studies, moderate risk in 11, and high risk in 4; 17 studies evaluated mainly single-turn interactions and 4 included multi-turn, sequential, or simulated-consultation elements. Most evidence concerned simulated or expert-judged patient-facing tasks rather than sustained real-world deployment. Three patterns recurred. Provenance-related safeguards improved transparency and checkability more consistently than they ensured safe downstream action. Communication-oriented safeguards improved readability or perceived comprehensibility while leaving recurring gaps in completeness or actionability. Boundary-control strategies, including source-bounded retrieval, clinician deferral, and escalation support, became more important as task actionability and interaction complexity increased.<h4>Conclusions</h4>In patient-facing chronic disease self-management, safety cannot be judged adequately by answer plausibility alone. This review develops a refined programme theory and a risk-linked, theory-generating heuristic framework, but many proposed mechanisms remain indirect and require real-world, longitudinal, multi-turn testing before deployment.","affiliation":"School of Nursing, Capital Medical University, No. 10 Xitoutiao, Youanmenwai, Fengtai District, Beijing 100069, China.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Review","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Chronic Disease","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"TH","qualifierName":"therapy","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Patient Safety"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Self-Management","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Generative Artificial Intelligence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Large Language Models"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Evaluation","Chronic disease","Self-management","Governance","Large Language Models","Patient-facing Ai","Safety Guardrails"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijmedinf.2026.106513"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-28","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-01","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-02","dateOfRevision":"2026-06-28","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-05-27","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-27"},{"id":"42483425","source":"MED","pmid":"42483425","doi":"10.3389/fdgth.2026.1871438","title":"A testable framework linking diagnostic AI contribution to outcome measurement in clinical decision support.","authorString":"Kirchhoff J, Berns F, Schieder C, Schobel J.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Kirchhoff J","firstName":"Jan","lastName":"Kirchhoff","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"DigiHealth Institute, Neu-Ulm University of Applied Sciences, Neu-Ulm, Germany."},{"affiliation":"medicalvalues GmbH, Karlsruhe, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Berns F","firstName":"Fabian","lastName":"Berns","initials":"F","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"medicalvalues GmbH, Karlsruhe, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Schieder C","firstName":"Christian","lastName":"Schieder","initials":"C","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Weiden Business School, OTH Amberg-Weiden, Weiden, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Schobel J","firstName":"Johannes","lastName":"Schobel","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"DigiHealth Institute, Neu-Ulm University of Applied Sciences, Neu-Ulm, Germany."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"8","journalIssueId":4117475,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Frontiers in digital health","medlineAbbreviation":"Front Digit Health","isoabbreviation":"Front Digit Health","nlmid":"101771889","essn":"2673-253X","issn":"2673-253X"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1871438","abstractText":"Artificial intelligence-enabled clinical decision support systems (AI-CDSS) are increasingly embedded in diagnostic and therapeutic workflows, yet evaluation, procurement, and governance often remain anchored in model-centric indicators such as discrimination, calibration, sensitivity, and specificity. These indicators are necessary but insufficient for determining whether a deployed AI-CDSS improves patient-relevant outcomes, clinical workflow, resource use, economic performance, clinician and patient experience, learning, and equity. This article proposes a testable conceptual framework linking the Diagnostic AI Contribution Score (DACS) to outcome-domain prioritization and auditable measurement design. The contribution is theory-building and methodological rather than empirically validating: no new patient-level data were generated or analyzed. We conducted a concept-driven structured narrative synthesis across clinical AI evaluation, clinical decision support, health-services research, value-based care, software measurement, AI governance, and large language model (LLM) deployment. We used the synthesis to derive a DACS-informed framework for selecting outcome domains, specifying exposure-action-outcome linkage, and defining minimum telemetry needed for auditability. The revised framework provides four core outputs: (i) a six-domain taxonomy of outcome metrics for AI-CDSS, separating learning/governance from equity; (ii) a comparison with existing AI evaluation and reporting frameworks to clarify the framework's incremental contribution; (iii) a DACS-to-domain mapping and explicitly illustrative prioritization heuristic, accompanied by a threshold-sensitivity logic; and (iv) a four-step measurement framework with minimum telemetry requirements and clinical implementation guardrails. LLM-specific extensions address provenance and version tracking, human mediation, documentation-quality audits, latency and compute metering, and workflow-mediated effects. We further specify clinical safety considerations including alert fatigue, override patterns by user group, automation bias and diagnostic anchoring, educational effects, de-implementation criteria, fallback workflows, integration with existing quality-management infrastructure, and patient-facing disclosure where appropriate. The proposed framework should be interpreted as a structured, hypothesis-generating model for proportional outcome measurement and governance. Future work should empirically test inter-rater reliability of DACS scoring, validate DACS-to-domain mappings through expert elicitation and prospective deployments, calibrate thresholds across use cases, and evaluate whether standardized telemetry improves attribution, monitoring, and accountability for AI-CDSS.","affiliation":"DigiHealth Institute, Neu-Ulm University of Applied Sciences, Neu-Ulm, Germany.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Clinical Decision Support Systems","Health Informatics","Learning Health Systems","Ai Governance","Large Language Models","Diagnostic Ai Contribution Score","Outcome-based Metrics"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fdgth.2026.1871438"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-07-22","dateOfCreation":"2026-07-22","firstIndexDate":"2026-07-22","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-07-07","firstPublicationDate":"2026-07-07"},{"id":"41943730","source":"MED","pmid":"41943730","pmcid":"PMC13048763","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13048763"]},"doi":"10.32481/djph.2026.03.15","title":"Maria in 2035: Delaware as a Living Laboratory for AI-Enabled Public Health.","authorString":"Hockstein NG, Callahan PJ.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Hockstein NG","firstName":"Neil G","lastName":"Hockstein","initials":"NG","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Chair, Delaware Health Care Commission."}]}},{"fullName":"Callahan PJ","firstName":"Patrick J","lastName":"Callahan","initials":"PJ","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Founder Keel3.ai, Co-Founder Acellus Health."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"12","journalIssueId":4173613,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Mar","monthOfPublication":3,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-03-01","journal":{"title":"Delaware journal of public health","medlineAbbreviation":"Dela J Public Health","isoabbreviation":"Dela J Public Health","nlmid":"101769648","essn":"2639-6378","issn":"2639-6378"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"114-116","affiliation":"Chair, Delaware Health Care Commission.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["discussion","Journal Article"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.32481/djph.2026.03.15"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13048763"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13048763?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by-nc-nd","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-04-07","dateOfCreation":"2026-04-07","firstIndexDate":"2026-04-07","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-04-08","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-03-31","firstPublicationDate":"2026-03-31"},{"id":"42472405","source":"MED","pmid":"42472405","doi":"10.1007/s44192-026-00552-y","title":"Comprehensive integration of mental health screening and triage with mental performance services through a sport science hub model in collegiate sport.","authorString":"Stamatis A, Rimer E, Wells N.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Stamatis A","firstName":"Andreas","lastName":"Stamatis","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9438-0558"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Health and Sport Sciences, University of Louisville, Louisville, USA. coach_stam@rocketmail.com."},{"affiliation":"University of Louisville Health, Sports Medicine Institute, Louisville, USA. coach_stam@rocketmail.com."}]}},{"fullName":"Rimer E","firstName":"Ernest","lastName":"Rimer","initials":"E","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"University of Louisville Health, Sports Medicine Institute, Louisville, USA."},{"affiliation":"Athletic Department, University of Louisville, Louisville, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Wells N","firstName":"Nathan","lastName":"Wells","initials":"N","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Athletic Department, University of Louisville, Louisville, USA."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9438-0558"}]},"journalInfo":{"journalIssueId":4222975,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jul","monthOfPublication":7,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-07-01","journal":{"title":"Discover mental health","medlineAbbreviation":"Discov Ment Health","isoabbreviation":"Discov Ment Health","nlmid":"9918350483906676","essn":"2731-4383","issn":"2731-4383"}},"pubYear":"2026","abstractText":"Collegiate sport environments often bring mental health (MH) and performance (MP) into close interaction; yet, screening, triage, referral, and MP support often remain operationally fragmented. This fragmentation may weaken follow-through after screening, limit coordination across clinical and performance services, and create uncertainty around privacy, scope boundaries, and accountability. In this Perspective, drawing on implementation science, integrated care, and systems-oriented organizational design, we suggest that a promising direction in athlete MH-MP support is not simply expanding screening availability, but building governed infrastructure that better connects identification to action. We propose a sport science hub model for coordinating two linked but distinct lanes: (1) structured MH screening, triage, and clinician-led referral workflows, and (2) MP profiling and skills-based support delivered within non-clinical scope. The model is organized around explicit guardrails, including role separation, privacy-secured and auditable access, non-punitive use protections, and emergency escalation pathways. We discuss the rationale for using structured screening and referral processes while acknowledging the limitations of current screening tools, including false-negative and false-positive concerns, and the importance of interpreting performance disruption in context rather than medicalizing normal variation. We conclude with governance principles and a forward-looking research agenda focused on implementation, trust, acceptability, screening accuracy, and prospective evaluation of integrated MH-MP systems.","affiliation":"Department of Health and Sport Sciences, University of Louisville, Louisville, USA. coach_stam@rocketmail.com.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Collegiate Athletes","Integrated Care","Implementation Science","Ncaa","Mental Toughness","Data Governance","Organizational Design","Smhat-1"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s44192-026-00552-y"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"N","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-07-19","firstIndexDate":"2026-07-21","dateOfRevision":"2026-07-19","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-07-19","firstPublicationDate":"2026-07-19"},{"id":"41818744","source":"MED","pmid":"41818744","pmcid":"PMC13022535","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13022535"]},"doi":"10.2196/87830","title":"Trustworthy AI-Augmented Objective Structured Clinical Examinations in Nursing Education: Taiwan-Japan Viewpoint on 5 AI Roles, Governance, and Cross-Border Implementation.","authorString":"Kubota K, Nishimura A, Seto R, Li L.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Kubota K","firstName":"Kazumi","lastName":"Kubota","initials":"K","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2270-2313"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Research Organization, Shimonoseki City University, Shimonoseki, Yamaguchi, Japan."},{"affiliation":"Department of Healthcare Information Management, University of Tokyo Hospital, Tokyo, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Nishimura A","firstName":"Ayako","lastName":"Nishimura","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6981-1667"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Healthcare, Division of Nursing, Tokyo Healthcare University, Tokyo, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Seto R","firstName":"Ryoma","lastName":"Seto","initials":"R","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6154-9342"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Healthcare, Division of Healthcare Informatics, Tokyo Healthcare University, Tokyo, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Li L","firstName":"Liu","lastName":"Li","initials":"L","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4800-644X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Show Chwan Health Care System, Taipei, Taiwan."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4800-644X"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6154-9342"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6981-1667"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2270-2313"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"10","journalIssueId":4136778,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Mar","monthOfPublication":3,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-03-01","journal":{"title":"JMIR formative research","medlineAbbreviation":"JMIR Form Res","isoabbreviation":"JMIR Form Res","nlmid":"101726394","essn":"2561-326X","issn":"2561-326X"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e87830","abstractText":"Generative artificial intelligence (AI) is arriving in high-stakes assessment; however, governance, validity evidence, and faculty readiness remain uneven. From a Taiwan-Japan perspective, we outline a pragmatic, transferable approach to integrating AI into nursing objective structured clinical examinations (OSCEs) using a 5-AI-role model-learning assistant, AI‑augmented standardized patient, assessment assistant, case generator, and learning analyst-mapped across pre-OSCE, peri-OSCE, and post-OSCE workflows with human-in-the-loop final judgment. Taiwan contributes agile interdisciplinary development, staged pilots (practice, mock OSCE, and limited high-stakes stations), A/B comparisons, and explainability-by-design logging that links scores to time-stamped evidence. Japan contributes robust policy scaffolding (national AI use guidance in K-12, a revised nursing model core curriculum with outcomes and assessment blueprints, and institutional research cultures that support auditability and quality assurance). We distill 4 cross-cutting governance pillars-human oversight, learning process transparency, ethics and safety, and traceability-into implementable techniques (machine-readable rubrics, standardized patient persona cards, bias monitoring, and targeted faculty development). Aligning with international principles (International Advisory Committee for AI; Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development; United Nations Educational, Scientific and Cultural Organization; World Health Organization; European Commission's High Level Expert Group; and National Institute of Standards and Technology), we propose a joint road map and shared registry to benchmark reliability, validity, equity, and workload impact. This viewpoint targets OSCE directors, nursing educators, and institutional leaders and provides a phase-gated governance blueprint rather than reporting original trial outcomes. Taiwan-led agility, complemented by Japan's standards-driven assurance, can form an Asia-Pacific reference model for trustworthy AI‑augmented OSCE in nursing education.","affiliation":"Research Organization, Shimonoseki City University, Shimonoseki, Yamaguchi, Japan.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Education, Nursing","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods","majorTopic_YN":"N"},{"abbreviation":"ST","qualifierName":"standards","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Educational Measurement","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods","majorTopic_YN":"N"},{"abbreviation":"ST","qualifierName":"standards","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Artificial Intelligence","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"ST","qualifierName":"standards","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Japan"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Taiwan"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","AI","Nursing Education","Standardized Patient","Osce","Objective Structured Clinical Examination","Generative Artificial Intelligence","Generative Ai","Taiwan-japan Collaboration","Assessment Governance","Transparency And Traceability"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2196/87830"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13022535"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13022535?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":1,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-07-11","dateOfCreation":"2026-03-12","firstIndexDate":"2026-03-13","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-03-28","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-03-12","firstPublicationDate":"2026-03-12"},{"id":"41853155","source":"MED","pmid":"41853155","pmcid":"PMC12992295","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12992295"]},"doi":"10.3389/fpubh.2026.1774367","title":"Equity at the point of care: auditing AI-supported resource allocation in obstetric emergencies.","authorString":"Gao F, Xie D.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Gao F","firstName":"Fan","lastName":"Gao","initials":"F","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"West China Second University Hospital, Sichuan University, Chengdu, China."},{"affiliation":"Key Laboratory of Birth Defects and Related Diseases of Women and Children, Ministry of Education, Sichuan University, Chengdu, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Xie D","firstName":"Danli","lastName":"Xie","initials":"D","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"West China Second University Hospital, Sichuan University, Chengdu, China."},{"affiliation":"Key Laboratory of Birth Defects and Related Diseases of Women and Children, Ministry of Education, Sichuan University, Chengdu, China."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"14","journalIssueId":4110762,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Frontiers in public health","medlineAbbreviation":"Front Public Health","isoabbreviation":"Front Public Health","nlmid":"101616579","essn":"2296-2565","issn":"2296-2565"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1774367","abstractText":"Equity in artificial intelligence-supported obstetric emergency care should be assessed as a service outcome, not as a model property, because preventable harm is mediated through operational delay, escalation, and resource contention. This perspective synthesizes implementation-relevant literature and quality and safety principles to propose a practical approach for translating equity from an abstract aspiration into auditable operations. We argue for using \"avoidable delay\" as a shared denominator across emergencies (such as postpartum hemorrhage, hypertensive crises, and obstetric sepsis) and for evaluating equity across the full chain from risk detection to resource delivery. We propose a minimum fairness audit set that can be captured largely from routine timestamps and logs: consistency of triggering across comparable presentations; timeliness of first response and definitive treatment; readiness of critical resources (blood products at bedside, operating room access and anesthesia start, and monitored-bed availability); completion of escalation and transfer steps; and structured documentation of overrides, missing data, and exception reasons. We further outline governance requirements-clear cross-service accountability, change control with re-audit after threshold or workflow modifications, and patient-facing transparency-so that equity is treated as an accountable, measured, and managed risk item within routine quality improvement rather than a one-time publication metric. In this perspective, \"AI-supported\" is used as a pragmatic umbrella to encompass deployed algorithmic decision-support systems at the point of care, including static rules-based early warning triggers, machine-learning risk scores, and operational routing/queuing engines; the Minimum Fairness Audit Set (MFAS) audits the service-chain consequences of any such trigger when it is coupled to an executable pathway with auditable timestamps.","affiliation":"West China Second University Hospital, Sichuan University, Chengdu, China.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["brief-report","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Emergencies"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Obstetrics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Pregnancy"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Resource Allocation"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Artificial Intelligence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Emergency Medical Services"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Medical Audit"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Female"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Quality improvement","Resource allocation","Postpartum hemorrhage","Clinical Decision Support","Obstetric Emergencies","Avoidable Delay","Equity Audit"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fpubh.2026.1774367"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12992295"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12992295?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-07-11","dateOfCreation":"2026-03-19","firstIndexDate":"2026-03-20","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-03-18","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-03-03","firstPublicationDate":"2026-03-03"},{"id":"41681893","source":"MED","pmid":"41681893","pmcid":"PMC12897117","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12897117"]},"doi":"10.3390/cancers18030421","title":"Smart Lies and Sharp Eyes: Pragmatic Artificial Intelligence for Cancer Pathology: Promise, Pitfalls, and Access Pathways.","authorString":"Bani MA.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Bani MA","firstName":"Mohamed-Amine","lastName":"Bani","initials":"MA","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4834-4905"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medical Biology and Pathology, Gustave Roussy, 94800 Villejuif, France."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4834-4905"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"3","volume":"18","journalIssueId":4120554,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jan","monthOfPublication":1,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Cancers","medlineAbbreviation":"Cancers (Basel)","isoabbreviation":"Cancers (Basel)","nlmid":"101526829","essn":"2072-6694","issn":"2072-6694"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"421","abstractText":"<b>Background</b>: Whole-slide imaging and algorithmic advances have moved computational pathology from research to routine consideration. Despite notable successes, real-world deployment remains limited by generalization, validation gaps, and human-factor risks, which can be amplified in resource-constrained settings. <b>Content/Scope</b>: This narrative review and implementation perspective summarizes clinically proximate AI capabilities in cancer pathology, including lesion detection, metastasis triage, mitosis counting, immunomarker quantification, and prediction of selected molecular alterations from routine histology. We also summarize recurring failure modes, dataset leakage, stain/batch/site shifts, misleading explanation overlays, calibration errors, and automation bias, and distinguish applications supported by external retrospective validation, prospective reader-assistance or real-world studies, and regulatory-cleared use. We translate these evidence patterns into a practical checklist covering dataset design, external and temporal validation, robustness testing, calibration and uncertainty handling, explainability sanity checks, and workflow-safety design. <b>Equity Focus</b>: We propose a stepwise adoption pathway for low- and middle-income countries: prioritize narrow, high-impact use cases; match compute and storage requirements to local infrastructure; standardize pre-analytics; pool validation cohorts; and embed quality management, privacy protections, and audit trails. <b>Conclusions</b>: AI can already serve as a reliable second reader for selected tasks, reducing variance and freeing expert time. Safe, equitable deployment requires disciplined validation, calibrated uncertainty, and guardrails against human-factor failure. With pragmatic scoping and shared infrastructure, pathology programs can realize benefits while preserving trust and accountability.","affiliation":"Department of Medical Biology and Pathology, Gustave Roussy, 94800 Villejuif, France.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Validation","Digital Pathology","Low- And Middle-income Countries","External Validation","Deep Learning","Automation Bias","Biomarker Prediction","Computational Pathology","Stain Normalization"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/cancers18030421"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12897117"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12897117?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":2,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-02-13","dateOfCreation":"2026-02-13","firstIndexDate":"2026-02-13","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-02-14","dateOfRevision":"2026-02-16","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-01-28","firstPublicationDate":"2026-01-28"},{"id":"42282245","source":"MED","pmid":"42282245","pmcid":"PMC13250432","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13250432"]},"doi":"10.1177/20552076261459551","title":"Safeguarding AI-driven digital health - An adaptation of the Swiss cheese model for safety.","authorString":"Denecke K.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Denecke K","firstName":"Kerstin","lastName":"Denecke","initials":"K","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6691-396X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Institute Patient-centered Digital Health, Bern University of Applied Sciences, Biel, Switzerland."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6691-396X"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"12","journalIssueId":4085000,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jan-Dec","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Digital health","medlineAbbreviation":"Digit Health","isoabbreviation":"Digit Health","nlmid":"101690863","essn":"2055-2076","issn":"2055-2076"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"20552076261459551","abstractText":"The use of artificial intelligence (AI) in digital health applications is increasing rapidly, creating new opportunities as well as safety challenges. AI-related errors can be systematic, repeating consistently across similar cases and, when deployed at scale, potentially affecting multiple patients within a short time. In this paper, we extend James Reason's classic distinction between person-based and system-based approaches to error by incorporating a third, technology-based perspective to account for failures inherent to AI-driven digital health systems and their lifecycle, including issues related to data, model design, deployment, and monitoring. Protecting patients in an increasingly AI-driven healthcare system requires not only model-centric controls, but an integrated set of overlapping technical, organizational, and regulatory safeguards designed explicitly for AI-driven digital health.To explore how such safeguards may be structured, we conducted a qualitative, exploratory analysis of 15 real-world incidents retrieved from an AI incident database. We extracted contributing factors for these incidents and conducted a thematic analysis. Aiming for conceptual alignment with Reason's Swiss cheese model for safety, the themes were grouped into higher-level system layers. As a result, we propose five protective layers to safeguard AI-driven digital health: (1) data governance and quality assurance; (2) model development, evaluation and change control; (3) sociotechnical integration and human oversight; (4) governance, regulatory and ethical compliance; and (5) post-market monitoring, incident response, and learning. We propose safeguards that were also derived from the incident analysis to operationalize the layers, but they are not intended to represent an exhaustive or consensus-based set of recommendations, but rather an exploratory, incident-informed framework to support analysis and system design. Finally, we argue for a \"just culture\" in AI-driven digital health, where researchers, healthcare professionals, and patients are encouraged to report critical incidents and errors, as this is essential for learning and system improvement.","affiliation":"Institute Patient-centered Digital Health, Bern University of Applied Sciences, Biel, Switzerland.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Adverse effects","Patient Safety","Swiss Cheese Model","Digital Health","Digitalovigilance","Unexpected Harm"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1177/20552076261459551"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13250432"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13250432?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by-nc","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-12","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-12","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-13","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-13","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-09","firstPublicationDate":"2026-01-01"},{"id":"42141031","source":"MED","pmid":"42141031","pmcid":"PMC13179377","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13179377"]},"doi":"10.1038/s41746-026-02740-0","title":"Large language models require a new form of oversight: capability-based monitoring.","authorString":"Kellogg KC, Ye B, Hu Y, Savova GK, Wallace B, Bitterman DS.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Kellogg KC","firstName":"Katherine C","lastName":"Kellogg","initials":"KC","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"MIT Sloan School of Management, Boston, MA, USA. kkellogg@mit.edu."}]}},{"fullName":"Ye B","firstName":"Bingyang","lastName":"Ye","initials":"B","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"AI in Medicine Program, Mass General Brigham, Harvard Medical School, Boston, MA, USA."},{"affiliation":"Department of Radiation Oncology, Brigham and Women's Hospital/Dana-Farber Cancer Institute, Boston, MA, USA."},{"affiliation":"Department of Computer Science, Brandeis University, Waltham, MA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Hu Y","firstName":"Yifan","lastName":"Hu","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Harvard John A. Paulson School Of Engineering And Applied Sciences, Cambridge, MA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Savova GK","firstName":"Guergana K","lastName":"Savova","initials":"GK","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Computation Health Informatics Program, Boston Children's Hospital, Harvard Medical School, Boston, MA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Wallace B","firstName":"Byron","lastName":"Wallace","initials":"B","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Khoury College of Computer Sciences, Northeastern University, Boston, MA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Bitterman DS","firstName":"Danielle S","lastName":"Bitterman","initials":"DS","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"AI in Medicine Program, Mass General Brigham, Harvard Medical School, Boston, MA, USA. dbitterman@bwh.harvard.edu."},{"affiliation":"Department of Radiation Oncology, Brigham and Women's Hospital/Dana-Farber Cancer Institute, Boston, MA, USA. dbitterman@bwh.harvard.edu."},{"affiliation":"Computation Health Informatics Program, Boston Children's Hospital, Harvard Medical School, Boston, MA, USA. dbitterman@bwh.harvard.edu."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"9","journalIssueId":4188236,"dateOfPublication":"2026 May","monthOfPublication":5,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-05-01","journal":{"title":"NPJ digital medicine","medlineAbbreviation":"NPJ Digit Med","isoabbreviation":"NPJ Digit Med","nlmid":"101731738","essn":"2398-6352","issn":"2398-6352"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"375","affiliation":"MIT Sloan School of Management, Boston, MA, USA. kkellogg@mit.edu.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Letter"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"grantId":"ACS.ASTRO-CSDG-24-1244514-01-CTPS.pc.gr.222210","agency":"American Cancer Society","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"R01 CA294033","agency":"NCI NIH HHS","acronym":"CA","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"U24CA248010","agency":"National Cancer Institute","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"UM1TR004408","agency":"National Center for Advancing Translational Sciences","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"R01CA294033-01","agency":"Division of Cancer Prevention, National Cancer Institute","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"U24CA248010","agency":"NCI NIH HHS","acronym":"CA","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"U54CA274516-01A1","agency":"National Cancer Institute","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"U24 CA248010","agency":"NCI NIH HHS","acronym":"CA","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"UM1TR004408","agency":"NCATS NIH HHS","acronym":"TR","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"ME-2024C2-37484","agency":"Patient-Centered Outcomes Research Institute","acronym":"PCORI","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"U54CA274516-01A1","agency":"NCI NIH HHS","acronym":"CA","orderIn":0}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41746-026-02740-0"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13179377"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13179377?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":2,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-05-15","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-15","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-17","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-21","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-05-15","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-15"},{"id":"PPR1237467","source":"PPR","doi":"10.64898/2026.05.19.26353565","title":"Evidence-Graded Decision Authorization for Safe Clinical AI: A Constrained Reasoning Framework","authorString":"Lin C, Lin J, Lin Y.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Lin C","firstName":"Che","lastName":"Lin","initials":"C"},{"fullName":"Lin J","firstName":"Jia-Yi","lastName":"Lin","initials":"J"},{"fullName":"Lin Y","firstName":"Yao-San","lastName":"Lin","initials":"Y"}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5922-5971"}]},"pubYear":"2026","abstractText":"Clinical AI systems have achieved strong predictive performance; however, prediction accuracy is not sufficient for clinical safety. Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) improves factual accuracy, and general-purpose LLM guardrails constrain surface-level output safety, but these mechanisms do not govern the inferential gap between available clinical evidence and permissible clinical claims. We propose Evidence-Graded Decision Authorization (EGDA), a framework that separates evidence extraction, sufficiency assessment, and claim-level authorization through domain-specific rules. In a controlled experiment using 60 breast cancer decision-snapshot cases (1,260 system outputs across three arms evaluated by LLM-as-Judge with expert calibration), EGDA reduced the unjustified inference rate to 8.0% (vs. 48.7% for unconstrained LLM and 47.7% for RAG; risk difference vs. unconstrained −40.7%, 95% CI −46.9 to −34.0, p < 0.001), raised the appropriate refusal rate to 95.0% (vs. 56.9% and 56.9%; risk difference vs. unconstrained +38.1%, 95% CI +31.3 to +44.5, p < 0.001), and achieved the highest factual correctness at 96.4% (vs. 69.8% and 74.5%). An unexpected finding was that retrieval-augmented generation without an authorization gate failed to reduce unjustified inference relative to the unconstrained baseline (47.7% vs. 48.7%, p = 0.870) and produced no improvement in appropriate refusal (56.9% vs. 56.9%, p = 1.0), showing that information supply alone is not sufficient for inferential governance. We argue that domain-specific, evidence-graded reasoning governance should serve as a deployment reference standard for safety-critical clinical AI. <h4>Highlights</h4>  Evidence-graded authorization is formalized for safer LLM clinical decisions Three behavioral safety metrics gauge inferential governance, not accuracy RAG-only systems do not reduce unjustified inference vs unconstrained LLM Proposed framework cuts unjustified inference rate from 48.7% to 8.0% Robustness confirmed across two model versions and via component ablation","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Preprint"]},"bookOrReportDetails":{"publisher":"medRxiv","yearOfPublication":2026},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.64898/2026.05.19.26353565"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-24","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-24","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-22"},{"id":"41788189","source":"MED","pmid":"41788189","pmcid":"PMC12959241","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12959241"]},"doi":"10.1093/ehjdh/ztag019","title":"Total product lifecycle regulatory considerations and recommendations for generative AI-enabled medical devices.","authorString":"Armoundas AA, Singh JP.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Armoundas AA","firstName":"Antonis A","lastName":"Armoundas","initials":"AA","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5006-1547"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Cardiovascular Research Center, Massachusetts General Hospital, 149 13th Street, Charlestown, MA 02129, USA."},{"affiliation":"Broad Institute, Massachusetts Institute of Technology, Cambridge, MA 02139, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Singh JP","firstName":"Jagmeet P","lastName":"Singh","initials":"JP","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Cardiology Division, Cardiac Arrhythmia Service, Massachusetts General Hospital, Boston, MA 02215, USA."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5006-1547"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"3","volume":"7","journalIssueId":4143256,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Apr","monthOfPublication":4,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-04-01","journal":{"title":"European heart journal. Digital health","medlineAbbreviation":"Eur Heart J Digit Health","isoabbreviation":"Eur Heart J Digit Health","nlmid":"101778323","essn":"2634-3916","issn":"2634-3916"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"ztag019","abstractText":"As generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) emerges within the healthcare ecosystem, the regulatory environment surrounding these technologies remains fragmented. Importantly, GenAI in healthcare requires adapting the established Total Product Lifecycle (TPLC) paradigm to non-deterministic, rapidly evolving software, translating it into concrete steps for pre-market evaluation, change controls, and post-market performance monitoring to keep devices safe and effective as they change. This manuscript elucidates the need for developers, healthcare providers, and clinicians to engage proactively with regulatory compliance during the development and deployment phases of genAI-enabled medical devices. By understanding the nuances of national and international regulations, stakeholders can better navigate the unique risks of the evolving landscape of GenAI-enabled medical devices, while ensuring patient safety and outcome optimisation. Finally, this manuscript highlights the importance of the TPLC approach in managing the risks associated with GenAI-enabled technologies, offering recommendations for improving health outcomes and balancing the interests of various stakeholders.","affiliation":"Cardiovascular Research Center, Massachusetts General Hospital, 149 13th Street, Charlestown, MA 02129, USA.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["discussion","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"grantId":"1 R01 HL161008-01","agency":"NIH","orderIn":0},{"agency":"American Heart Association","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"17UNPG33840017","agency":"Institute of Precision Medicine","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"1 R01 HL135335-01","agency":"NIH","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"1 R21 HL137870-01","agency":"NIH","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"3R21EB026164-02S1","agency":"NIH","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"1 R21EB026164-01","agency":"NIH","orderIn":0},{"agency":"RICBAC Foundation","orderIn":0}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Regulation","Performance","Policy","Health care","Equity","Generative Artificial Intelligence","Large Language Models"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/ehjdh/ztag019"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12959241"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12959241?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":2,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-03-06","dateOfCreation":"2026-03-06","firstIndexDate":"2026-03-07","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-03-05","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-02-03","firstPublicationDate":"2026-02-03"},{"id":"41740154","source":"MED","pmid":"41740154","pmcid":"PMC12935426","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12935426"]},"doi":"10.2196/80028","title":"Scalable Agile Framework for Execution in AI for Medical AI Ethics Policy Design in Small- and Medium-Sized Enterprises.","authorString":"Nemteanu I, Mancebo A, Joe L, Lopez R, Lopez P, Pettine WW.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Nemteanu I","firstName":"Ion","lastName":"Nemteanu","initials":"I","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0009-6680-1728"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Nemsee LLC, San Diego, CA, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Mancebo A Jr","firstName":"Adir","lastName":"Mancebo","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0007-4791-8950"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Data Science Alliance, San Diego, CA, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Joe L","firstName":"Leslie","lastName":"Joe","initials":"L","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0006-2399-2788"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Data Science Alliance, San Diego, CA, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Lopez R","firstName":"Ryan","lastName":"Lopez","initials":"R","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0009-8646-0035"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Data Science Alliance, San Diego, CA, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Lopez P","firstName":"Patricia","lastName":"Lopez","initials":"P","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0008-7095-6485"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Data Science Alliance, San Diego, CA, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Pettine WW","firstName":"Warren Woodrich","lastName":"Pettine","initials":"WW","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0063-0902"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry, University of Utah, 501 Chipeta Way, Salt Lake City, UT, 84108, United States, 1 801-583-2500."},{"affiliation":"Mountain Biometrics, Inc., Salt Lake City, UT, United States."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0063-0902"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0006-2399-2788"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0007-4791-8950"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0008-7095-6485"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0009-6680-1728"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0009-8646-0035"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"28","journalIssueId":4112524,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Feb","monthOfPublication":2,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-02-01","journal":{"title":"Journal of medical Internet research","medlineAbbreviation":"J Med Internet Res","isoabbreviation":"J Med Internet Res","nlmid":"100959882","essn":"1438-8871","issn":"1439-4456"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e80028","abstractText":"<h4>Unlabelled</h4>Artificial intelligence (AI) is transforming patient care, but it also raises ethical questions, such as bias and transparency. While a range of well-established frameworks exist to guide responsible AI practice, most were designed for academic or regulatory settings and can be hard to operationalize within fast-moving, resource-limited small and medium-sized enterprises (SMEs). We report on the collaborative design of the SAFE-AI (Scalable Agile Framework for Execution in AI), an approach that embeds ethical safeguards, including fairness, transparency, responsibility metrics, and continuous monitoring, directly into standard Agile development cycles. In keeping with established Agile principles, SAFE-AI provides \"just enough structure\" to integrate ethical oversight into existing workflows without prescribing extensive new governance layers. Similar to other Agile frameworks, such as Scrum, which is described as a \"lightweight framework\" designed to help teams solve complex problems through iterative learning and minimal process overhead, SAFE-AI aims to remain practical for organizations that may not have dedicated ethics or compliance staff. Rather than simplifying technical methods, SAFE-AI simplifies when and how ethical review is triggered and documented, making responsible AI practices feasible even in environments with limited ethics, governance, or compliance resources. SAFE-AI assumes the presence of qualified data scientists and engineers, and it does not replace the need for statistical or technical expertise but instead provides a lightweight structure for coordinating and documenting work that those experts already perform. We followed a design-science, practice-oriented approach over 20 weeks. After a discovery workshop, a cross-functional team was assembled that included SME employees, ethics researchers, and academic partners. The SME's role was limited to informing design constraints and feasibility considerations during the co-design phase. No operational pilot or production deployment was conducted as part of this study. To reduce the risk of internal design bias and improve generalizability, we also consulted external stakeholders through structured feedback sessions, including clinicians, health care domain experts, and regulatory specialists. Their feedback was incorporated into each prototype-feedback cycle, ensuring that priorities reflected not only the SME's immediate context but also broader clinical and regulatory perspectives. The co-design process produced a 4-phase SAFE-AI life cycle: discovery, assessment, development, and monitoring. SAFE-AI's phase-specific checklists meld acceptance, fairness, and transparency metrics into each Agile sprint. A novel scenario-based probability analogy mapping method was added to translate model risk and uncertainty into plain-language narratives for nontechnical stakeholders, forming the framework's core \"responsibility metrics\" layer. SAFE-AI is presented as a proposed framework showing that meaningful ethical safeguards can be embedded easily within common workflows used by SMEs that already use basic Agile or iterative development practices. Its checklist-driven phases and automatic review triggers provide a defensible way to track fairness, transparency, and responsibility throughout the model lifecycle.","affiliation":"Nemsee LLC, San Diego, CA, United States.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","risk assessment","Transparency","Medical Ethics","Machine Learning","Ehealth","Small Business","Agile Methodology","Digital Health","Software Development Life Cycle","Trustworthy Ai","Fairness Metrics"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2196/80028"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12935426"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12935426?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":1,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCreation":"2026-02-25","firstIndexDate":"2026-02-25","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-02-26","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-02-25","firstPublicationDate":"2026-02-25"},{"id":"42050181","source":"MED","pmid":"42050181","pmcid":"PMC13321079","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13321079"]},"doi":"10.1038/s41591-026-04368-9","title":"Clinical trials for continuously monitored and updated AI systems.","authorString":"van Amsterdam WAC, Oberst M, Feng J, Wiens J, Tang S, Joshi S, Ranganath R, Sendak M, Shalit U, Vogt JE, Beaulieu-Jones B, Mamdani M, Kent D, Heagerty PJ, Fleming TR, Goldenberg A.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"van Amsterdam WAC","firstName":"Wouter A C","lastName":"van Amsterdam","initials":"WAC","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3181-0810"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Julius Center for Health Sciences and Primary Care, University Medical Center Utrecht, Utrecht, the Netherlands."}]}},{"fullName":"Oberst M","firstName":"Michael","lastName":"Oberst","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1720-6702"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Johns Hopkins University, Baltimore, MD, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Feng J","firstName":"Jean","lastName":"Feng","initials":"J","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2041-3104"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"University of California, San Francisco, CA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Wiens J","firstName":"Jenna","lastName":"Wiens","initials":"J","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1057-7722"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"University of Michigan, Ann Arbor, MI, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Tang S","firstName":"Shengpu","lastName":"Tang","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4213-2015"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Emory University, Atlanta, GA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Joshi S","firstName":"Shalmali","lastName":"Joshi","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Columbia University, New York, NY, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Ranganath R","firstName":"Rajesh","lastName":"Ranganath","initials":"R","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2854-3376"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"New York University, New York, NY, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Sendak M","firstName":"Mark","lastName":"Sendak","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5828-4497"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Vega Health, Durham, NC, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Shalit U","firstName":"Uri","lastName":"Shalit","initials":"U","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Tel Aviv University, Tel Aviv, Israel."}]}},{"fullName":"Vogt JE","firstName":"Julia E","lastName":"Vogt","initials":"JE","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"ETH Zurich, Zurich, Switzerland."}]}},{"fullName":"Beaulieu-Jones B","firstName":"Brett","lastName":"Beaulieu-Jones","initials":"B","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6700-1468"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"University of Chicago, Chicago, IL, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Mamdani M","firstName":"Muhammad","lastName":"Mamdani","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Ontario Health, Toronto, Ontario, Canada."},{"affiliation":"University of Toronto, Toronto, Ontario, Canada."},{"affiliation":"Vector Institute, Toronto, Ontario, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Kent D","firstName":"David","lastName":"Kent","initials":"D","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Tufts University, Boston, MA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Heagerty PJ","firstName":"Patrick J","lastName":"Heagerty","initials":"PJ","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"University of Washington, Seattle, WA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Fleming TR","firstName":"Thomas R","lastName":"Fleming","initials":"TR","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0420-5068"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"University of Washington, Seattle, WA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Goldenberg A","firstName":"Anna","lastName":"Goldenberg","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"University of Toronto, Toronto, Ontario, Canada. anna.goldenberg@utoronto.ca."},{"affiliation":"Vector Institute, Toronto, Ontario, Canada. anna.goldenberg@utoronto.ca."},{"affiliation":"Hospital for Sick Children, Toronto, Ontario, Canada. anna.goldenberg@utoronto.ca."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5828-4497"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0420-5068"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1057-7722"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3181-0810"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4213-2015"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6700-1468"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1720-6702"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2041-3104"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2854-3376"}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"6","volume":"32","journalIssueId":4218836,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-06-01","journal":{"title":"Nature medicine","medlineAbbreviation":"Nat Med","isoabbreviation":"Nat Med","nlmid":"9502015","essn":"1546-170X","issn":"1078-8956"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1972-1974","affiliation":"Julius Center for Health Sciences and Primary Care, University Medical Center Utrecht, Utrecht, the Netherlands.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"grantId":"R37-AI29168","agency":"U.S. Department of Health & Human Services | National Institutes of Health (NIH)","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"R01 HS027431","agency":"AHRQ HHS","acronym":"HS","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"R01HS027431","agency":"U.S. Department of Health & Human Services | Agency for Healthcare Research and Quality (AHRQ)","orderIn":0}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41591-026-04368-9"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13321079"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13321079?pdf=render"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41591-026-04368-9"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"Y","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"Y","manuscriptId":"NIHMS2180591","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-04-28","firstIndexDate":"2026-04-30","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-08-19","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-01"},{"id":"41668809","source":"MED","pmid":"41668809","pmcid":"PMC12883952","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12883952"]},"doi":"10.3389/fphar.2025.1699906","title":"Designing integrated early-phase protocols to reduce substantial modifications, including considerations for patient cohorts - a multi-stakeholder consensus view for a practical approach in Germany.","authorString":"Schultz-Heienbrok R, Baumann S, Böhm R, Bonertz A, Breithaupt-Groegler K, Buckpesch-Heberer U, Coenen M, Erb-Zohar K, Faisst N, Grass G, Höchel J, Kovar A, Muehlenbroich A, Rengelshausen J, Riedel C, Schug B, Sudhop T, Warnke A, Ziegele B.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Schultz-Heienbrok R","firstName":"R","lastName":"Schultz-Heienbrok","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Charité Research Organisation GmbH, Berlin, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Baumann S","firstName":"S","lastName":"Baumann","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"CRS Clinical Research Services Management, Berlin, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Böhm R","firstName":"R","lastName":"Böhm","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"SocraTec R&D, Oberursel, Germany."},{"affiliation":"Universitätsklinikum Schleswig-Holstein, Kiel, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Bonertz A","firstName":"A","lastName":"Bonertz","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Paul-Ehrlich-Institut, Langen, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Breithaupt-Groegler K","firstName":"K","lastName":"Breithaupt-Groegler","initials":"K","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Consultant Clinical Pharmacology, Frankfurt, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Buckpesch-Heberer U","firstName":"U","lastName":"Buckpesch-Heberer","initials":"U","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Paul-Ehrlich-Institut, Langen, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Coenen M","firstName":"M","lastName":"Coenen","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0439-4409"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Institute of Clinical Chemistry and Clinical Pharmacology, University Hospital Bonn, Bonn, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Erb-Zohar K","firstName":"K","lastName":"Erb-Zohar","initials":"K","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Clinphase, Schotten, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Faisst N","firstName":"N","lastName":"Faisst","initials":"N","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Dr. Falk Pharma GmbH, Freiburg, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Grass G","firstName":"G","lastName":"Grass","initials":"G","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Spezialisierte Ethikkommission für besondere Verfahren, Bonn, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Höchel J","firstName":"J","lastName":"Höchel","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Bayer AG, Berlin, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Kovar A","firstName":"A","lastName":"Kovar","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Sanofi, Frankfurt, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Muehlenbroich A","firstName":"A","lastName":"Muehlenbroich","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Novartis, Basel, Switzerland."}]}},{"fullName":"Rengelshausen J","firstName":"J","lastName":"Rengelshausen","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Uniklinik RWTH Aachen, Aachen, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Riedel C","firstName":"C","lastName":"Riedel","initials":"C","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Bundesinstitut für Arzneimittel und Medizinprodukte, Bonn, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Schug B","firstName":"B","lastName":"Schug","initials":"B","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"SocraTec R&D, Oberursel, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Sudhop T","firstName":"T","lastName":"Sudhop","initials":"T","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Bundesinstitut für Arzneimittel und Medizinprodukte, Bonn, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Warnke A","firstName":"A","lastName":"Warnke","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"SocraTec R&D, Oberursel, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Ziegele B","firstName":"B","lastName":"Ziegele","initials":"B","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Institute of Clinical Chemistry and Clinical Pharmacology, University Hospital Bonn, Bonn, Germany."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0439-4409"}]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"16","journalIssueId":3893159,"dateOfPublication":"2025 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2025,"printPublicationDate":"2025-01-01","journal":{"title":"Frontiers in pharmacology","medlineAbbreviation":"Front Pharmacol","isoabbreviation":"Front Pharmacol","nlmid":"101548923","essn":"1663-9812","issn":"1663-9812"}},"pubYear":"2025","pageInfo":"1699906","abstractText":"• Frequent substantial modifications for initiating new trial parts slow down development and reduce transparency. • Combining healthy volunteers and patients in a single early-phase trial raises safety, feasibility, and regulatory concerns. • A multi-stakeholder consensus in Germany identified regulatory pathways to enable seamless transitions within integrated protocols for non-ATMP (advanced therapy medicinal product), non-oncology trials. • The EMA FIH (first-in-human) guideline supports predefined transitions if scope, safety thresholds, and decision rules are clearly described. • The German guardrail concept clarifies when transitions do not require a substantial modification, enhancing planning certainty. • Actionable recommendations are provided on protocol design, decision criteria, and governance structures to enable early inclusion of patients without compromising safety or compliance. • First positive experience has been gained with the presented framework in Germany.","affiliation":"Charité Research Organisation GmbH, Berlin, Germany.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["other","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Clinical Trials","First-in-human","Investigational Medicinal Product","Complex Trial Design","Integrated Protocols","Substantial Modification"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fphar.2025.1699906"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12883952"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12883952?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-02-11","dateOfCreation":"2026-02-11","firstIndexDate":"2026-02-11","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-02-14","dateOfRevision":"2026-02-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-01-26","firstPublicationDate":"2026-01-26"},{"id":"42222849","source":"MED","pmid":"42222849","pmcid":"PMC13219290","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13219290"]},"doi":"10.3389/frai.2026.1816692","title":"Recent advances in defending the privacy attacks of large language models for healthcare applications: a concise review.","authorString":"Aroua R, Kammoun I, Louati MA, Adjeroh DA, Duan T.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Aroua R","firstName":"Rahma","lastName":"Aroua","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Computer Science, College of Computing, Grand Valley State University, Allendale, MI, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Kammoun I","firstName":"Islem","lastName":"Kammoun","initials":"I","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Computer Science, College of Computing, Grand Valley State University, Allendale, MI, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Louati MA","firstName":"Mahmoud Aziz","lastName":"Louati","initials":"MA","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Computer Science, College of Computing, Grand Valley State University, Allendale, MI, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Adjeroh DA","firstName":"Donald A","lastName":"Adjeroh","initials":"DA","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Lane Department of Computer Science and Electrical Engineering, West Virginia University, Morgantown, WV, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Duan T","firstName":"Tiehang","lastName":"Duan","initials":"T","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Computer Science, College of Computing, Grand Valley State University, Allendale, MI, United States."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"9","journalIssueId":4117457,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Frontiers in artificial intelligence","medlineAbbreviation":"Front Artif Intell","isoabbreviation":"Front Artif Intell","nlmid":"101770551","essn":"2624-8212","issn":"2624-8212"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1816692","abstractText":"Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly adopted across healthcare applications, including clinical decision support and medical documentation systems. However, their deployment in medical settings raises significant privacy and security concerns due to the sensitivity of protected health information and stringent regulatory requirements. Recent studies have shown that LLM-based medical applications can cause medical data leakage through related API usages. This raises ethical concerns and threatens HIPAA (Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act) compliance, blocking the trustworthy deployment of LLMs in the medical domain. This review examines emerging privacy attacks and the related defense approaches in LLMs with a special focus on healthcare applications. We organize the attack and defense approaches based on Secure AI Framework (SAIF), systematically covering vulnerabilities across the data, model, application and infrastructure layers. We performed detailed analysis on major classes of privacy attacks and further examined state-of-the-art defense mechanisms under realistic healthcare application scenarios. A key finding of this review is the persistent privacy-utility tradeoff: stronger privacy protection often leads to substantial degradation in clinical performance, which may render models unsuitable for mission-critical medical tasks. The healthcare related deployment of LLMs needs to be evaluated against clinical utility thresholds rather than generic language modeling metrics. We identify open challenges in evaluation, system-level deployment and regulatory verification, and outline research directions that balance clinical utility with regulatory compliance.","affiliation":"Department of Computer Science, College of Computing, Grand Valley State University, Allendale, MI, United States.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Healthcare Informatics","Differential Privacy","Hipaa Compliance","Trustworthy Ai","Large Language Models"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/frai.2026.1816692"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13219290"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13219290?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-01","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-01","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-02","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-02","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-05-15","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-15"},{"id":"41988082","source":"MED","pmid":"41988082","pmcid":"PMC13077672","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13077672"]},"doi":"10.1093/pnasnexus/pgag076","title":"Neurodivergent influenceability in agentic AI as a contingent solution to the AI alignment problem.","authorString":"Hernández-Espinosa A, Abrahão FS, Witkowski O, Zenil H.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Hernández-Espinosa A","firstName":"Alberto","lastName":"Hernández-Espinosa","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0007-4791-0625"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Oxford Immune Algorithmics, Oxford University Innovation, London Institute for Healthcare Engineering, London, United Kingdom."},{"affiliation":"The Arrival Institute, London, United Kingdom."}]}},{"fullName":"Abrahão FS","firstName":"Felipe S","lastName":"Abrahão","initials":"FS","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Oxford Immune Algorithmics, Oxford University Innovation, London Institute for Healthcare Engineering, London, United Kingdom."},{"affiliation":"The Arrival Institute, London, United Kingdom."}]}},{"fullName":"Witkowski O","firstName":"Olaf","lastName":"Witkowski","initials":"O","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2101-2428"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"The Arrival Institute, London, United Kingdom."},{"affiliation":"Cross Labs, Kyoto, Japan."},{"affiliation":"University of Tokyo, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Zenil H","firstName":"Hector","lastName":"Zenil","initials":"H","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0634-4384"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Oxford Immune Algorithmics, Oxford University Innovation, London Institute for Healthcare Engineering, London, United Kingdom."},{"affiliation":"Cross Labs, Kyoto, Japan."},{"affiliation":"University of Tokyo, Japan."},{"affiliation":"Algorithmic Dynamics Lab, Research Department of Biomedical Computing and Digital Twins, School of Biomedical Engineering and Imaging Sciences, King's Institute for Artificial Intelligence & King's College London, United Kingdom."},{"affiliation":"The Alan Turing Institute, British Library, London, United Kingdom."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2101-2428"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0634-4384"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0007-4791-0625"}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"4","volume":"5","journalIssueId":4175453,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Apr","monthOfPublication":4,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-04-01","journal":{"title":"PNAS nexus","medlineAbbreviation":"PNAS Nexus","isoabbreviation":"PNAS Nexus","nlmid":"9918367777906676","essn":"2752-6542","issn":"2752-6542"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"pgag076","abstractText":"Ensuring that AI systems, including artificial general intelligence and artificial superintelligence, behave in alignment with human values and interests presents significant challenges and is known as the AI alignment problem. As AI advances, concerns about control and existential risks become increasingly relevant. Here, we introduce the concept of agentic influenceability, behavioral neurodivergent diversity, opinion attack, associated opinion, and influenceability scores, and a mathematical proof of the inevitability of misalignment and the impossibility of full orchestrated controllability of agentic systems based on formal undecidability and irreducibility arguments. We explore whether embracing this inevitable misalignment can foster a dynamic ecosystem of adversarial and collaborative AI agents without central orchestration, which itself would constitute another agent, while still offering some degree of soft controllability. The investigation demonstrates that misalignment in foundation models can serve as a counterbalancing mechanism, enabling cooperation among agents most aligned with human interests to prevent divergent dominance by any single agent. Experiments with large language models show that open models exhibit greater behavioral diversity, whereas proprietary models, constrained by artificial guardrails, display more limited controllability. The findings advocate for neurodivergent influenceability as a contingent response to mathematically uncontrollable misalignment, leveraging agent divergence to improve AI safety.","affiliation":"Oxford Immune Algorithmics, Oxford University Innovation, London Institute for Healthcare Engineering, London, United Kingdom.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"grantId":"2021/14501-8","agency":"Sao Paulo Research Foundation","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"2023/05593-1","agency":"Sao Paulo Research Foundation","orderIn":0}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Orchestration","Ai Alignment","Multiagent Swarms","Agentic Ai","Red Teaming"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/pnasnexus/pgag076"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13077672"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13077672?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-04-16","dateOfCreation":"2026-04-16","firstIndexDate":"2026-04-17","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-04-20","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-04-14","firstPublicationDate":"2026-04-14"},{"id":"42028724","source":"MED","pmid":"42028724","pmcid":"PMC13108442","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13108442"]},"doi":"10.1093/ijnp/pyag010","title":"Responsible artificial intelligence integration framework for psychiatric guidelines.","authorString":"Mendlovic S, Frankova I, Vermetten E, Wasserman D, Schulze TG, Falkai P, Fountoulakis KN, Adorjan K, Uchida H, Fruchter E, Gobbi G, Zohar J.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Mendlovic S","firstName":"Shlomo","lastName":"Mendlovic","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Shalvata Mental Health Center, Hod Hasharon, Israel."},{"affiliation":"Tel Aviv University, School of Medicine, Tel Aviv, Israel."}]}},{"fullName":"Frankova I","firstName":"Iryna","lastName":"Frankova","initials":"I","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Clinical, Neuro- and Developmental Psychology, Amsterdam Public Health Institute and World Health Organization Collaborating Center for Research and Dissemination of Psychological Interventions, Vrije Universiteit Amsterdam, Amsterdam, The Netherlands."},{"affiliation":"Family Outpatient Unit, Centrum'45, ARQ National Psychotrauma Centre, Oegstgeest, The Netherlands."},{"affiliation":"Department of Medical Psychology, Psychosomatic Medicine and Psychotherapy, Educational and Scientific Institute of Mental Health, Bohomolets National Medical University, Kyiv, Ukraine."}]}},{"fullName":"Vermetten E","firstName":"Eric","lastName":"Vermetten","initials":"E","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry, Leiden University Medical Center, Leiden, The Netherlands."}]}},{"fullName":"Wasserman D","firstName":"Danuta","lastName":"Wasserman","initials":"D","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"The World Psychiatric Association (WPA), Thônex, Geneva, Switzerland."},{"affiliation":"National Centre for Suicide Research and Prevention of Mental Ill-Health, Karolinska Institutet, Stockholm, Sweden."}]}},{"fullName":"Schulze TG","firstName":"Thomas G","lastName":"Schulze","initials":"TG","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"The World Psychiatric Association (WPA), Thônex, Geneva, Switzerland."},{"affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry and Behavioral Sciences, Norton College of Medicine, SUNY Upstate Medical University, Syracuse, NY, United States."},{"affiliation":"Institute of Psychiatric Phenomics and Genomics (IPPG), University Hospital, LMU Munich, Munich, Bavaria, Germany."},{"affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry and Behavioral Sciences, Johns Hopkins University, Baltimore, MD, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Falkai P","firstName":"Peter","lastName":"Falkai","initials":"P","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry and Psychotherapy, Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität München, Munich, Germany."},{"affiliation":"Max-Planck Institute for Psychiatry, Munich, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Fountoulakis KN","firstName":"Konstantinos N","lastName":"Fountoulakis","initials":"KN","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5503-0811"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"3rd Department of Psychiatry, School of Medicine, Faculty of Health Sciences, Aristotle University of Thessaloniki, Thessaloniki, Greece."},{"affiliation":"Institute of Psychiatry, Thessaloniki, Greece."},{"affiliation":"Society for Neurosciences and Rehabilitation (E.N.A.), Thessaloniki, Greece."}]}},{"fullName":"Adorjan K","firstName":"Kristina","lastName":"Adorjan","initials":"K","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Institute of Psychiatric Phenomics and Genomics (IPPG), LMU University Hospital, LMU Munich, Munich, Germany."},{"affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry, University Hospital of Psychiatry and Psychotherapy, University of Bern, Bern, Switzerland."}]}},{"fullName":"Uchida H","firstName":"Hiroyuki","lastName":"Uchida","initials":"H","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0628-7036"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Neuropsychiatry, Keio University School of Medicine, Tokyo, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Fruchter E","firstName":"Eyal","lastName":"Fruchter","initials":"E","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Icar Collective and Rappoport Medical Faculty, Technion, Haifa, Israel."}]}},{"fullName":"Gobbi G","firstName":"Gabriella","lastName":"Gobbi","initials":"G","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4124-9825"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Neurobiological Psychiatry Unit, Department of Psychiatry, McGill University and Research Institute, McGill University Health Center, Montréal, Québec, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Zohar J","firstName":"Joseph","lastName":"Zohar","initials":"J","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6925-9104"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Sheba Medical Center, Tel Aviv University, Tel Aviv, Israel."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5503-0811"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0628-7036"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6925-9104"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4124-9825"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"4","volume":"29","journalIssueId":4174654,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Apr","monthOfPublication":4,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-04-01","journal":{"title":"The international journal of neuropsychopharmacology","medlineAbbreviation":"Int J Neuropsychopharmacol","isoabbreviation":"Int J Neuropsychopharmacol","nlmid":"9815893","essn":"1469-5111","issn":"1461-1457"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"pyag010","abstractText":"Artificial intelligence (AI) is reshaping medicine, promising advances in diagnosis, monitoring, and treatment, and psychiatry will be no exception. Yet the field remains fragmented: ethical guidelines, technical standards, and clinical workflows have evolved in parallel, creating uncertainty about how to integrate AI safely and meaningfully into psychiatric care. Existing frameworks often address isolated domains (explainability, data protection, or harm prevention [HP]) without providing a coherent structure that connects them to everyday clinical realities. This article introduces a global framework for the responsible integration of AI in psychiatry, built on 4 non-negotiable system capabilities: Explainable AI to ensure transparency and trust; Shared Decision-Making to protect patient autonomy; Electronic Health Record integration to secure continuity and accountability; and HP to embed multilayered safety controls. Together, these pillars define a responsibility-by-design approach that aligns technological development with psychiatry's ethical foundations. The framework offers clinicians, policymakers, and developers a roadmap for aligning innovation with human values and measurable improvements in clinical outcomes. By translating ethical commitments into auditable, non-negotiable system capabilities, it establishes a concrete foundation for regulatory oversight, guideline endorsement, and responsible AI deployment in psychiatry.","affiliation":"Shalvata Mental Health Center, Hod Hasharon, Israel.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Practice Guideline","review-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"Canada Research Chair in Therapeutics for Mental Health","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Mental Disorders","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"DI","qualifierName":"diagnosis","majorTopic_YN":"N"},{"abbreviation":"TH","qualifierName":"therapy","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Psychiatry","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods","majorTopic_YN":"N"},{"abbreviation":"ST","qualifierName":"standards","majorTopic_YN":"N"},{"abbreviation":"ES","qualifierName":"ethics","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Artificial Intelligence","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"ST","qualifierName":"standards","majorTopic_YN":"N"},{"abbreviation":"ES","qualifierName":"ethics","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Electronic Health Records","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"ST","qualifierName":"standards","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Decision Making, Shared"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Integration","Psychiatry","Guideline","Ethics","AI"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/ijnp/pyag010"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13108442"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13108442?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by-nc","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-07-15","dateOfCreation":"2026-04-24","firstIndexDate":"2026-04-24","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-18","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","firstPublicationDate":"2026-04-01"},{"id":"42137795","source":"MED","pmid":"42137795","pmcid":"PMC13168041","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13168041"]},"doi":"10.3389/fmicb.2026.1814993","title":"Know your scientist: KYC as biosecurity infrastructure.","authorString":"Feldman J, Feldman T, Anton AI.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Feldman J","firstName":"Jonathan","lastName":"Feldman","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"College of Computing, Georgia Institute of Technology, Atlanta, GA, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Feldman T","firstName":"Tal","lastName":"Feldman","initials":"T","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Yale Law School, New Haven, CT, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Anton AI","firstName":"Annie I","lastName":"Anton","initials":"AI","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"College of Computing, Georgia Institute of Technology, Atlanta, GA, United States."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"17","journalIssueId":4112147,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Frontiers in microbiology","medlineAbbreviation":"Front Microbiol","isoabbreviation":"Front Microbiol","nlmid":"101548977","essn":"1664-302X","issn":"1664-302X"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1814993","abstractText":"Biological AI tools for protein design and structure prediction are advancing rapidly, creating dual-use risks that existing safeguards cannot adequately address. Current model-level restrictions, including keyword filtering, output screening, and content-based access denials, are fundamentally ill-suited to biology, where reliable function prediction remains beyond reach and novel threats evade detection by design. Because the full spectrum of risks cannot be managed by any single actor, effective oversight requires shared responsibility between research institutions and model hosts. Hence, we propose a three-tier Know Your Customer (KYC) framework, inspired by anti-money laundering (AML) practices in the financial sector, that augments existing approaches, supplementing content inspection with complementary layers of user verification and monitoring. Tier I leverages research institutions as trust anchors to vouch for affiliated researchers and assume responsibility for vetting. Tier II applies output screening through sequence homology searches and functional annotation. Tier III monitors behavioral patterns to detect anomalies inconsistent with declared research purposes. This layered approach preserves access for legitimate researchers while raising the cost of misuse through institutional accountability and traceability. The framework can be implemented immediately using existing institutional infrastructure, requiring no new legislation or regulatory mandates.","affiliation":"College of Computing, Georgia Institute of Technology, Atlanta, GA, United States.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["brief-report","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Biosecurity","Kyc","Access Control","Ai Governance","Biological Design Tools","Dual-use Risk"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fmicb.2026.1814993"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13168041"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13168041?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":1,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-05-15","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-15","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-16","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-20","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-04-29","firstPublicationDate":"2026-04-29"},{"id":"42091163","source":"MED","pmid":"42091163","pmcid":"PMC13161640","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13161640"]},"doi":"10.1177/00469580261449981","title":"Six-Domain Moral Ecology Framework for Quality Leadership in Healthcare: A Conceptual Synthesis.","authorString":"Lotfi A.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Lotfi A","firstName":"Amir","lastName":"Lotfi","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8154-8497"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Cardiovascular Medicine, Baystate Medical Centerand Division of Cardiovascular Medicine, University of Massachusetts-Baystate, Springfield, MA, USA."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8154-8497"}]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"63","journalIssueId":4082475,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jan-Dec","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Inquiry : a journal of medical care organization, provision and financing","medlineAbbreviation":"Inquiry","isoabbreviation":"Inquiry","nlmid":"0171671","essn":"1945-7243","issn":"0046-9580"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1","abstractText":"IntroductionHealthcare leaders are expected to deliver safer care, better outcomes, and improved access while navigating workforce shortages, moral distress, and administrative burden. Improvement efforts oscillate between culture-first and systems-first approaches, each generating predictable failure modes - metric gaming, burnout, superficial compliance, and persistent safety variation. A practical, integrative framework for quality leadership that spans culture, systems, incentives, and governance is lacking.MethodsThis conceptual synthesis develops a 'moral ecology' model for quality leadership by analyzing six philosophical traditions - Aristotle, Avicenna, Taoist thought, Hobbes, Mandeville, and Voltaire. Traditions were selected using explicit inclusion criteria: each must foreground a distinct assumption about human motivation, generate a unique and recurring failure mode in healthcare quality, and correspond to a recognizable domain in quality improvement literature. The synthesis was conducted without geographic restriction, with healthcare leadership evidence drawn from publications through 2025. Reporting follows an SRQR-adapted framework for conceptual synthesis.ResultsThe six-domain moral ecology framework specifies: professionalism and character formation (Aristotle); truth-seeking and psychological safety (Avicenna); flow and simplification of work (Taoism); enforceable order in crisis (Hobbes); incentive alignment (Mandeville); and dignity with procedural justice (Voltaire). Each domain addresses a predictable failure mode - moralizing without redesign, purity culture, permissive tolerance, fear-based compliance, metric gaming, or scapegoating - and maps to specific leadership levers. The framework includes a five-step quick-start guide, six leadership commitments, diagnostic questions, and a balanced scorecard pairing quantitative indicators with qualitative signals. A worked acute myocardial infarction example and two additional cardiology mini-scenarios illustrate application across procedural and imaging contexts.ConclusionHigh-reliability care cannot be achieved by exhorting clinicians to be better people or by building ever more rules that assume they are not. The six-domain moral ecology framework reframes quality leadership as stewardship of an interconnected environment of character, learning, work design, authority, incentives, and fair governance. The framework provides a practical decision aid when initiatives stall or backfire - including under conditions of epistemic uncertainty and ambiguous causal attribution - by identifying the missing moral lever and the failure mode shaping current performance.","affiliation":"Department of Cardiovascular Medicine, Baystate Medical Centerand Division of Cardiovascular Medicine, University of Massachusetts-Baystate, Springfield, MA, USA.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Leadership"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Morals"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Organizational Culture"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Delivery of Health Care","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"OG","qualifierName":"organization & administration","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Quality of Health Care","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"OG","qualifierName":"organization & administration","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Quality Improvement"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Electrophysiology","Quality improvement","Leadership","Incentives","Procedural Justice","Safety Culture","Healthcare Access","Virtue Ethics","Psychological Safety","Moral Ecology","Incentive Alignment","Conceptual Synthesis"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1177/00469580261449981"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13161640"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13161640?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by-nc","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-07-16","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-06","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-13","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-20","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-05-06","firstPublicationDate":"2026-01-01"},{"id":"42352444","source":"MED","pmid":"42352444","pmcid":"PMC13297468","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13297468"]},"doi":"10.3390/cancers18121909","title":"Advances in Precision Diagnostics and Personalized Therapeutics for Prostate Cancer: An Integrated Precision Continuum from Risk-Adapted Detection to Biomarker-Directed Therapy and Dynamic Monitoring.","authorString":"Noro T, Utsumi T, Ikeda R, Sugimoto T, Ishitsuka N, Kadono Y, Suzuki Y, Iijima S, Sugizaki Y, Somoto T, Oka R, Endo T, Kamiya N, Suzuki H.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Noro T","firstName":"Takahide","lastName":"Noro","initials":"T","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0005-7869-2742"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Urology, Toho University Sakura Medical Center, Sakura 285-8741, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Utsumi T","firstName":"Takanobu","lastName":"Utsumi","initials":"T","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9423-7361"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Urology, Toho University Sakura Medical Center, Sakura 285-8741, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Ikeda R","firstName":"Rino","lastName":"Ikeda","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Urology, Toho University Sakura Medical Center, Sakura 285-8741, Japan."},{"affiliation":"Department of Urology, Toho University Graduate School of Medicine, Tokyo 143-8540, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Sugimoto T","firstName":"Tatsuharu","lastName":"Sugimoto","initials":"T","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Urology, Toho University Graduate School of Medicine, Tokyo 143-8540, Japan."},{"affiliation":"Department of Urology, Seirei Sakura Citizen Hospital, Sakura 285-8765, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Ishitsuka N","firstName":"Naoki","lastName":"Ishitsuka","initials":"N","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Urology, Toho University Sakura Medical Center, Sakura 285-8741, Japan."},{"affiliation":"Department of Urology, Toho University Graduate School of Medicine, Tokyo 143-8540, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Kadono Y","firstName":"Yodai","lastName":"Kadono","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Urology, Toho University Graduate School of Medicine, Tokyo 143-8540, Japan."},{"affiliation":"Department of Urology, Mihama Hospital, Chiba 261-0013, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Suzuki Y","firstName":"Yuta","lastName":"Suzuki","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Urology, Toho University Sakura Medical Center, Sakura 285-8741, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Iijima S","firstName":"Shota","lastName":"Iijima","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Urology, Toho University Sakura Medical Center, Sakura 285-8741, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Sugizaki Y","firstName":"Yuka","lastName":"Sugizaki","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Urology, Toho University Sakura Medical Center, Sakura 285-8741, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Somoto T","firstName":"Takatoshi","lastName":"Somoto","initials":"T","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Urology, Toho University Sakura Medical Center, Sakura 285-8741, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Oka R","firstName":"Ryo","lastName":"Oka","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Urology, Toho University Sakura Medical Center, Sakura 285-8741, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Endo T","firstName":"Takumi","lastName":"Endo","initials":"T","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Urology, Toho University Sakura Medical Center, Sakura 285-8741, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Kamiya N","firstName":"Naoto","lastName":"Kamiya","initials":"N","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4183-2490"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Urology, Toho University Sakura Medical Center, Sakura 285-8741, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Suzuki H","firstName":"Hiroyoshi","lastName":"Suzuki","initials":"H","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5838-114X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Urology, Toho University Sakura Medical Center, Sakura 285-8741, Japan."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5838-114X"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4183-2490"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9423-7361"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0005-7869-2742"}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"12","volume":"18","journalIssueId":4220345,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-06-01","journal":{"title":"Cancers","medlineAbbreviation":"Cancers (Basel)","isoabbreviation":"Cancers (Basel)","nlmid":"101526829","essn":"2072-6694","issn":"2072-6694"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1909","abstractText":"Precision medicine in prostate cancer (PCa) is increasingly best understood as a continuum linking risk-adapted detection, multimodal diagnosis and phenotyping, and implementation-ready decision pathways. Contemporary clinical guidelines emphasize structured diagnostic strategies, appropriate use of advanced imaging, and selective deployment of biomarkers when results can alter management. Upstream risk enrichment using polygenic risk scores and multivariable prediction models may improve the yield of clinically significant disease while mitigating harms related to overdiagnosis. At the point of suspicion, magnetic resonance imaging-first pathways and reflex biomarker testing provide practical tools to reduce unnecessary biopsy while maintaining safeguards for the detection of clinically important disease. Beyond diagnosis, prostate-specific membrane antigen positron emission tomography refines disease-state phenotyping in initial staging, biochemical recurrence, and limited-burden presentations, while standardized acquisition and reporting improve reproducibility and multidisciplinary communication. Germline and tumor-based molecular profiling should be operationalized as a longitudinal care process with clear consent, turnaround targets, and test-to-action rules that define what each result enables at specific decision nodes. Finally, longitudinal monitoring approaches, including liquid biopsy and artificial intelligence-enabled pathology, are evolving rapidly and require transparent reporting and rigorous risk-of-bias appraisal before broad clinical adoption. This narrative review synthesizes key evidence across the precision continuum and outlines a decision-node-based, test-to-action framework for maximizing clinical benefit, maintaining quality, and ensuring equitable access.","affiliation":"Department of Urology, Toho University Sakura Medical Center, Sakura 285-8741, Japan.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Magnetic Resonance Imaging","prostate cancer","Implementation Science","Precision Medicine","Tumor Profiling","Liquid Biopsy","Psma Pet","Germline Testing","Risk-Adapted Detection"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/cancers18121909"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13297468"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13297468?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"N","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-26","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-26","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-26","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-27","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-11","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-11"},{"id":"PMC13296990","source":"PMC","pmcid":"PMC13296990","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13296990"]},"title":"Beyond accuracy: how generative artificial intelligence reshapes the foundations of public health discourse","journalInfo":{"issue":"4","volume":"36","journalIssueId":4214586,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-06-01","journal":{"title":"European journal of public health","medlineAbbreviation":"Eur J Public Health","isoabbreviation":"Eur J Public Health","nlmid":"9204966","essn":"1464-360X","issn":"1101-1262"}},"pubYear":"2026","language":"eng","pubModel":"Undetermined","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Comment","discussion"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"html","site":"PubMedCentral","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC13296990/?tool=EBI"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"PubMedCentral","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC13296990/pdf/?tool=EBI"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13296990"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13296990?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"N","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by-nc","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-27","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-27","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-27","dateOfRevision":"2026-06-27","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-25","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-25"},{"id":"42214035","source":"MED","pmid":"42214035","pmcid":"PMC13221120","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13221120"]},"doi":"10.2196/97972","title":"Real-World Data Needs Real-World Doctors: When Automation Advances Faster Than Clinical Workflow.","authorString":"Price A, Von Raesfeld C.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Price A","firstName":"Amy","lastName":"Price","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6937-2628"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Community and Family Medicine (CFMED), Dartmouth-Hitchcock Clinics, Lebanon, NH, United States."},{"affiliation":"Journal of Participatory Medicine, JMIR Publications, Toronto, ON, Canada."},{"affiliation":"Center for Global Health, Colorado School of Public Health, University of Colorado Anschutz Medical Campus, 13001 E 17th Pl # B119, Aurora, CO, 80045, United States, 1 5618437372."}]}},{"fullName":"Von Raesfeld C","firstName":"Christine","lastName":"Von Raesfeld","initials":"C","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0000-6767-3160"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"The Light Collective, Eugene, OR, United States."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6937-2628"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0000-6767-3160"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"18","journalIssueId":4187513,"dateOfPublication":"2026 May","monthOfPublication":5,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-05-01","journal":{"title":"Journal of participatory medicine","medlineAbbreviation":"J Particip Med","isoabbreviation":"J Particip Med","nlmid":"101539422","essn":"2152-7202","issn":"2152-7202"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e97972","abstractText":"<h4>Unlabelled</h4>Health care is entering an era of unprecedented detection. Artificial intelligence (AI)-driven monitors and real-world data streams now identify clinical risks in minutes, promising a future of proactive, earlier intervention. While AI automation is often marketed as a tool to reduce administrative burden and allow health care providers to focus more on direct patient care, this unrealized potential currently stands in contrast to our reality. Building high-speed data \"freeways\" without \"off-ramps\" such as clinical staffing, workflow synergy, and the patient education required for meaningful response is like building a superhighway without well-engineered off-ramps to provide a safe way to get home, and this creates a dangerous paradox. Earlier detection without earlier care does not improve outcomes; it simply redistributes anxiety and extends the patient's period of uncertainty. We argue that the \"public as a sensor\" is already signaling a systemic infrastructure gap. True safety in clinical AI isn't found in more algorithmic guardrails, but in participatory co-design that ensures every digital alert has a viable human pathway to care and resolution. We must stop building high-speed roads that lead to a cliff edge of clinical unavailability and consider that while the technology is a feat of engineering, it's our human architecture that makes it medicine.","affiliation":"Department of Community and Family Medicine (CFMED), Dartmouth-Hitchcock Clinics, Lebanon, NH, United States.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Editorial"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Patient-centered Care","Clinical Decision Support Systems","Diagnostic Accuracy","Care Coordination","Implementation Science","Alert Fatigue","Real-world Data","Participatory Medicine","Physician Burnout","Remote Patient Monitoring","Artificial Intelligence In Medicine","Workforce Capacity","Patient-led Research","Algorithmic Governance","Clinical Ai","Digital Health Infrastructure","Clinical Workflow Integration","Coproduction Of Health Care","Health Equity In Ai"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2196/97972"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13221120"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13221120?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-05-29","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-29","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-31","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-02","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-05-29","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-29"},{"id":"42317854","source":"MED","pmid":"42317854","pmcid":"PMC13274300","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13274300"]},"title":"Logit Fingerprinting: A Novel, Accuracy-Independent Method for Validating Large Language Model Stability in High-Stakes Clinical Applications.","authorString":"Logan WV, Bumgardner VKC.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Logan WV","firstName":"W Vaiden","lastName":"Logan","initials":"WV","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Center For Applied Artificial Intelligence, University of Kentucky, Lexington, KY."}]}},{"fullName":"Bumgardner VKC","firstName":"V K Cody","lastName":"Bumgardner","initials":"VKC","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Center For Applied Artificial Intelligence, University of Kentucky, Lexington, KY."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"2026","journalIssueId":4218929,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"AMIA Joint Summits on Translational Science proceedings. AMIA Joint Summits on Translational Science","medlineAbbreviation":"AMIA Jt Summits Transl Sci Proc","isoabbreviation":"AMIA Jt Summits Transl Sci Proc","nlmid":"101539486","essn":"2153-4063","issn":"2153-4063"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"287-292","abstractText":"The integration of Large Language Models (LLMs) into clinical settings requires quality assurance mechanisms capable of detecting the hidden effects of model compression and architectural instability. Conventional accuracy metrics often fail to capture the behavioral volatility introduced by quantization, distillation, and sparse architectures. We propose the \"Single-Token Forced-Choice Logit Probe,\" a method that generates a \"behavioral fingerprint\" of a model by analyzing its decision-making stability on a domain-specific (MedQA) benchmark. Validated on 11 local model families, our approach achieved 100% accuracy in distinguishing full-precision models from quantized variants. Furthermore, a longitudinal audit of commercial APIs revealed a distinct \"Stability Gap\": distilled \"Nano\" models exhibited nearly double the decision instability (2.82% vs. 1.58% Flip Rate) of their standard counterparts. Forensic classification identified the underlying compression techniques (Q8 vs. FP8), while analysis suggests the inherent non-determinism stems from Sparse Mixture-of-Experts (SMoE) routing. We conclude that Flip Rate is a critical safety metric and that distilled and quantized models require rigorous stability auditing before clinical deployment.","affiliation":"Center For Applied Artificial Intelligence, University of Kentucky, Lexington, KY.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13274300"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13274300?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-19","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-19","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-20","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-24","dateOfRevision":"2026-06-19","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-01","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-01"},{"id":"42167742","source":"MED","pmid":"42167742","pmcid":"PMC13193742","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13193742"]},"doi":"10.4258/hir.2026.32.2.196","title":"Qualitative Framework for Evaluating Clinical Data Science Systems: Beyond Technical Validation.","authorString":"Heslin SM.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Heslin SM","firstName":"Samita M","lastName":"Heslin","initials":"SM","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Emergency Medicine, Renaissance School of Medicine, Stony Brook University, NY, USA. samita.heslin@stonybrookmedicine.edu."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8054-3387"}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"2","volume":"32","journalIssueId":4199328,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Apr","monthOfPublication":4,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-04-01","journal":{"title":"Healthcare informatics research","medlineAbbreviation":"Healthc Inform Res","isoabbreviation":"Healthc Inform Res","nlmid":"101534553","essn":"2093-369X","issn":"2093-3681"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"196-199","affiliation":"Department of Emergency Medicine, Renaissance School of Medicine, Stony Brook University, NY, USA. samita.heslin@stonybrookmedicine.edu.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.4258/hir.2026.32.2.196"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13193742"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13193742?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by-nc","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-05-21","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-21","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-22","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-22","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-04-30","firstPublicationDate":"2026-04-30"},{"id":"PPR1255870","source":"PPR","doi":"10.22541/au.177516434.43699420/v1","title":"From Loop To Partnership: A Model For a Changing Paradigm Of Human-Ai Partnership","authorString":"Patil S, Sharma S, Mahdavi M.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Patil S","firstName":"Sagar","lastName":"Patil","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0003-7494-4063"}},{"fullName":"Sharma S","firstName":"Suneel","lastName":"Sharma","initials":"S"},{"fullName":"Mahdavi M","firstName":"Mehregan","lastName":"Mahdavi","initials":"M"}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0003-7494-4063"}]},"pubYear":"2026","abstractText":"Human-in-the-loop (HIL) once was described to clarify the role of human in AI systems, but has been extended to cover interactions that represent divergent forms, compromising our designers intentions and ethical guardrails. This paper seeks to resolve this conceptual ambiguity by proposing a tripartite framework-HIL (AI-led, automation-first), AI-in-the-loop (AI2L; human-led, augmentation-first), and Hybrid Intelligence (HI; co-creative partnership)and by suggesting a paradigm-domain fit that associates each paradigm with objectives pertaining to efficiency, accountability, or creativity. We synthesize evidence of the performance paradox-human-AI teams often lag behind AI alone-and the agencyperformance trade-off that emerges in high-stakes settings. The paper contends that ethical oversight must transform from a \"thin human in the loop\" defence to participatory governance, which permeates multi-stakeholder accountability, embedding accountability to take effect across the lifecycle. We tackle two significant problems: AI-generated content detectors that are unreliable and cultural homogenization risks as generative models grow. A structured research agenda emphasizes team formation, maintenance, evaluation in the wild and governance. This framework is designed to help both researchers and practitioners build human-AI systems which are compatible with the intended goals of the domain, and ensure that bias, overabundance, and dispersed responsibility are minimized.","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Preprint"]},"bookOrReportDetails":{"publisher":"Authorea Preprints","yearOfPublication":2026},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.22541/au.177516434.43699420/v1"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","versionList":{"version":[{"id":"PPR1255870","source":"PPR","firstPublishDate":"2026-04-02","versionNumber":1,"pubTypeList":{"pubType":["preprint"]},"hasEvaluations":"N"}]},"versionNumber":1,"hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-20","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-20","firstPublicationDate":"2026-04-02"},{"id":"41834945","source":"MED","pmid":"41834945","pmcid":"PMC12983458","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12983458"]},"doi":"10.1093/pnasnexus/pgag030","title":"Toward a science of human-AI teaming for decision making: A complementarity framework.","authorString":"Gonzalez C, Donahue K, Goldstein DG, Heidari H, Jalali MS, Schelble B, Singh A, Woolley AW.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Gonzalez C","firstName":"Cleotilde","lastName":"Gonzalez","initials":"C","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6244-2918"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Social and Decision Sciences Department, Carnegie Mellon University, 5000 Forbes Avenue, Pittsburgh, PA 15213, USA."},{"affiliation":"Software and Societal Systems Department, Carnegie Mellon University, 5000 Forbes Avenue, Pittsburgh, PA 15213, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Donahue K","firstName":"Kate","lastName":"Donahue","initials":"K","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6482-3952"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Computer Science Department, Massachusetts Institute of Technology, University of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign, 601 E. John Street, Champaign, IL 61820, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Goldstein DG","firstName":"Daniel G","lastName":"Goldstein","initials":"DG","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0970-5598"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Microsoft Research, 300 Lafayette Street, New York, NY 10012, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Heidari H","firstName":"Hoda","lastName":"Heidari","initials":"H","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-3710-4076"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Machine Learning Department, Carnegie Mellon University, 5000 Forbes Avenue, Pittsburgh, PA 15213, USA."},{"affiliation":"Software and Societal Systems Department, Carnegie Mellon University, 5000 Forbes Avenue, Pittsburgh, PA 15213, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Jalali MS","firstName":"Mohammad S","lastName":"Jalali","initials":"MS","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6769-2732"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Harvard Medical School, Harvard University, 25 Shattuck Street, Boston, MA 02115, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Schelble B","firstName":"Beau","lastName":"Schelble","initials":"B","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-3704-697X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Industrial and Systems Engineering, University of Tennessee at Knoxville, 527 Andy Holt Tower, Knoxville, TN 37996, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Singh A","firstName":"Aarti","lastName":"Singh","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Machine Learning Department, Carnegie Mellon University, 5000 Forbes Avenue, Pittsburgh, PA 15213, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Woolley AW","firstName":"Anita Williams","lastName":"Woolley","initials":"AW","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0620-4744"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Tepper School of Business, Carnegie Mellon University, 5000 Forbes Avenue, Pittsburgh, PA 15213, USA."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6482-3952"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6769-2732"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0970-5598"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6244-2918"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0620-4744"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-3704-697X"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-3710-4076"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"3","volume":"5","journalIssueId":4136598,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Mar","monthOfPublication":3,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-03-01","journal":{"title":"PNAS nexus","medlineAbbreviation":"PNAS Nexus","isoabbreviation":"PNAS Nexus","nlmid":"9918367777906676","essn":"2752-6542","issn":"2752-6542"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"pgag030","abstractText":"As artificial intelligence (AI) becomes embedded in critical decisions involving health, safety, finance, and governance, the key challenge is no longer whether humans and AI will collaborate, but rather how to structure this collaboration to achieve true complementarity. Human-AI complementarity refers to the conditions under which human-AI teams outperform either humans alone or AI systems alone. This paper advances the science of human-AI teaming for decision making by integrating insights from cognitive science, AI, human factors, organizational behavior, and ethics. We propose a framework grounded in collective intelligence and anchored in the foundational cognitive processes-reasoning, memory, and attention-to understand and engineer effective human-AI teams. We examine the sociotechnical factors that shape team effectiveness, including team composition, trust calibration, shared mental models, training, and task structure. We then outline design principles for achieving complementarity: defining goals and constraints, partitioning roles, orchestrating attention and interrogation, building knowledge infrastructures, and establishing continuous training and evaluation. We conclude with theoretical, practical, and policy implications, emphasizing alignment with human values, accountability, and equity. Together, these insights offer a roadmap for building human-AI teams that are not only high-performing and adaptive, but also transparent, trustworthy, and fundamentally human-centered.","affiliation":"Social and Decision Sciences Department, Carnegie Mellon University, 5000 Forbes Avenue, Pittsburgh, PA 15213, USA.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"grantId":"2229881","agency":"AI Institute for Societal Decision Making","orderIn":0},{"agency":"National Science Foundation","orderIn":0},{"agency":"AI Research Institutes Program","orderIn":0}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Alignment","Complementarity","Human–ai Teaming"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/pnasnexus/pgag030"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12983458"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12983458?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":4,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-03-16","dateOfCreation":"2026-03-16","firstIndexDate":"2026-03-16","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-03-14","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-02-19","firstPublicationDate":"2026-02-19"},{"id":"42028135","source":"MED","pmid":"42028135","pmcid":"PMC13100853","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13100853"]},"doi":"10.1016/j.jointm.2025.10.003","title":"Artificial intelligence in ARDS: From automated support to personalized ventilation.","authorString":"Muñoz J, Fernández-Araujo NJ, Ruíz-Cacho R, Muñoz-Visedo J.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Muñoz J","firstName":"Javier","lastName":"Muñoz","initials":"J","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9794-4507"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Intensive Care Medicine, Hospital General Universitario Gregorio Marañón, Madrid, Spain."},{"affiliation":"Instituto de Investigación Sanitaria Gregorio Marañón (IiSGM), Madrid, Spain."}]}},{"fullName":"Fernández-Araujo NJ","firstName":"Nerio José","lastName":"Fernández-Araujo","initials":"NJ","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Intensive Care Medicine, Hospital General Universitario Gregorio Marañón, Madrid, Spain."},{"affiliation":"Instituto de Investigación Sanitaria Gregorio Marañón (IiSGM), Madrid, Spain."}]}},{"fullName":"Ruíz-Cacho R","firstName":"Rocío","lastName":"Ruíz-Cacho","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Intensive Care Medicine, Hospital General Universitario Gregorio Marañón, Madrid, Spain."}]}},{"fullName":"Muñoz-Visedo J","firstName":"Javier","lastName":"Muñoz-Visedo","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Escuela Superior de Ciencias Experimentales y Tecnología, Universidad Rey Juan Carlos, Madrid, Spain."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7976-0544"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9794-4507"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"2","volume":"6","journalIssueId":4185448,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Apr","monthOfPublication":4,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-04-01","journal":{"title":"Journal of intensive medicine","medlineAbbreviation":"J Intensive Med","isoabbreviation":"J Intensive Med","nlmid":"9918539389006676","essn":"2667-100X","issn":"2097-0250"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"128-131","affiliation":"Department of Intensive Care Medicine, Hospital General Universitario Gregorio Marañón, Madrid, Spain.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Editorial"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jointm.2025.10.003"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13100853"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13100853?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-04-24","dateOfCreation":"2026-04-24","firstIndexDate":"2026-04-24","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-04-23","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2025-12-02","firstPublicationDate":"2025-12-02"},{"id":"41873312","source":"MED","pmid":"41873312","pmcid":"PMC13006122","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13006122"]},"doi":"10.7759/cureus.103995","title":"Ethical Governance of Artificial Intelligence in Cardiovascular Disease Management: A Health Policy Perspective.","authorString":"Ogbuefi C, Ezika OL, Egbunike JO, Ogbuefi KE.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Ogbuefi C","firstName":"Cfc","lastName":"Ogbuefi","initials":"C","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Family Medicine, Federal University Teaching Hospital, Owerri, NGA."}]}},{"fullName":"Ezika OL","firstName":"O L","lastName":"Ezika","initials":"OL","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Family Medicine, Federal University Teaching Hospital, Owerri, NGA."}]}},{"fullName":"Egbunike JO","firstName":"J O","lastName":"Egbunike","initials":"JO","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Family Medicine, Federal Medical Center Jabi, Abuja, NGA."}]}},{"fullName":"Ogbuefi KE","firstName":"K E","lastName":"Ogbuefi","initials":"KE","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Family Medicine, Federal Medical Center, Umuahia, NGA."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"2","volume":"18","journalIssueId":4110283,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Feb","monthOfPublication":2,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-02-01","journal":{"title":"Cureus","medlineAbbreviation":"Cureus","isoabbreviation":"Cureus","nlmid":"101596737","essn":"2168-8184","issn":"2168-8184"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e103995","abstractText":"Artificial intelligence (AI) is increasingly integrated into cardiovascular disease (CVD) prevention, diagnosis, and risk stratification, offering opportunities to improve early detection and population health outcomes. However, rapid adoption of AI technologies has outpaced the development of ethical, regulatory, and equity-centered governance frameworks, raising concerns about algorithmic bias, transparency, interoperability, and public trust. This editorial examines challenges in the governance of AI for cardiovascular care in the United States and situates them within global digital health policy discussions. While current federal oversight primarily focuses on clinical safety, this editorial advances a policy-oriented framework, formulated by the authors as the Artificial Intelligence Driven Cardiovascular Health Equity and Data Integration Act (AI-CVD Equity Act). This proposed act builds upon the existing Food and Drug Administration (FDA) and Department of Health and Human Services (HHS) initiatives by introducing mandatory, standardized demographic bias audits and regionally coordinated community oversight with critical layers of governance currently absent from federal frameworks to support equitable, transparent, and accountable AI deployment. Strengthening governance, workforce capacity, and community engagement is essential to ensure that AI-driven innovation reduces, rather than exacerbates, cardiovascular health disparities.","affiliation":"Department of Family Medicine, Federal University Teaching Hospital, Owerri, NGA.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Editorial"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Cardiovascular disease","Health Policy","Health Equity","Health Ethics"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.7759/cureus.103995"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13006122"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13006122?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":2,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-03-24","dateOfCreation":"2026-03-24","firstIndexDate":"2026-03-24","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-03-23","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-02-20","firstPublicationDate":"2026-02-20"},{"id":"41874150","source":"MED","pmid":"41874150","pmcid":"PMC13010680","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13010680"]},"doi":"10.3390/mps9020033","title":"A Review of Multi-Agent AI Systems for Biological and Clinical Data Analysis.","authorString":"Spieser J, Balapour A, Meller J, Patra KC, Shamsaei B.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Spieser J","firstName":"Jackson","lastName":"Spieser","initials":"J","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0009-5789-2605"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"College of Medicine Cincinnati, University of Cincinnati, Cincinnati, OH 45267, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Balapour A","firstName":"Ali","lastName":"Balapour","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Computing and Analytics, Northern Kentucky University, Highland Heights, KY 41099, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Meller J","firstName":"Jarek","lastName":"Meller","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biostatistics, Health Informatics and Data Sciences, College of Medicine, University of Cincinnati, Cincinnati, OH 45267, USA."},{"affiliation":"Division of Biostatistics and Bioinformatics, Department of Environmental and Public Health Sciences, College of Medicine, University of Cincinnati, Cincinnati, OH 45267, USA."},{"affiliation":"Division of Biomedical Informatics, Cincinnati Children's Hospital Medical Center, Cincinnati, OH 45229, USA."},{"affiliation":"Institute of Engineering and Technology, Faculty of Physics, Astronomy and Informatics, Nicolaus Copernicus University, 87-100 Torun, Poland."},{"affiliation":"Department of Computer Science, College of Engineering and Applied Sciences, University of Cincinnati, Cincinnati, OH 45219, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Patra KC","firstName":"Krushna C","lastName":"Patra","initials":"KC","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Cancer Biology, College of Medicine, University of Cincinnati, Cincinnati, OH 45267, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Shamsaei B","firstName":"Behrouz","lastName":"Shamsaei","initials":"B","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0672-5382"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biostatistics, Health Informatics and Data Sciences, College of Medicine, University of Cincinnati, Cincinnati, OH 45267, USA."},{"affiliation":"Division of Biostatistics and Bioinformatics, Department of Environmental and Public Health Sciences, College of Medicine, University of Cincinnati, Cincinnati, OH 45267, USA."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0672-5382"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0009-5789-2605"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"2","volume":"9","journalIssueId":4159095,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Feb","monthOfPublication":2,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-02-01","journal":{"title":"Methods and protocols","medlineAbbreviation":"Methods Protoc","isoabbreviation":"Methods Protoc","nlmid":"101720073","essn":"2409-9279","issn":"2409-9279"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"33","abstractText":"This review evaluates the emerging paradigm of multi-agent systems (MASs) for biomedical and clinical data analysis, focusing on their ability to overcome the reasoning and reliability limitations of standalone large language models (LLMs). We synthesize findings from recent architectural frameworks, specifically LangGraph, CrewAI, and the Model Context Protocol (MCP), to examine how specialized agent teams divide labor, utilize precision tools, and cross-verify outputs. We find that MAS architectures yield significant performance gains in various domains: recent implementations improved oncology decision-making accuracy from 30.3% to 87.2% and reached a peak of 93.2% accuracy on USMLE-style benchmarks through simulated clinical evolution. In clinical trial matching, multi-agent frameworks achieved 87.3% accuracy and enhanced clinician screening efficiency by 42.6% (<i>p</i> < 0.001). However, we also highlight critical operational challenges, including an unreliability tax of 15-50× higher token consumption compared to standalone models and the risk of cascading errors where initial hallucinations are amplified across the agent collective. We conclude that while MAS enables a shift toward collaborative intelligence in biomedicine, its clinical and research adoption requires the development of deterministic orchestration and rigorous cost-utility frameworks to ensure safety and expert-centered oversight.","affiliation":"College of Medicine Cincinnati, University of Cincinnati, Cincinnati, OH 45267, USA.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"grantId":"5R37CA272854-04","agency":"NIH Common Fund","orderIn":0}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Clinical Decision Support","Multi-agent Systems","Autonomous Agents","Collaborative Intelligence","Ai Safety","Large Language Models (Llms)","Biomedical Ai","Orchestration Frameworks"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/mps9020033"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13010680"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13010680?pdf=render"}]},"commentCorrectionList":{"commentCorrection":[{"id":"PPR1138768","source":"PPR","type":"Preprint in","note":"CrossRef Pre-print loader","orderIn":10002}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-03-24","dateOfCreation":"2026-03-24","firstIndexDate":"2026-03-24","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-03-25","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-02-28","firstPublicationDate":"2026-02-28"},{"id":"40269244","source":"MED","pmid":"40269244","pmcid":"PMC12019307","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12019307"]},"doi":"10.1038/s41746-025-01618-x","title":"Global Initiative on AI for Health (GI-AI4H): strategic priorities advancing governance across the United Nations.","authorString":"Muralidharan V, Ng MY, AlSalamah S, Pujari S, Kalra K, Singh R, Schalet D, Olantuji T, Malpani R, Matin RN, Omiye JA, Zhao Y, Sands A, Reis A, Diaz Mendoza JE, Hernandez-Boussard T, Daneshjou R, Labrique AB.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Muralidharan V","firstName":"Vijaytha","lastName":"Muralidharan","initials":"V","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0226-8468"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Dermatology, Stanford University, Stanford, CA, USA. vmurali5@stanford.edu."},{"affiliation":"Department of Dermatology, Aneurin Bevan NHS Health Trust, Newport, UK. vmurali5@stanford.edu."}]}},{"fullName":"Ng MY","firstName":"Madelena Y","lastName":"Ng","initials":"MY","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-3824-9349"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medicine (Biomedical Informatics), Stanford University, Stanford, CA, USA. madelena@stanford.edu."}]}},{"fullName":"AlSalamah S","firstName":"Shada","lastName":"AlSalamah","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Information Systems Department, College of Computer and Information Sciences, King Saud University, Riyadh, Saudi Arabia."},{"affiliation":"Digital Health and Innovation Department, Science Division, World Health Organization, Geneva, Switzerland."}]}},{"fullName":"Pujari S","firstName":"Sameer","lastName":"Pujari","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Digital Health and Innovation Department, Science Division, World Health Organization, Geneva, Switzerland."}]}},{"fullName":"Kalra K","firstName":"Kanika","lastName":"Kalra","initials":"K","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Digital Health and Innovation Department, Science Division, World Health Organization, Geneva, Switzerland."}]}},{"fullName":"Singh R","firstName":"Rajeshwari","lastName":"Singh","initials":"R","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5980-0203"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Digital Health and Innovation Department, Science Division, World Health Organization, Geneva, Switzerland."}]}},{"fullName":"Schalet D","firstName":"Denise","lastName":"Schalet","initials":"D","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Digital Health and Innovation Department, Science Division, World Health Organization, Geneva, Switzerland."}]}},{"fullName":"Olantuji T","firstName":"Tobi","lastName":"Olantuji","initials":"T","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0009-7214-2132"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Intron Health, San Francisco, CA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Malpani R","firstName":"Rohit","lastName":"Malpani","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Global Antibiotic Research and Development Partnership, Geneva, Switzerland."}]}},{"fullName":"Matin RN","firstName":"Rubeta N","lastName":"Matin","initials":"RN","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9695-5412"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Dermatology, Oxford University Hospitals NHS Foundation Trust, Oxford, UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Omiye JA","firstName":"Jesutofunmi A","lastName":"Omiye","initials":"JA","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Dermatology, Stanford University, Stanford, CA, USA."},{"affiliation":"Department of Biomedical Data Science, Stanford University, Stanford, CA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Zhao Y","firstName":"Yu","lastName":"Zhao","initials":"Y","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9655-4379"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Digital Health and Innovation Department, Science Division, World Health Organization, Geneva, Switzerland."}]}},{"fullName":"Sands A","firstName":"Anita","lastName":"Sands","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8424-754X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Regulation and Prequalification Department, World Health Organization, Geneva, Switzerland."}]}},{"fullName":"Reis A","firstName":"Andreas","lastName":"Reis","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Global Health Ethics Team, World Health Organization, Geneva, Switzerland."}]}},{"fullName":"Diaz Mendoza JE","firstName":"Jose Eduardo","lastName":"Diaz Mendoza","initials":"JE","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Digital Health and Innovation Department, Science Division, World Health Organization, Geneva, Switzerland."}]}},{"fullName":"Hernandez-Boussard T","firstName":"Tina","lastName":"Hernandez-Boussard","initials":"T","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6553-3455"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medicine (Biomedical Informatics), Stanford University, Stanford, CA, USA."},{"affiliation":"Department of Biomedical Data Science, Stanford University, Stanford, CA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Daneshjou R","firstName":"Roxana","lastName":"Daneshjou","initials":"R","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7988-9356"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Dermatology, Stanford University, Stanford, CA, USA."},{"affiliation":"Department of Medicine (Biomedical Informatics), Stanford University, Stanford, CA, USA."},{"affiliation":"Department of Biomedical Data Science, Stanford University, Stanford, CA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Labrique AB","firstName":"Alain B","lastName":"Labrique","initials":"AB","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Digital Health and Innovation Department, Science Division, World Health Organization, Geneva, Switzerland."},{"affiliation":"Department of International Health, Johns Hopkins Bloomberg School of Public Health, Baltimore, MD, USA."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5980-0203"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6553-3455"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7988-9356"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8424-754X"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9655-4379"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9695-5412"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0226-8468"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-3824-9349"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0009-7214-2132"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"8","journalIssueId":3925403,"dateOfPublication":"2025 Apr","monthOfPublication":4,"yearOfPublication":2025,"printPublicationDate":"2025-04-01","journal":{"title":"NPJ digital medicine","medlineAbbreviation":"NPJ Digit Med","isoabbreviation":"NPJ Digit Med","nlmid":"101731738","essn":"2398-6352","issn":"2398-6352"}},"pubYear":"2025","pageInfo":"219","abstractText":"The Global Initiative on Artificial Intelligence for Health (GI-AI4H), established by the World Health Organization, serves to harmonize governance standards for artificial intelligence (AI). The GI-AI4H spearheads novel on-the-ground efforts, especially in low- and middle-income countries, to advance ethical, regulatory, implementation, and operational dimensions of global governance for health AI. The GI-AI4H's efforts across the United Nations drives safe, ethical, equitable, and sustainable health AI use for the global community.","affiliation":"Department of Dermatology, Stanford University, Stanford, CA, USA. vmurali5@stanford.edu.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"grantId":"T15LM007033","agency":"U.S. Department of Health &amp; Human Services | NIH | U.S. National Library of Medicine","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"T15LM007033","agency":"U.S. Department of Health & Human Services | NIH | U.S. National Library of Medicine (NLM)","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"001","agency":"World Health Organization","acronym":"WHO_","orderIn":0}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41746-025-01618-x"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12019307"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12019307?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":13,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by-nc-nd","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2025-04-24","firstIndexDate":"2025-04-24","fullTextReceivedDate":"2025-11-27","dateOfRevision":"2025-06-05","electronicPublicationDate":"2025-04-23","firstPublicationDate":"2025-04-23"},{"id":"42212181","source":"MED","pmid":"42212181","pmcid":"PMC13212166","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13212166"]},"doi":"10.2147/jmdh.s581251","title":"Cybersecurity and Privacy Risks of Generative AI Mental-Health Chatbots: A Systematic Review and Regulatory Framework.","authorString":"Sawesi S, Sabbineni H, Shagamreddy RR, Rashrash B.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Sawesi S","firstName":"Suhila","lastName":"Sawesi","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3510-8543"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Information Sciences and Technologies, College of Computing, Grand Valley State University, Grand Rapids, MI, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Sabbineni H","firstName":"Hemalatha","lastName":"Sabbineni","initials":"H","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0006-9517-7889"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Information Sciences and Technologies, College of Computing, Grand Valley State University, Grand Rapids, MI, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Shagamreddy RR","firstName":"Rajashekhar Reddy","lastName":"Shagamreddy","initials":"RR","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Information Sciences and Technologies, College of Computing, Grand Valley State University, Grand Rapids, MI, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Rashrash B","firstName":"Budur","lastName":"Rashrash","initials":"B","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medicine, West Virginia School of Osteopathic Medicine, Lewisburg, WV, USA."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3510-8543"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0006-9517-7889"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"19","journalIssueId":4091825,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Journal of multidisciplinary healthcare","medlineAbbreviation":"J Multidiscip Healthc","isoabbreviation":"J Multidiscip Healthc","nlmid":"101512691","essn":"1178-2390","issn":"1178-2390"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"581251","abstractText":"<h4>Purpose</h4>Large language models (LLMs) and other generative artificial intelligence systems are increasingly used in mental health care for psychoeducation, emotional support, screening, and crisis-related interactions. To our knowledge, this is the first structured synthesis explicitly mapping LLM-specific cybersecurity and privacy risks to Software as a Medical Device (SaMD) regulatory frameworks. We aimed to characterize deployment patterns, identify multi-layered risks, and evaluate alignment of reported safeguards with established healthcare governance standards.<h4>Methods</h4>A PRISMA-guided systematic review was conducted using PubMed, APA PsycNet, and Google Scholar. After screening eligible records against predefined inclusion criteria, 33 studies were included. Two reviewers independently extracted data on application domains, deployment settings, risk categories, attack surfaces, data sensitivity, and reported or recommended controls.<h4>Results</h4>Generative AI chatbots were most frequently used for therapy or emotional support (13/33, 39.4%), followed by safety evaluation or benchmarking (9/33, 27.3%) and psychoeducation or advice (6/33, 18.2%). Suicide prevention or crisis detection was the most common domain (10/33, 30.3%). Most systems relied on general-purpose LLMs (21/33, 63.6%) and were deployed via consumer-facing platforms (16/33, 48.5%). Key risks included harmful or unsafe outputs, failures in crisis response, exposure of sensitive personal information, and limited transparency. Critically, 78.8% of studies (26/33) were rated high risk for cybersecurity evaluation rigor, indicating that formal adversarial testing and structured threat modeling remain rare.<h4>Conclusion</h4>Current governance frameworks have not fully adapted to generative conversational AI in mental health contexts. Because the therapeutic interface functions as a primary attack surface, single-layer security evaluation (assessing only software validation or content safety in isolation) is inadequate. More comprehensive approaches are needed, including stronger cybersecurity controls, privacy-preserving data practices, and explicit alignment with FDA SaMD guidance, HIPAA, ISO 14971, and the NIST AI Risk Management Framework.","affiliation":"Department of Information Sciences and Technologies, College of Computing, Grand Valley State University, Grand Rapids, MI, USA.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["risk management","Clinical Safety","Data Protection","Regulatory Frameworks","Digital Therapeutics","Conversational Systems"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2147/JMDH.S581251"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13212166"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13212166?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":1,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by-nc","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-05-29","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-29","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-31","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-02","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-05-22","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-22"},{"id":"41970542","source":"MED","pmid":"41970542","pmcid":"PMC13068647","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13068647"]},"doi":"10.1016/j.lansea.2026.100765","title":"AI for health in practice: local voices at the India AI impact summit 2026.","authorString":"Dharmendra A.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Dharmendra A","firstName":"Apoorva","lastName":"Dharmendra","initials":"A"}]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"48","journalIssueId":4179407,"dateOfPublication":"2026 May","monthOfPublication":5,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-05-01","journal":{"title":"The Lancet regional health. Southeast Asia","medlineAbbreviation":"Lancet Reg Health Southeast Asia","isoabbreviation":"Lancet Reg Health Southeast Asia","nlmid":"9918419282806676","essn":"2772-3682","issn":"2772-3682"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"100765","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["other","Journal Article"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.lansea.2026.100765"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13068647"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13068647?pdf=render"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.lansea.2026.100765"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"N","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-04-13","dateOfCreation":"2026-04-13","firstIndexDate":"2026-04-13","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-04-12","dateOfRevision":"2026-04-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-03-30","firstPublicationDate":"2026-03-30"},{"id":"42293851","source":"MED","pmid":"42293851","pmcid":"PMC13263806","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13263806"]},"doi":"10.21037/tau-2026-1-0097","title":"Large language models for post-discharge follow-up in erectile dysfunction care: a narrative review.","authorString":"Zhang Y, Lv B, Xu R, Shi C.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Zhang Y","firstName":"Yiman","lastName":"Zhang","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Nursing, The Second Affiliated Hospital Zhejiang University School of Medicine, Hangzhou, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Lv B","firstName":"Bodong","lastName":"Lv","initials":"B","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Urology, The Second Affiliated Hospital Zhejiang University School of Medicine, Hangzhou, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Xu R","firstName":"Runnan","lastName":"Xu","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Urology, The Second Affiliated Hospital Zhejiang University School of Medicine, Hangzhou, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Shi C","firstName":"Chenghao","lastName":"Shi","initials":"C","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0001-4402-814X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Nursing, The Second Affiliated Hospital Zhejiang University School of Medicine, Hangzhou, China."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0001-4402-814X"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"5","volume":"15","journalIssueId":4217778,"dateOfPublication":"2026 May","monthOfPublication":5,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-05-01","journal":{"title":"Translational andrology and urology","medlineAbbreviation":"Transl Androl Urol","isoabbreviation":"Transl Androl Urol","nlmid":"101581119","essn":"2223-4691","issn":"2223-4691"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"185","abstractText":"<h4>Background and objective</h4>Erectile dysfunction (ED) is common and closely linked to cardiometabolic disease, psychological distress, and relationship problems. Although effective treatments are available, outcomes depend on sustained post-discharge follow-up to assess response, adherence, adverse effects, and psychosocial needs. In practice, ED follow-up is often hindered by stigma, embarrassment, limited access, and fragmented communication. This narrative review examines the potential role, risks, and implementation requirements of large language model (LLM)-assisted follow-up in ED care.<h4>Methods</h4>We conducted a targeted literature search in PubMed and Embase up to 15 January 2026, supplemented by Google Scholar for citation tracking. We selected clinically relevant evidence and conceptual work on ED follow-up, conversational artificial intelligence (AI), and safety governance, and synthesized findings thematically.<h4>Key content and findings</h4>Among 280 included sources, only 10 were ED-specific, while 270 provided transferable evidence from chronic disease, oncology, and mental health follow-up settings. LLMs may reduce communication barriers, enable low-threshold symptom check-ins, reinforce discharge education, and collect patient-reported outcomes more frequently than traditional follow-up. Potential clinical value is greatest for structured, low-risk tasks such as education reinforcement, adherence support, and routine symptom monitoring. However, ED follow-up includes medication safety, nuanced counseling, mental health and relationship contexts, and sensitive data governance. Key risks include inaccurate or unsafe recommendations, privacy breaches, inappropriate handling of mental health red flags, and unclear accountability. A risk-stratified, human-in-the-loop implementation model can balance feasibility and safety by reserving autonomous LLM interactions for low-risk content while triggering clinician review and escalation for predefined high-risk scenarios.<h4>Conclusions</h4>LLM-assisted follow-up could become a useful adjunct in ED care if implemented with clear boundaries, auditability, privacy protection, and robust human oversight. A nurse-supervised, trigger-based governance model, which aligns with current follow-up workflows, may enhance continuity and responsiveness without compromising clinical accountability. Prospective evaluations should prioritize patient acceptability, safety outcomes, workload impact, and implementation feasibility.","affiliation":"Department of Nursing, The Second Affiliated Hospital Zhejiang University School of Medicine, Hangzhou, China.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Erectile dysfunction (ED)","Human-in-the-loop","Post-discharge Follow-up","Large Language Model (Llm)","Conversational Artificial Intelligence (Conversational Ai)"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.21037/tau-2026-1-0097"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13263806"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13263806?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by-nc-nd","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-15","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-15","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-16","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-24","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-05-26","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-26"},{"id":"42375201","source":"MED","pmid":"42375201","pmcid":"PMC13312419","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13312419"]},"doi":"10.3389/fmed.2026.1836430","title":"Leveraging transformer-based artificial intelligence for enhanced anesthetic decision-making in orthopedic surgery.","authorString":"Mao Y, Huang L, Li P, Qin Z, Wang L, Yan Y.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Mao Y","firstName":"Yuanzhou","lastName":"Mao","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Anesthesiology, Sichuan Province Orthopedic Hospital, Chengdu, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Huang L","firstName":"Lingyuan","lastName":"Huang","initials":"L","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Chengdu Studying Biotechnology Co., LTD, Chengdu, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Li P","firstName":"Peiyu","lastName":"Li","initials":"P","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Anesthesiology, Sichuan Province Orthopedic Hospital, Chengdu, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Qin Z","firstName":"Zhijun","lastName":"Qin","initials":"Z","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Anesthesiology, Sichuan Province Orthopedic Hospital, Chengdu, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Wang L","firstName":"Liting","lastName":"Wang","initials":"L","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Chengdu Studying Biotechnology Co., LTD, Chengdu, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Yan Y","firstName":"Yalan","lastName":"Yan","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Anesthesiology, Sichuan Province Orthopedic Hospital, Chengdu, China."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"13","journalIssueId":4110742,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Frontiers in medicine","medlineAbbreviation":"Front Med (Lausanne)","isoabbreviation":"Front Med (Lausanne)","nlmid":"101648047","essn":"2296-858X","issn":"2296-858X"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1836430","abstractText":"Orthopedic anesthesia necessitates the real-time integration of rapidly changing physiological states, specific interventions, and contextual narratives to avert hypotension, postoperative nausea and vomiting, and uncontrolled pain. We present Ortho PeriFT, a multimodal transformer that integrates perioperative prediction, therapeutic recommendations, and continuous monitoring with a calibrated uncertainty. The encoder was designed to align with clinical time scales by processing second-level waveform patches and minute-level numerical data, alongside medication and event tokens and extended clinical text, with optional prompts from preoperative imaging. Self-supervised pre-training on extensive clinical time series was followed by multitask fine-tuning for the primary endpoints. A constrained Decision Transformer suggests titrations of fluids, vasopressors, and anesthetic depth under guideline-aware action masks, with all outputs accompanied by conformal risk intervals to allow abstention when confidence is low. Across both internal and external cohorts, Ortho PeriFT enhanced discrimination and precision-recall for all primary outcomes compared to robust classical and neural baselines, reduced calibration error and negative log-likelihood, and maintained narrow uncertainty bands. Off-policy estimators indicate higher counterfactual utility than clinician behavior and behavior cloning while ensuring zero-guardrail violations. Streaming analyses demonstrated earlier warnings at matched false alarm rates, and performance generalized across orthopedic subtypes with stable calibration across demographic strata. Attribution maps and prototype trajectory retrieval offer case-based rationales that are aligned with clinical reasoning. These findings illustrate that a hierarchical, safety-aware, and interpretable transformer can provide accurate risk estimates, actionable therapeutic suggestions, and timely alerts for orthopedic anesthesia within a unified framework.","affiliation":"Department of Anesthesiology, Sichuan Province Orthopedic Hospital, Chengdu, China.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Orthopedic Anesthesia","Interpretability","Intraoperative Hypotension","Decision Transformer","Multimodal Transformer","Perioperative Decision Support"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fmed.2026.1836430"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13312419"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13312419?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-30","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-30","firstIndexDate":"2026-07-02","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-08-19","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-15","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-15"},{"id":"PMC12738109","source":"PMC","pmcid":"PMC12738109","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12738109"]},"title":"The AD Knowledge Portal: an open‐access platform for sharing data, tools, and results of aging and dementia research","authorString":"Scanlan J, Zintel T, Leanza Z, Britton J, Kallaher A, Beck J, Peña A, Poehlman W, Wiley J, Baham V, Klein M, Linden A, Greenwood A, Leal K, Malenfant J, Heath L, Varma S.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Scanlan J","firstName":"Jo","lastName":"Scanlan","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Sage Bionetworks, Seattle, WA, USA"}]}},{"fullName":"Zintel T","firstName":"Trisha M","lastName":"Zintel","initials":"T","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Sage Bionetworks, Seattle, WA, USA"}]}},{"fullName":"Leanza Z","firstName":"Zoë","lastName":"Leanza","initials":"Z","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Sage Bionetworks, Seattle, WA, USA"}]}},{"fullName":"Britton J","firstName":"Jessica S","lastName":"Britton","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Sage Bionetworks, Seattle, WA, USA"}]}},{"fullName":"Kallaher A","firstName":"Amelia","lastName":"Kallaher","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Sage Bionetworks, Seattle, WA, USA"}]}},{"fullName":"Beck J","firstName":"Jaclyn","lastName":"Beck","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Sage Bionetworks, Seattle, WA, USA"}]}},{"fullName":"Peña A","firstName":"Anthony","lastName":"Peña","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Sage Bionetworks, Seattle, WA, USA"}]}},{"fullName":"Poehlman W","firstName":"William L.","lastName":"Poehlman","initials":"W","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Sage Bionetworks, Seattle, WA, USA"}]}},{"fullName":"Wiley J","firstName":"Jesse C","lastName":"Wiley","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Sage Bionetworks, Seattle, WA, USA"}]}},{"fullName":"Baham V","firstName":"Victor","lastName":"Baham","initials":"V","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Sage Bionetworks, Seattle, WA, USA"}]}},{"fullName":"Klein M","firstName":"Melissa","lastName":"Klein","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Sage Bionetworks, Seattle, WA, USA"}]}},{"fullName":"Linden A","firstName":"Abby Vander","lastName":"Linden","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Sage Bionetworks, Seattle, WA, USA"}]}},{"fullName":"Greenwood A","firstName":"Anna","lastName":"Greenwood","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Sage Bionetworks, Seattle, WA, USA"}]}},{"fullName":"Leal K","firstName":"Karina","lastName":"Leal","initials":"K","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Sage Bionetworks, Seattle, WA, USA"}]}},{"fullName":"Malenfant J","firstName":"Jessica","lastName":"Malenfant","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Sage Bionetworks, Seattle, WA, USA"}]}},{"fullName":"Heath L","firstName":"Laura M","lastName":"Heath","initials":"L","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Sage Bionetworks, Seattle, WA, USA"}]}},{"fullName":"Varma S","firstName":"Susheel","lastName":"Varma","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Sage Bionetworks, Seattle, WA, USA"}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"Suppl 1","volume":"21","journalIssueId":4078562,"dateOfPublication":"2025 Dec","monthOfPublication":12,"yearOfPublication":2025,"printPublicationDate":"2025-12-01","journal":{"title":"Alzheimer's & dementia : the journal of the Alzheimer's Association","medlineAbbreviation":"Alzheimers Dement","isoabbreviation":"Alzheimers Dement","nlmid":"101231978","essn":"1552-5279","issn":"1552-5260"}},"pubYear":"2025","language":"eng","pubModel":"Undetermined","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Abstract"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"html","site":"PubMedCentral","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC12738109/?tool=EBI"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"PubMedCentral","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC12738109/pdf/?tool=EBI"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12738109"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12738109?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2025-12-31","firstIndexDate":"2026-01-02","fullTextReceivedDate":"2025-12-31","dateOfRevision":"2025-12-31","electronicPublicationDate":"2025-12-01","firstPublicationDate":"2025-12-01"},{"id":"42051329","source":"MED","pmid":"42051329","pmcid":"PMC13111446","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13111446"]},"doi":"10.3389/fdgth.2026.1750172","title":"Exploring plausible futures for artificial intelligence in rural healthcare: insights from participatory foresight methods.","authorString":"Cain T, Curtis R, Singh B, Smith AE, Brinsley J, Maher C, Davis A.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Cain T","firstName":"Tara","lastName":"Cain","initials":"T","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0005-3566-9972"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Alliance for Research in Exercise Nutrition and Activity (ARENA), UniSA Allied Health and Human Performance, University of South Australia, Adelaide, SA, Australia."}]}},{"fullName":"Curtis R","firstName":"Rachel","lastName":"Curtis","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Alliance for Research in Exercise Nutrition and Activity (ARENA), UniSA Allied Health and Human Performance, University of South Australia, Adelaide, SA, Australia."}]}},{"fullName":"Singh B","firstName":"Ben","lastName":"Singh","initials":"B","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Alliance for Research in Exercise Nutrition and Activity (ARENA), UniSA Allied Health and Human Performance, University of South Australia, Adelaide, SA, Australia."}]}},{"fullName":"Smith AE","firstName":"Ashleigh E","lastName":"Smith","initials":"AE","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Alliance for Research in Exercise Nutrition and Activity (ARENA), UniSA Allied Health and Human Performance, University of South Australia, Adelaide, SA, Australia."}]}},{"fullName":"Brinsley J","firstName":"Jacinta","lastName":"Brinsley","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Alliance for Research in Exercise Nutrition and Activity (ARENA), UniSA Allied Health and Human Performance, University of South Australia, Adelaide, SA, Australia."}]}},{"fullName":"Maher C","firstName":"Carol","lastName":"Maher","initials":"C","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Alliance for Research in Exercise Nutrition and Activity (ARENA), UniSA Allied Health and Human Performance, University of South Australia, Adelaide, SA, Australia."}]}},{"fullName":"Davis A","firstName":"Aaron","lastName":"Davis","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"UniSA Creative, University of South Australia, Adelaide, SA, Australia."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0005-3566-9972"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"8","journalIssueId":4117475,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Frontiers in digital health","medlineAbbreviation":"Front Digit Health","isoabbreviation":"Front Digit Health","nlmid":"101771889","essn":"2673-253X","issn":"2673-253X"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1750172","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Artificial intelligence (AI) has the potential to transform rural healthcare delivery through automated monitoring, personalised care, and virtual support. Yet the future pathways for AI in rural contexts remain underexplored. Most AI applications are developed in urban-centric environments with limited consideration for infrastructure constraints, workforce realities, and sociocultural dynamics that shape rural healthcare delivery.<h4>Methods</h4>This study examined stakeholder perspectives on the future role of AI in rural healthcare, identifying key priorities, facilitators, and barriers to adoption. Using a participatory research approach incorporating horizon scanning and foresight methods, data were collected during a structured workshop at the South Australian Rural Health Research and Education Conference. Forty participants, including general practitioners, clinicians, medical students, researchers, and healthcare administrators, engaged in four sequential activities: historical events mapping, future event possibilities, experiential future scenarios, and priority setting using the MoSCoW framework. Written responses were systematically transcribed and analysed using reflexive thematic analysis.<h4>Results</h4>Four prominent themes emerged capturing stakeholder priorities and the guardrails they considered essential for future technological integration. These themes related to opportunities from AI and technology deployment for rural and remote equity, people at the centre of care, ethical challenges, and funding and systems issues. Participants acknowledged AI's potential to reduce geographical barriers and improve access to healthcare services, while also raising concerns about data privacy, governance, cultural appropriateness, and the risk of technology exacerbating existing health disparities. Across activities, participants expressed a strong preference for AI that supports rather than replaces human clinicians, and emphasised the importance of maintaining person-centred care, human connection, and local knowledge.<h4>Discussion</h4>This study shows how futures-oriented, participatory methods can surface both the promise and the constraints of AI in rural healthcare. Successful implementation requires co-design with rural communities, equity-driven approaches, transparent governance frameworks, and investment in infrastructure and workforce capacity so that future technology adoption supports, rather than exacerbates, existing health disparities.","affiliation":"Alliance for Research in Exercise Nutrition and Activity (ARENA), UniSA Allied Health and Human Performance, University of South Australia, Adelaide, SA, Australia.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Participatory Research","Stakeholder Engagement","Rural Healthcare","Digital Health","Artificial Intelligence (Ai)","Equitable Access","Foresight Methods"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fdgth.2026.1750172"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13111446"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13111446?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-04-29","dateOfCreation":"2026-04-29","firstIndexDate":"2026-04-30","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-18","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-04-13","firstPublicationDate":"2026-04-13"},{"id":"41952825","source":"MED","pmid":"41952825","pmcid":"PMC13054807","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13054807"]},"doi":"10.3389/fpubh.2026.1603238","title":"Toward clinical integration of generative AI in mental health: personalization, multimodality and inter-entity experience.","authorString":"Frisone F, Pupillo C, Rossi C, Riva G.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Frisone F","firstName":"Fabio","lastName":"Frisone","initials":"F","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1766-831X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Humane Technology Laboratory, Catholic University of the Sacred Heart, Milan, Italy."},{"affiliation":"Sophia University Institute, Figline e Incisa Valdarno (Firenze), Italy."}]}},{"fullName":"Pupillo C","firstName":"Chiara","lastName":"Pupillo","initials":"C","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0007-5764-1211"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Humane Technology Laboratory, Catholic University of the Sacred Heart, Milan, Italy."},{"affiliation":"Department of Computer Science, University of Pisa, Pisa, Italy."}]}},{"fullName":"Rossi C","firstName":"Chiara","lastName":"Rossi","initials":"C","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Humane Technology Laboratory, Catholic University of the Sacred Heart, Milan, Italy."},{"affiliation":"Department of Human Sciences, Guglielmo Marconi University, Rome, Italy."}]}},{"fullName":"Riva G","firstName":"Giuseppe","lastName":"Riva","initials":"G","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Humane Technology Laboratory, Catholic University of the Sacred Heart, Milan, Italy."},{"affiliation":"Applied Technology for Neuro-Psychology Laboratory, IRCCS Istituto Auxologico Italiano, Milan, Italy."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1766-831X"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0007-5764-1211"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"14","journalIssueId":4110762,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Frontiers in public health","medlineAbbreviation":"Front Public Health","isoabbreviation":"Front Public Health","nlmid":"101616579","essn":"2296-2565","issn":"2296-2565"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1603238","abstractText":"The integration of conversational agents (CAs) into mental health care presents a promising yet complex frontier. These systems have demonstrated potential in expanding access, enhancing user engagement and providing scalable support, particularly in contexts where human clinicians are unavailable. Despite these advantages, CAs face two fundamental limitations: their quality of interaction and the ability to facilitate self-reflection. This perspective article provides a theoretical framework with clinical relevance, identifying the critical challenges that must be addressed to transition Generative Artificial Intelligence (GenAI) from a conversational tool to an adjunctive psychological support. Firstly, personalization is essential. Advances in retrieval-augmented generation (RAG), fine-tuning, and emotional reasoning are necessary to enable context-aware and ethically grounded responses tailored to individual users. In addition, multimodal interaction (particularly through improvements in speech synthesis, prosody, and expressive dialogue) can help bridge the gap between human and AI communication, fostering greater emotional resonance and natural flow. Lastly, immersive environments, including embodied CA and virtual reality settings, may amplify presence, potentially engaging neural and psychological mechanisms typically associated with human-to-human interaction. These innovations must be accompanied by a strong ethical and regulatory foundation. Systems must ensure transparency, informed consent, and compliance with data privacy standards such as GDPR and HIPAA. Crucially, AI should not be viewed as a replacement for psychologists, but as an adaptive and supportive layer within a broader care ecosystem. By aligning technological capabilities with clinical intent, the future of GenAI in mental health may lie in its ability to complement human expertise and meaningfully extend psychological support.","affiliation":"Humane Technology Laboratory, Catholic University of the Sacred Heart, Milan, Italy.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["brief-report","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Communication"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Mental Health"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Artificial Intelligence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Generative Artificial Intelligence"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Virtual reality","Mental health","Psychological Support","General Psychology","Clinical Psychology","Generative Artificial Intelligence"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fpubh.2026.1603238"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13054807"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13054807?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":2,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-07-14","dateOfCreation":"2026-04-09","firstIndexDate":"2026-04-10","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-04-08","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-03-24","firstPublicationDate":"2026-03-24"},{"id":"42272686","source":"MED","pmid":"42272686","pmcid":"PMC13246499","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13246499"]},"doi":"10.1177/15357597261427887","title":"Show me the Money: Finance Models in Epilepsy Practice.","authorString":"Zutshi D, Klatte E, Basha M, Osburn L, Segal E, Garga N, Das RR.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Zutshi D","firstName":"Deepti","lastName":"Zutshi","initials":"D","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Wayne State University, Detroit, MI, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Klatte E","firstName":"Emily","lastName":"Klatte","initials":"E","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0009-5157-4147"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Ohio Health Neuroscience Center, Columbus, OH, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Basha M","firstName":"Maysaa","lastName":"Basha","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1561-1667"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Wayne State University, Detroit, MI, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Osburn L","firstName":"Leisha","lastName":"Osburn","initials":"L","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"NexGen Neuro LLC, Plainfield, IN, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Segal E","firstName":"Eric","lastName":"Segal","initials":"E","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Northeastern Regional Epilepsy Group, New York, NY, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Garga N","firstName":"Nina","lastName":"Garga","initials":"N","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0003-5903-5507"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"San Francisco VA Healthcare System, San Francisco, CA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Das RR","firstName":"Rohit R","lastName":"Das","initials":"RR","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0093-1799"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"VA Portland Healthcare System, Portland, OR, USA."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0093-1799"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1561-1667"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0003-5903-5507"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0009-5157-4147"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics"]},"journalInfo":{"journalIssueId":4208095,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-06-01","journal":{"title":"Epilepsy currents","medlineAbbreviation":"Epilepsy Curr","isoabbreviation":"Epilepsy Curr","nlmid":"101135954","essn":"1535-7511","issn":"1535-7597"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"15357597261427887","abstractText":"Healthcare models and the finances associated with such models are complex. Patients with epilepsy (PWE) receive care in a variety of settings, and each is associated with differing reimbursement, financial levers, as well as unique strengths and weaknesses. As the financial climate of healthcare system changes rapidly in the setting of an aging population, physician shortages, and increasing costs of healthcare delivery, it is important for clinicians to understand the financial aspects of caring for PWE. In this review, we examine basic concepts in healthcare economics and then review the financial aspects of care in an academic context, a private practice, a capitated healthcare system, and in tele-electroencephalography services.","affiliation":"Wayne State University, Detroit, MI, USA.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Epilepsy","Reimbursement","Finance","Telehealth","Healthcare Models"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1177/15357597261427887"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13246499"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13246499?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by-nc","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-11","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-11","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-10","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-08","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-08"},{"id":"41977066","source":"MED","pmid":"41977066","pmcid":"PMC13073410","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13073410"]},"doi":"10.3390/jcm15072767","title":"Artificial Intelligence in Head and Neck Surgical Oncology: A State-of-the-Art Review.","authorString":"Chen SX, Feucht M, Bhatt A, Farlow JL.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Chen SX","firstName":"Steven X","lastName":"Chen","initials":"SX","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Otolaryngology-Head and Neck Surgery, Indiana University School of Medicine, Indiana University, Indianapolis, IN 46202, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Feucht M","firstName":"Maria","lastName":"Feucht","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Otolaryngology-Head and Neck Surgery, Indiana University School of Medicine, Indiana University, Indianapolis, IN 46202, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Bhatt A","firstName":"Aditya","lastName":"Bhatt","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0003-2111-3813"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Otolaryngology-Head and Neck Surgery, Indiana University School of Medicine, Indiana University, Indianapolis, IN 46202, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Farlow JL","firstName":"Janice L","lastName":"Farlow","initials":"JL","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1214-4960"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Otolaryngology-Head and Neck Surgery, Indiana University School of Medicine, Indiana University, Indianapolis, IN 46202, USA."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1214-4960"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0003-2111-3813"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"7","volume":"15","journalIssueId":4179668,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Apr","monthOfPublication":4,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-04-01","journal":{"title":"Journal of clinical medicine","medlineAbbreviation":"J Clin Med","isoabbreviation":"J Clin Med","nlmid":"101606588","essn":"2077-0383","issn":"2077-0383"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"2767","abstractText":"Artificial intelligence (AI) is rapidly reshaping head and neck surgical oncology by augmenting decision-making across the full perioperative continuum. This state-of-the-art review aims to provide head and neck surgical oncologists with a conceptual framework for understanding and critically appraising AI tools entering clinical practice, summarizing how machine learning, deep learning, and generative AI are being integrated into contemporary surgical workflows. Preoperative applications include detection of occult nodal metastasis and extranodal extension. Intraoperative innovations include augmented reality-assisted navigation, real-time margin assessment, and improving visual clarity and tissue handling for robotic platforms. Postoperatively, AI can predict complications like free flap failure and oncologic outcomes. Large language models are being operationalized for clinician-facing applications such as documentation and inbox support, as well as patient-facing education. Despite promising results, broad clinical deployment remains limited by concerns about privacy, validation, reliability, safety, and ethics. Widespread adoption will require prospective clinical trials, robust governance, and human-centered workflows that ensure AI remains a safe, assistive copilot.","affiliation":"Department of Otolaryngology-Head and Neck Surgery, Indiana University School of Medicine, Indiana University, Indianapolis, IN 46202, USA.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Computer vision","Surgery","Oncology","Otorhinolaryngology","Head And Neck","Machine Learning","Augmented Reality","Deep Learning","Large Language Model"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/jcm15072767"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13073410"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13073410?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-04-14","dateOfCreation":"2026-04-14","firstIndexDate":"2026-04-14","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-04-20","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-04-06","firstPublicationDate":"2026-04-06"},{"id":"41643212","source":"MED","pmid":"41643212","pmcid":"PMC12875660","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12875660"]},"doi":"10.2196/88651","title":"Ethical Risks and Structural Implications of AI-Mediated Medical Interpreting.","authorString":"Lopez Vera A.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Lopez Vera A","firstName":"Alexandra","lastName":"Lopez Vera","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7659-3672"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"California University of Science and Medicine, 1501 Violet St, Colton, CA, 92324, United States, 1 9095809661."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7659-3672"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"5","journalIssueId":4116162,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Feb","monthOfPublication":2,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-02-01","journal":{"title":"JMIR AI","medlineAbbreviation":"JMIR AI","isoabbreviation":"JMIR AI","nlmid":"9918645789006676","essn":"2817-1705","issn":"2817-1705"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e88651","abstractText":"<h4>Unlabelled</h4>Artificial intelligence (AI) is increasingly used to support medical interpreting and public health communication, yet current systems introduce serious risks to accuracy, confidentiality, and equity, particularly for speakers of low-resource languages. Automatic translation models often struggle with regional varieties, figurative language, culturally embedded meanings, and emotionally sensitive conversations about reproductive health or chronic disease, which can lead to clinically significant misunderstandings. These limitations threaten patient safety, informed consent, and trust in health systems when clinicians rely on AI as if it were a professional interpreter. At the same time, the large data sets required to train and maintain these systems create new concerns about surveillance, secondary use of linguistic data, and gaps in existing privacy protections. This viewpoint examines the ethical and structural implications of AI-mediated interpreting in clinical and public health settings, arguing that its routine use as a replacement for qualified interpreters would normalize a lower standard of care for people with Non-English Language Preference and reinforce existing health disparities. Instead, AI tools should be treated as optional, carefully evaluated supplements that operate under the supervision of trained clinicians and professional interpreters, within clear regulatory guardrails for transparency, accountability, and community oversight. The paper concludes that language access must remain grounded in human expertise, language rights, and structural commitments to equity, rather than in cost-saving promises of automated systems.","affiliation":"California University of Science and Medicine, 1501 Violet St, Colton, CA, 92324, United States, 1 9095809661.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Health Equity","Clinical Communication","Language Access","Ai-Mediated Interpreting"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2196/88651"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12875660"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12875660?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-02-05","dateOfCreation":"2026-02-05","firstIndexDate":"2026-02-10","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-02-14","dateOfRevision":"2026-02-07","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-02-05","firstPublicationDate":"2026-02-05"},{"id":"41717587","source":"MED","pmid":"41717587","pmcid":"PMC12914512","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12914512"]},"doi":"10.1016/j.eclinm.2026.103784","title":"Do world-wide policy initiatives for regulating health care related artificial intelligence safeguard the declaration of Helsinki?","authorString":"Armoundas AA, Loscalzo J.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Armoundas AA","firstName":"Antonis A","lastName":"Armoundas","initials":"AA","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5006-1547"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Cardiovascular Research Center, Massachusetts General Hospital, United States."},{"affiliation":"Broad Institute, Massachusetts Institute of Technology, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Loscalzo J","firstName":"Joseph","lastName":"Loscalzo","initials":"J","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1153-8047"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Broad Institute, Massachusetts Institute of Technology, United States."},{"affiliation":"Division of Cardiovascular Medicine, Channing Division of Network Medicine, Department of Medicine, Brigham and Women's Hospital, Harvard Medical School, United States."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5006-1547"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1153-8047"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"92","journalIssueId":4112099,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Feb","monthOfPublication":2,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-02-01","journal":{"title":"EClinicalMedicine","medlineAbbreviation":"EClinicalMedicine","isoabbreviation":"EClinicalMedicine","nlmid":"101733727","essn":"2589-5370","issn":"2589-5370"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"103784","abstractText":"Digital health technologies and artificial intelligence (AI), are transforming medical research, health care, and public health. The ever-increasing usage of algorithms in health care has challenged governments, regulatory agencies, health organizations, developers, and providers, and AI raises novel ethical challenges that extend beyond the jurisdiction of traditional borders and regulatory health-care processes and structures. While there is growing consensus in recognizing these ethical challenges, there is less agreement over the necessary AI guardrails. This Viewpoint offers a synthesis of representative AI-enabled health policy approaches across jurisdictions and advances practical recommendations for an adaptive, international AI policy and governance framework that will be responsible for monitoring and advancing its regulations in pace with the rapid growth of AI technologies. We use the Declaration of Helsinki as a normative reference point, to derive risk-proportionate safeguards for AI-enabled health across research and non-research settings.","affiliation":"Cardiovascular Research Center, Massachusetts General Hospital, United States.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"grantId":"3R21EB026164-02S1","agency":"NIH","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"AHA24MERIT1185447","agency":"American Heart Association Inc","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"AHA24MERIT1185447","agency":"NIH","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"1 R01 HL166137","agency":"NIH","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"17UNPG33840017","agency":"American Heart Association Inc","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"1 R21 HL137870-01","agency":"NIH","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"1 R01 HL161008-01","agency":"NIH","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"5 R01 HL155107","agency":"NIH","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"U01 HG007691","agency":"NIH","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"1 R01 HL135335-01","agency":"NIH","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"AHA957729","agency":"American Heart Association Inc","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"AHA957729","agency":"NIH","orderIn":0}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Regulation","Policy","Human Rights","Global"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.eclinm.2026.103784"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12914512"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12914512?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":1,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-02-20","dateOfCreation":"2026-02-20","firstIndexDate":"2026-02-21","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-02-19","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-02-11","firstPublicationDate":"2026-02-11"},{"id":"42278712","source":"MED","pmid":"42278712","pmcid":"PMC13256130","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13256130"]},"doi":"10.3390/healthcare14111459","title":"Beyond Model Development in Healthcare AI: Post-Development Robustness, Post-Deployment Monitoring, and Lifecycle Governance-A Scoping Review of Reviews.","authorString":"El Arab RA, Mustafa MH, Almagharbeh WT, Saleem NH, Al Abdulmohsen S, Boathab R, Bu Washl M.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"El Arab RA","firstName":"Rabie Adel","lastName":"El Arab","initials":"RA","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3822-9236"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Almoosa College of Health Sciences, Al Ahsa 36422, Saudi Arabia."}]}},{"fullName":"Mustafa MH","firstName":"Mohammad Hussein","lastName":"Mustafa","initials":"MH","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0007-2937-4347"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Dr. Sulaiman Alhabib Medical Group, Riyadh 71491, Saudi Arabia."}]}},{"fullName":"Almagharbeh WT","firstName":"Wesam Taher","lastName":"Almagharbeh","initials":"WT","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8435-1208"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Medical and Surgical Nursing Department, Faculty of Nursing, University of Tabuk, Tabuk 71491, Saudi Arabia."}]}},{"fullName":"Saleem NH","firstName":"Noor Hafiz","lastName":"Saleem","initials":"NH","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Nursing Department, Fatima College of Health Sciences, Abu Dhabi P.O. Box 3798, United Arab Emirates."}]}},{"fullName":"Al Abdulmohsen S","firstName":"Shahad","lastName":"Al Abdulmohsen","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Almoosa College of Health Sciences, Al Ahsa 36422, Saudi Arabia."}]}},{"fullName":"Boathab R","firstName":"Ritaj","lastName":"Boathab","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Almoosa College of Health Sciences, Al Ahsa 36422, Saudi Arabia."}]}},{"fullName":"Bu Washl M","firstName":"Mohammed","lastName":"Bu Washl","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Almoosa College of Health Sciences, Al Ahsa 36422, Saudi Arabia."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3822-9236"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8435-1208"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0007-2937-4347"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"11","volume":"14","journalIssueId":4215131,"dateOfPublication":"2026 May","monthOfPublication":5,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-05-01","journal":{"title":"Healthcare (Basel, Switzerland)","medlineAbbreviation":"Healthcare (Basel)","isoabbreviation":"Healthcare (Basel)","nlmid":"101666525","essn":"2227-9032","issn":"2227-9032"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1459","abstractText":"<b>Background:</b> Clinical artificial intelligence (AI) is rapidly moving from retrospective model development into prospective evaluation, implementation, and routine care. Existing reviews have addressed specific aspects of this transition, including monitoring, drift, implementation, governance, and human-AI interaction; however, these bodies of work remain methodologically and conceptually fragmented across different review traditions. Methods: We conducted a scoping review of review-level and review-oriented literature. We searched MEDLINE, Embase, Scopus, and Web of Science Core Collection from database inception to 28 February 2026. We charted review characteristics and conducted an inductive thematic synthesis of extracted review-level findings, while distinguishing operational, deployment-proximal, methodological, and conceptual/governance-oriented evidence. <b>Results:</b> We included 25 review-level publications spanning systematic, scoping, methodological, narrative, and governance-oriented reviews. Three major themes emerged. First, clinically important risks were consistently framed as socio-technical rather than purely algorithmic: trustworthiness depended not only on technical performance, but also on fairness, transparency, workflow fit, human oversight, and organisational readiness. Second, the included review literature consistently recommended post-deployment monitoring but showed limited operational maturity; monitoring methods, action thresholds, fairness surveillance, and corrective responses were weakly standardised, and mature evidence from activated systems in routine care remained sparse. Third, trustworthy implementation was increasingly framed as a lifecycle governance challenge extending beyond procurement and initial validation to include local validation, subgroup auditing, drift detection, controlled updating, incident response, and, where necessary, rollback or retirement. <b>Discussion:</b> The review literature suggests a persistent normative-operational gap, meaning that recommendations about what trustworthy clinical AI should require have advanced faster than evidence on how monitoring, updating, and governance are implemented in routine care. The strongest unresolved challenge is therefore not principal generation alone, but the translation of monitoring and governance expectations into actionable operational systems. <b>Conclusions:</b> Post-development trustworthiness in clinical AI should be understood as a lifecycle property, not a one-time technical achievement. Future work should prioritise stronger operational evidence, clearer reporting of deployment-proximal and post-deployment evaluation, methodological standardisation of monitoring metrics and thresholds, implementation research on feasible governance models, and evaluation frameworks for assessing post-deployment safety, fairness, accountability, and sustainability.","affiliation":"Almoosa College of Health Sciences, Al Ahsa 36422, Saudi Arabia.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"grantId":"1232","agency":"Almoosa Collegeof Health Sciences will cover APC","orderIn":0}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Robustness","Trustworthiness","Model Updating","Dataset Shift","Human–Ai Interaction","Post-deployment Monitoring","Clinical Artificial Intelligence","Algorithmovigilance","Local Validation","Lifecycle Governance"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/healthcare14111459"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13256130"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13256130?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-12","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-12","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-12","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-13","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-05-25","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-25"},{"id":"PPR1181773","source":"PPR","doi":"10.20944/preprints202604.1595.v1","title":"Stateful Guardrails for Multi-Turn LLM Systems: A Conversational Risk Accumulation Framework","authorString":"Mishra S, Naik GR.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Mishra S","firstName":"Sanjay","lastName":"Mishra","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0006-7854-996X"}},{"fullName":"Naik GR","firstName":"Ganesh","lastName":"Naik","initials":"GR"}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0006-7854-996X"}]},"pubYear":"2026","abstractText":"Guardrail systems for large language models (LLMs) are designed under a foundational but rarely examined assumption: that safety is a property of individual input–output exchanges. This assumption is adequate for single-turn deployments but fails structurally in multi-turn conversational systems, where risk does not reside in any single message but emerges from the accumulated trajectory of a session. We formalize this failure mode as Conversational Risk Accumulation (CRA), a class of adversarial and incidental threat patterns in which individually policy-compliant turns collectively produce outcomes that violate safety intent. We propose a stateful guardrail architecture, the CRA Framework, comprising three novel constructs: (1) a Semantic Drift Monitor that tracks divergence from declared session intent; (2) an Information Accumulation Graph (IAG) that models cross-turn entity and attribute disclosure as a growing knowledge structure; and (3) a Compliance Gradient Detector that identifies progressive erosion of refusal behavior across turns. These three signals are fused into a session-level CRA Score, which triggers guardrail intervention at the conversation layer rather than the message layer. We formalize the threat taxonomy, define the mathematical properties of the CRA Score, and derive theoretical bounds on detection latency. The framework is domain-agnostic and architecturally composable with existing single-turn guardrail systems. We discuss instantiation across the enterprise RAG deployments, agentic pipelines, and educational AI systems, and identify open problems in stateful safety that the framework surfaces.","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Preprint"]},"bookOrReportDetails":{"publisher":"Preprints.org","yearOfPublication":2026},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.20944/preprints202604.1595.v1"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"N","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","versionList":{"version":[{"id":"PPR1181773","source":"PPR","firstPublishDate":"2026-04-22","versionNumber":1,"pubTypeList":{"pubType":["preprint"]},"hasEvaluations":"N"}]},"versionNumber":1,"hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-04-25","firstIndexDate":"2026-04-25","firstPublicationDate":"2026-04-22"},{"id":"41780204","source":"MED","pmid":"41780204","doi":"10.1016/j.nedt.2026.107055","title":"Constructing digital professionalism in the generative AI era: A qualitative study with nursing students and faculty.","authorString":"Ali SI, Shaban M.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Ali SI","firstName":"Sayed Ibrahim","lastName":"Ali","initials":"SI","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8808-6296"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Family and Community Medicine, College of Medicine, King Faisal University, 31982, Al-Ahsa, Saudi Arabia. Electronic address: seali@kfu.edu.sa."}]}},{"fullName":"Shaban M","firstName":"Mostafa","lastName":"Shaban","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Gerontological Nursing Department, Faculty Of Nursing, Cairo University, Cairo, Egypt. Electronic address: mostafa.shaban2020@cu.edu.eg."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8808-6296"}]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"162","journalIssueId":4179561,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jul","monthOfPublication":7,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-07-01","journal":{"title":"Nurse education today","medlineAbbreviation":"Nurse Educ Today","isoabbreviation":"Nurse Educ Today","nlmid":"8511379","essn":"1532-2793","issn":"0260-6917"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"107055","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Generative artificial intelligence (AI) is entering coursework, simulation, and assessment in nursing programs. Conventional digital professionalism focused on online conduct now requires visible accountability for algorithmic assistance to protect learning, integrity, and equity.<h4>Aim</h4>To explore how pre-licensure nursing students and faculty conceptualize and enact digital professionalism when using generative AI in coursework, simulation, and assessment, and to develop an explanatory model to inform policy, pedagogy, and assessment.<h4>Design</h4>Qualitative phenomenological study.<h4>Methods</h4>Twenty-four participants from a public college of nursing and affiliated teaching sites in Saudi Arabia (16 students, 8 faculty) completed in-depth, semi-structured interviews in Arabic or English. Audio-recorded interviews were transcribed, translated as needed, and analyzed thematically using Braun and Clarke's six-phase approach supported by NVivo. Trustworthiness was enhanced through maximum-variation sampling, member reflections, an audit trail, and analyst triangulation. Reporting followed SRQR.<h4>Results</h4>Four themes described how participants stabilized professional practice with AI. 1) Professionalism as transparent practice: disclosure as academic provenance and verification as routine ethical habit. 2) Uneven rules, uneven risks: inconsistent course policies and tacit norms prompting avoidance or minimal disclosure, with greater perceived risk for language learners and less resourced students. 3) Human-in-the-loop professionalism: right-sized guardrails that fit real workloads, including brief provenance notes, proportionate verification, no-upload rules for protected materials, and human-led debriefing and marking. 4) Becoming a professional with AI: identity work from stigma to legitimacy supported by shared vocabulary, model disclosures, and concise verification checklists. These findings informed a simplified model in which disclose, verify, and revise under human authorship are enabled when guardrails are feasible and expectations explicit.<h4>Conclusions</h4>Digital professionalism with AI functions as a teachable accountability process. Clear, proportionate expectations promote integrity, assessor independence, and improved equity.","affiliation":"Department of Family and Community Medicine, College of Medicine, King Faisal University, 31982, Al-Ahsa, Saudi Arabia. Electronic address: seali@kfu.edu.sa.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"King Faisal University","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Qualitative Research"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Education, Nursing, Baccalaureate","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Faculty, Nursing","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"PX","qualifierName":"psychology","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Students, Nursing","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"PX","qualifierName":"psychology","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Adult"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Saudi Arabia"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Female"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Male"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Professionalism","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"ST","qualifierName":"standards","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Academia"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Generative Artificial Intelligence"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Nursing Education","Qualitative Study","Academic Integrity","Digital Professionalism","Generative Artificial Intelligence"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.nedt.2026.107055"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":1,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-07-10","dateOfCreation":"2026-03-04","firstIndexDate":"2026-03-06","dateOfRevision":"2026-07-10","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-03-03","firstPublicationDate":"2026-03-03"},{"id":"PPR1163978","source":"PPR","doi":"10.20944/preprints202603.0668.v1","title":"<span class=\"word\">A Sovereign<span class=\"word\"> <span class=\"word\">Conversational <span class=\"word\">Assistant <span class=\"word\"><span class=\"changedDisabled\">Powered <span class=\"word\">by <span class=\"word allCaps\">ALIA <span class=\"word\">and <span class=\"word\">Mistral <span class=\"word\">for <span class=\"word\">the <span class=\"word allCaps\">AI <span class=\"word\">Act <span class=\"word\"><span class=\"changedDisabled\">Age: <span class=\"word\">Architecture, <span class=\"word\">Governance <span class=\"word\">and <span class=\"word\">Evaluation","authorString":"Carmona-Martínez A, Jara A, Asín A.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Carmona-Martínez A","firstName":"Alejandro","lastName":"Carmona-Martínez","initials":"A"},{"fullName":"Jara A","firstName":"Antonio","lastName":"Jara","initials":"A"},{"fullName":"Asín A","firstName":"Alicia","lastName":"Asín","initials":"A"}]},"pubYear":"2026","abstractText":"Cultural-heritage destinations are adopting digital twins and Living Labs to improve conservation, safety, and visitor experience. Operationalising these initiatives requires trustworthy interfaces capable of answering questions grounded in authoritative sources under public-sector governance constraints. We present a Sovereign Conversational Assistant (SCA) based on a small-language-model (SLM) plus retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) platform designed for the next generation Libelium Heritage Living Lab. This assistant is, therefore, agnostic from any specific LLM. Testing has focused on the usage of newly released, Barcelona Supercomputing Center’s own BSC-LT/ALIA-40b-instruct-2601 as well as the mistralai/Mistral-Small-3.2-24B-Instruct-2506, one of the SoTA standard bearer for mid-size SLMs. It integrates provenance logging, safety controls, and language enforcement. We evaluate the assistant benchmark on 19 tests across five categories: historical queries, client experience, data analysis, hallucination resistance, and safety/ethics. Our findings reveal that while both models adeptly retrieve factual historical and operational information, their reliability diverges under complex conditions. Mistral achieved a 100% pass rate across all tests, demonstrating strong analytical capabilities without hallucination and keeping up with the multilingual and safety guardrails, too. In contrast, ALIA struggled with numerical values drifting during data analysis and exhibited vulnerabilities in cross-language scenarios. The results show that a compact, sovereign RAG stack running on ALIA can meet core information needs in English and Spanish for Heritage Living Labs, while highlighting the necessity of refusal robustness and explicit multilingual control for public-facing deployment.","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Preprint"]},"bookOrReportDetails":{"publisher":"Preprints.org","yearOfPublication":2026},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.20944/preprints202603.0668.v1"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"N","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","versionList":{"version":[{"id":"PPR1163978","source":"PPR","firstPublishDate":"2026-03-09","versionNumber":1,"pubTypeList":{"pubType":["preprint"]},"hasEvaluations":"N"}]},"versionNumber":1,"hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-03-12","firstIndexDate":"2026-03-12","firstPublicationDate":"2026-03-09"},{"id":"41865007","source":"MED","pmid":"41865007","pmcid":"PMC13172386","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13172386"]},"doi":"10.1038/s41467-026-70917-y","title":"Revealing the intrinsic ethical vulnerability of aligned large language models.","authorString":"Lian J, Pan J, Wang L, Wang Y, Wang X, Lu Y, Mei S, Chau LP.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Lian J","firstName":"Jiawei","lastName":"Lian","initials":"J","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3899-0797"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Electrical and Electronic Engineering, The Hong Kong Polytechnic University, Hong Kong SAR, China."},{"affiliation":"School of Electronics and Information, Northwestern Polytechnical University, Xi'an, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Pan J","firstName":"Jianhong","lastName":"Pan","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Electrical and Electronic Engineering, The Hong Kong Polytechnic University, Hong Kong SAR, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Wang L","firstName":"Lefan","lastName":"Wang","initials":"L","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0007-8645-5872"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Electronics and Information, Northwestern Polytechnical University, Xi'an, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Wang Y","firstName":"Yi","lastName":"Wang","initials":"Y","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8659-4724"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Electrical and Electronic Engineering, The Hong Kong Polytechnic University, Hong Kong SAR, China. yi-eie.wang@polyu.edu.hk."}]}},{"fullName":"Wang X","firstName":"Xiaofei","lastName":"Wang","initials":"X","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0004-6683-3969"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Electronics and Information, Northwestern Polytechnical University, Xi'an, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Lu Y","firstName":"Yingjie","lastName":"Lu","initials":"Y","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0000-9096-1208"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Electronics and Information, Northwestern Polytechnical University, Xi'an, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Mei S","firstName":"Shaohui","lastName":"Mei","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8018-596X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Electronics and Information, Northwestern Polytechnical University, Xi'an, China. meish@nwpu.edu.cn."}]}},{"fullName":"Chau LP","firstName":"Lap-Pui","lastName":"Chau","initials":"LP","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4932-0593"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Electrical and Electronic Engineering, The Hong Kong Polytechnic University, Hong Kong SAR, China. lap-pui.chau@polyu.edu.hk."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8659-4724"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3899-0797"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8018-596X"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4932-0593"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0000-9096-1208"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0004-6683-3969"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0007-8645-5872"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"17","journalIssueId":4138066,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Mar","monthOfPublication":3,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-03-01","journal":{"title":"Nature communications","medlineAbbreviation":"Nat Commun","isoabbreviation":"Nat Commun","nlmid":"101528555","essn":"2041-1723","issn":"2041-1723"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"4295","abstractText":"Large language models (LLMs) represent foundational advances toward artificial general intelligence, yet their alignment with human values via instruction tuning and preference learning achieves only superficial ethical compliance. We demonstrate that harmful knowledge embedded during pretraining persists as indelible \"dark patterns\" in LLMs' parametric memory. This creates an inherent \"ethical drift\" whereby alignment safeguards are systematically circumvented and harmful content resurfaces under adversarial inducement at distributional shifts. Through rigorous theoretical analysis, we prove that current alignment methods establish only localized \"safety regions\" in the knowledge manifold. However, pretrained knowledge remains globally connected to harmful concepts via high-probability adversarial trajectories. We empirically validate these theoretical insights through a straightforward yet theoretically grounded methodology-semantic coherence inducement under distributional shifts. The effectiveness of this approach, achieving a 100% attack success rate across 22 out of 26 state-of-the-art aligned LLMs (including DeepSeek-R1, Llama-3, and Qwen3, among others), is not incidental but a direct consequence of our theoretical framework, demonstrating that the vulnerability is architectural rather than implementation-specific and revealing a fundamental structural weakness in current aligned LLMs.","affiliation":"Department of Electrical and Electronic Engineering, The Hong Kong Polytechnic University, Hong Kong SAR, China.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"grantId":"62171381","agency":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"62171381","agency":"National Natural Science Foundation of China (National Science Foundation of China)","orderIn":0}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-026-70917-y"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13172386"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13172386?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by-nc-nd","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-03-22","firstIndexDate":"2026-03-23","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-20","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-03-21","firstPublicationDate":"2026-03-21"},{"id":"40322603","source":"MED","pmid":"40322603","pmcid":"PMC12048886","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12048886"]},"doi":"10.7759/cureus.83390","title":"The Agentic Era: Why Biopharma Must Embrace Artificial Intelligence That Acts, Not Just Informs.","authorString":"Lakhan SE.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Lakhan SE","firstName":"Shaheen E","lastName":"Lakhan","initials":"SE","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2994-3630"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Bioscience, Global Neuroscience Initiative Foundation, Miami, USA."},{"affiliation":"Neurology, A.T. Still University School of Osteopathic Medicine in Arizona, Mesa, USA."},{"affiliation":"Neurology, Morehouse School of Medicine, Atlanta, USA."},{"affiliation":"Neurology, Western University of Health Sciences, Pomona, USA."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2994-3630"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"5","volume":"17","journalIssueId":3937269,"dateOfPublication":"2025 May","monthOfPublication":5,"yearOfPublication":2025,"printPublicationDate":"2025-05-01","journal":{"title":"Cureus","medlineAbbreviation":"Cureus","isoabbreviation":"Cureus","nlmid":"101596737","essn":"2168-8184","issn":"2168-8184"}},"pubYear":"2025","pageInfo":"e83390","abstractText":"Agentic artificial intelligence (AI), characterized by its capacity for autonomous task execution rather than mere assistance, heralds a fundamental paradigm shift in biopharmaceutical operations. From accelerating discovery and streamlining development to revolutionizing commercialization and governance, agentic AI systems are poised to redefine the industry's core processes. This editorial explores the burgeoning role of agentic AI as a novel operating model for biopharma, highlighting its increasing relevance across critical functions, including drug development, clinical trials, regulatory affairs, and strategic decision-making. As regulatory frameworks adapt to accommodate AI-driven methodologies, agentic systems are positioned to supersede legacy infrastructures with self-directed, adaptive engines of innovation. The future of biopharma will be defined not only by groundbreaking molecular discoveries but also by the intelligent orchestration of these advancements through autonomous machines.","affiliation":"Bioscience, Global Neuroscience Initiative Foundation, Miami, USA.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Editorial"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Clinical Trials","Drug Development","Autonomous Systems","Regulatory Transformation","Agentic Ai"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.7759/cureus.83390"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12048886"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12048886?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":3,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2025-05-05","firstIndexDate":"2025-05-06","fullTextReceivedDate":"2025-12-02","dateOfRevision":"2025-05-07","electronicPublicationDate":"2025-05-03","firstPublicationDate":"2025-05-03"},{"id":"41485858","source":"MED","pmid":"41485858","pmcid":"PMC12965389","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12965389"]},"doi":"10.1016/j.mayocp.2025.11.008","title":"Building Artificial Intelligence and Data Literacy Across the Health Care Research Workforce in the Mayo Clinic Center for Clinical and Translational Science: Why It Matters and What We Are Doing About It.","authorString":"Zand J, Suvakov M, Overgaard SM, Kozikowski CG, Jimison RC, Nelson KB, Silvano CJ, Meiners LM, Fan JW, Schultz KE, Carter RE, Wieland ML, Patten CA, Dugani SB, Weis JA, Poe JD, Holmes DR, Griffiths LG, Dieter HL, Juhn YJ, Enders FT, Callstrom MR, Shah V, Meyer FB, Garovic VD.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Zand J","firstName":"Jaleh","lastName":"Zand","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Nephrology and Hypertension, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Suvakov M","firstName":"Milovan","lastName":"Suvakov","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Quantitative Health Sciences, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Overgaard SM","firstName":"Shauna M","lastName":"Overgaard","initials":"SM","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4494-0008"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Center for Digital Health, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Kozikowski CG","firstName":"Chester G","lastName":"Kozikowski","initials":"CG","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Center for Clinical and Translational Science, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Jimison RC","firstName":"Ryan C","lastName":"Jimison","initials":"RC","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0006-1931-7018"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Center for Clinical and Translational Science, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Nelson KB","firstName":"Kristina B","lastName":"Nelson","initials":"KB","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0004-7045-6414"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Center for Clinical and Translational Science, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Silvano CJ","firstName":"Carmen J","lastName":"Silvano","initials":"CJ","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Center for Clinical and Translational Science, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Meiners LM","firstName":"Laura M","lastName":"Meiners","initials":"LM","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7308-1812"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Center for Clinical and Translational Science, Mayo Clinic, Rochester,