]}},{"fullName":"Meyer FB","firstName":"Fred B","lastName":"Meyer","initials":"FB","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Neurologic Surgery, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Garovic VD","firstName":"Vesna D","lastName":"Garovic","initials":"VD","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Nephrology and Hypertension, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN; Center for Clinical and Translational Science, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN. Electronic address: garovic.vesna@mayo.edu."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4494-0008"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7308-1812"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0000-8507-8373"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0004-7045-6414"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0006-1931-7018"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"101","journalIssueId":4084538,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jan","monthOfPublication":1,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Mayo Clinic proceedings","medlineAbbreviation":"Mayo Clin Proc","isoabbreviation":"Mayo Clin Proc","nlmid":"0405543","essn":"1942-5546","issn":"0025-6196"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"17-25","affiliation":"Division of Nephrology and Hypertension, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"National Center for Advancing Translational Sciences","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"K23 MD016230","agency":"National Institute on Minority Health and Health Disparities","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"K23 MD016230","agency":"NIMHD NIH HHS","acronym":"MD","orderIn":0},{"agency":"National Institutes of Health","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"UL1 TR002377","agency":"NCATS NIH HHS","acronym":"TR","orderIn":0}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.mayocp.2025.11.008"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12965389"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12965389?pdf=render"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.mayocp.2025.11.008"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":1,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"Y","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"Y","manuscriptId":"NIHMS2147061","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-01-04","firstIndexDate":"2026-01-05","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-02","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","firstPublicationDate":"2026-01-01"},{"id":"42154550","source":"MED","pmid":"42154550","pmcid":"PMC13213961","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13213961"]},"doi":"10.1073/pnas.2535868123","title":"Persuading large language models to comply with objectionable requests.","authorString":"Meincke L, Shapiro D, Duckworth AL, Mollick E, Mollick L, Van den Bulte C, Cialdini R.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Meincke L","firstName":"Lennart","lastName":"Meincke","initials":"L","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0000-2260-011X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Generative AI Labs, The Wharton School, University of Pennsylvania, Philadelphia, PA 19104."},{"affiliation":"WHU-Otto Beisheim School of Management, Vallendar 56179, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Shapiro D","firstName":"Dan","lastName":"Shapiro","initials":"D","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Generative AI Labs, The Wharton School, University of Pennsylvania, Philadelphia, PA 19104."},{"affiliation":"Glowforge Inc, Seattle, WA 98134."}]}},{"fullName":"Duckworth AL","firstName":"Angela L","lastName":"Duckworth","initials":"AL","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2644-3729"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychology, University of Pennsylvania, Philadelphia, PA 19104."},{"affiliation":"Department of Operations, Information and Decisions, The Wharton School, University of Pennsylvania, Philadelphia, PA 19104."}]}},{"fullName":"Mollick E","firstName":"Ethan","lastName":"Mollick","initials":"E","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Generative AI Labs, The Wharton School, University of Pennsylvania, Philadelphia, PA 19104."},{"affiliation":"Department of Management, The Wharton School, University of Pennsylvania, Philadelphia, PA 19104."}]}},{"fullName":"Mollick L","firstName":"Lilach","lastName":"Mollick","initials":"L","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Generative AI Labs, The Wharton School, University of Pennsylvania, Philadelphia, PA 19104."}]}},{"fullName":"Van den Bulte C","firstName":"Christophe","lastName":"Van den Bulte","initials":"C","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9708-1596"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Marketing, The Wharton School, University of Pennsylvania, Philadelphia, PA 19104."}]}},{"fullName":"Cialdini R","firstName":"Robert","lastName":"Cialdini","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychology, Arizona State University, Tempe, AZ 85287."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9708-1596"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2644-3729"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0000-2260-011X"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","plain_english"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"21","volume":"123","journalIssueId":4192038,"dateOfPublication":"2026 May","monthOfPublication":5,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-05-01","journal":{"title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America","medlineAbbreviation":"Proc Natl Acad Sci U S A","isoabbreviation":"Proc Natl Acad Sci U S A","nlmid":"7505876","essn":"1091-6490","issn":"0027-8424"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e2535868123","abstractText":"Are large language models (LLMs) susceptible to the same persuasive appeals as humans? We tested whether classic persuasion principles (authority, commitment, liking, reciprocity, scarcity, social proof, and unity) could induce three widely used LLMs (GPT-5 mini, Claude Haiku 4.5, and Gemini 3 Flash) to comply with requests to assist with the synthesis of regulated substances. Across 126,000 conversations, persuasion principles increased compliance from 35.3% (at baseline) to 51.3% (using any principle). Although LLMs are not human, these findings underscore their parahuman (i.e., humanlike) nature and reveal the risk of manipulation by malicious users seeking to circumvent safety guardrails.","affiliation":"Generative AI Labs, The Wharton School, University of Pennsylvania, Philadelphia, PA 19104.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Persuasive Communication"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Generative Artificial Intelligence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Large Language Models"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Social Influence","Persuasion","Prompt Engineering","Large Language Models","Ai Compliance"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2535868123"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13213961"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13213961?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":1,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by-nc-nd","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-07-16","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-19","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-19","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-02","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-05-19","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-19"},{"id":"PPR1112674","source":"PPR","doi":"10.1101/2025.10.30.25339143","title":"Staffable ICU early-warning with calibrated multi-centre validation and alert-budget guardrails","authorString":"Zafar SA, Qin W, Soomro TA, Khan AA, Faisal MS.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Zafar SA","firstName":"Sanwal Ahmad","lastName":"Zafar","initials":"SA"},{"fullName":"Qin W","firstName":"Wei","lastName":"Qin","initials":"W"},{"fullName":"Soomro TA","firstName":"Toufique Ahmed","lastName":"Soomro","initials":"TA"},{"fullName":"Khan AA","firstName":"Areeba Ali","lastName":"Khan","initials":"AA"},{"fullName":"Faisal MS","firstName":"Muhammad Salman","lastName":"Faisal","initials":"MS"}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0006-4506-4560"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics"]},"pubYear":"2025","abstractText":"We present a staffable ICU early-warning system with calibrated multi-centre validation and alert-budget guardrails. A multi-horizon mortality model was developed on a large public ICU database and externally validated across independent hospitals at fixed post-admission landmarks (6/12/18/24 h). Evaluation was prespecified to emphasize actionability: discrimination, calibration (slope/intercept, Brier, ECE), decision-curve net benefit, and operating-point performance. To make deployment resource-aware, we translate threshold choice into  alerts per 100 admissions and map alerts to  clinician review time and  net monetary benefit (NMB), yielding guardrails that bound workload while preserving utility. We further provide a  silent-trial readout that estimates lead time prior to activation, supporting governance and cautious piloting. Across centres and horizons, the model maintained strong discrimination, tight calibration, and positive net benefit at clinically relevant thresholds. Guardrail selection produced  workload-constrained operating points with stable subgroup performance. By coupling calibrated multicentre validation with alert-budget guardrails and deployment-proximal readouts, this study reframes ICU early-warning from maximizing AUC to delivering a  staffable, value-aware program ready for monitored roll-out and periodic recalibration.","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Preprint"]},"bookOrReportDetails":{"publisher":"medRxiv","yearOfPublication":2025},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1101/2025.10.30.25339143"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"N","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by-nc","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2025-11-04","firstIndexDate":"2025-11-04","firstPublicationDate":"2025-11-02"},{"id":"41685095","source":"MED","pmid":"41685095","pmcid":"PMC12892354","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12892354"]},"doi":"10.1093/nsr/nwag040","title":"The enchanted loom of artificial general intelligence.","authorString":"Poo MM.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Poo MM","firstName":"Mu-Ming","lastName":"Poo","initials":"MM","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Executive Editor-in-Chief of NSR."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"3","volume":"13","journalIssueId":4117466,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Feb","monthOfPublication":2,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-02-01","journal":{"title":"National science review","medlineAbbreviation":"Natl Sci Rev","isoabbreviation":"Natl Sci Rev","nlmid":"101633095","essn":"2053-714X","issn":"2095-5138"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"nwag040","abstractText":"With this enchanted loom in the horizon, serious deliberation of what AGI entails and how it could impact society is in order.","affiliation":"Executive Editor-in-Chief of NSR.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Editorial"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/nsr/nwag040"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12892354"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12892354?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"N","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-02-13","dateOfCreation":"2026-02-13","firstIndexDate":"2026-02-14","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-02-14","dateOfRevision":"2026-02-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-01-30","firstPublicationDate":"2026-01-30"},{"id":"41868097","source":"MED","pmid":"41868097","pmcid":"PMC13003343","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13003343"]},"doi":"10.3389/fsysb.2026.1804193","title":"Streamlining IRB review of AI human subjects research (AIHSR): the three-stage framework.","authorString":"Eto T, Miller H, Vidal D, Lifson M.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Eto T","firstName":"Tamiko","lastName":"Eto","initials":"T","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"TechInHSR, Madison, AL, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Miller H","firstName":"Heather","lastName":"Miller","initials":"H","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"North Star Review Board, Seattle, WA, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Vidal D","firstName":"David","lastName":"Vidal","initials":"D","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Lifson M","firstName":"Mark","lastName":"Lifson","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Mayo Clinic, Walla Walla, WA, United States."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7485-8606"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"6","journalIssueId":4133845,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Frontiers in systems biology","medlineAbbreviation":"Front Syst Biol","isoabbreviation":"Front Syst Biol","nlmid":"9918435075106676","essn":"2674-0702","issn":"2674-0702"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1804193","abstractText":"Oversight of Artificial Intelligence in Human Subjects Research (AI HSR) presents unique challenges. These challenges arise from both the non-linear and iterative nature of AI development, as well as from the way AI shifts risk from individual research subjects to larger populations affected by AI-driven decisions and data handling. Traditional Institutional Review Boards (IRBs) often struggle to keep pace with these changes, which can lead to gaps in risk assessment and delays in the review process. There is a growing need for transparent, repeatable methods to manage AI risk in healthcare. This paper introduces the Three-Stage Framework, a risk-based oversight model designed to align ethical and regulatory review with an AI project's stage of maturity and potential human impact. By aligning the level and timing of IRB review with the types of risks present at each stage of AI system development, the framework supports appropriate regulatory pathways and documents expectations while maintaining effective protection of human subjects. Through gradual, stage-appropriate documentation, the approach supports responsible and adaptive innovation while preparing AI systems for safe and ethical use across biomedical, social, behavioral, and educational domains. This approach prepares AI systems for safe and ethical use, accelerates compliant research, and helps IRBs and institutions maintain trustworthiness while protecting human subjects.","affiliation":"TechInHSR, Madison, AL, United States.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["other","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Research Ethics","Irb Review","Research Compliance","Human Research Protections","Ai Governance","Practical Frameworks"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fsysb.2026.1804193"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13003343"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13003343?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":1,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-03-23","dateOfCreation":"2026-03-23","firstIndexDate":"2026-03-23","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-03-22","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-03-06","firstPublicationDate":"2026-03-06"},{"id":"41728578","source":"MED","pmid":"41728578","pmcid":"PMC12920046","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12920046"]},"doi":"10.7759/cureus.101890","title":"Large Language Models in Radiologist-Patient Communication: A Narrative Review for Clinical Practice.","authorString":"Naidu J, Muthyala H, Naidu SS, Muralidharan S, Baskaradoss VK.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Naidu J","firstName":"Jatin","lastName":"Naidu","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Radiology, Division of Surgery and Interventional Science, University College London, London, GBR."},{"affiliation":"Radiology, Lister Hospital, East and North Hertforshire NHS Trust, Stevanage, GBR."}]}},{"fullName":"Muthyala H","firstName":"Hitesh","lastName":"Muthyala","initials":"H","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Radiology, University College London Medical School, London, GBR."}]}},{"fullName":"Naidu SS","firstName":"Sonia S","lastName":"Naidu","initials":"SS","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology, University College London Medical School, London, GBR."}]}},{"fullName":"Muralidharan S","firstName":"Sandeep","lastName":"Muralidharan","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Computer Science, University of Hull, Hull, GBR."}]}},{"fullName":"Baskaradoss VK","firstName":"Vasanth K","lastName":"Baskaradoss","initials":"VK","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Radiology, Kettering General Hospital NHS Foundation Trust, Kettering, GBR."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9849-1976"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"18","journalIssueId":4084352,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jan","monthOfPublication":1,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Cureus","medlineAbbreviation":"Cureus","isoabbreviation":"Cureus","nlmid":"101596737","essn":"2168-8184","issn":"2168-8184"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e101890","abstractText":"Large language models (LLMs) are used in radiology to simplify reports, translate findings, and support patient-facing communication, yet their clinical value and safety remain uncertain. This narrative review was conducted in accordance with the Scale for the Assessment of Narrative Review Articles (SANRA) quality criteria and synthesises evidence from 49 studies published between 2020 and 2025, focusing on clinician-mediated use of LLMs across four domains: report simplification, multilingual translation, patient education, and patient attitudes. Across studies, LLMs consistently improved readability by 2-6 grade levels, but only one randomised trial directly assessed patient comprehension. A professional review was required in up to 80% of outputs in controlled settings, compared with <10% in observational studies. Harmful factual errors were uncommon but non-negligible (0-10% depending on task and model). Translation performance was highest for high-resource languages, while semantic drift was more frequent in low-resource languages, necessitating bilingual review. Patients generally accepted AI-assisted communication when clinician oversight was explicit. Current regulatory and professional guidance support supervised, institution-hosted deployment. Evidence supports specific use cases, patient summaries, translation drafts, and educational materials, but does not justify autonomous deployment or direct patient self-use. Key evidence gaps remain in comprehension outcomes, workflow impact, and real-world validation.","affiliation":"Radiology, Division of Surgery and Interventional Science, University College London, London, GBR.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Radiology","Governance","Patient Communication","Large Language Models"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.7759/cureus.101890"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12920046"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12920046?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-02-23","dateOfCreation":"2026-02-23","firstIndexDate":"2026-02-23","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-02-21","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-01-20","firstPublicationDate":"2026-01-20"},{"id":"41974554","source":"MED","pmid":"41974554","pmcid":"PMC13154028","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13154028"]},"doi":"10.14444/8876","title":"Response to Cruz-Almeida: Accountability and Operationalizable Deliverables in the NIH HEAL Initiative (Re: \"Billions Spent, Few Saved…\").","authorString":"Lewandrowski KU, Blum K, Lorio MP.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Lewandrowski KU","firstName":"Kai-Uwe","lastName":"Lewandrowski","initials":"KU","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Center For Advanced Spine Care of Southern Arizona, Tucson, AZ, USA business@tucsonspine.com."}]}},{"fullName":"Blum K","firstName":"Kenneth","lastName":"Blum","initials":"K","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Case Western Reserve University, School of Medicine, Cleveland, OH, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Lorio MP","firstName":"Morgan P","lastName":"Lorio","initials":"MP","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Orlando College of Osteopathic Medicine, Winter Garden, FL, USA."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"2","volume":"20","journalIssueId":4189334,"dateOfPublication":"2026 May","monthOfPublication":5,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-05-01","journal":{"title":"International journal of spine surgery","medlineAbbreviation":"Int J Spine Surg","isoabbreviation":"Int J Spine Surg","nlmid":"101579005","essn":"2211-4599","issn":"2211-4599"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"220-221","affiliation":"Center For Advanced Spine Care of Southern Arizona, Tucson, AZ, USA business@tucsonspine.com.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["letter","Journal Article"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.14444/8876"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13154028"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13154028?pdf=render"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.14444/8876"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-05-04","dateOfCreation":"2026-04-13","firstIndexDate":"2026-04-14","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-20","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-05-04","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-04"},{"id":"42194128","source":"MED","pmid":"42194128","pmcid":"PMC13204476","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13204476"]},"doi":"10.3390/children13050602","title":"Bridging Data, Semantics, and Clinical Reasoning: A Knowledge Graph Framework for Pediatric Obstructive Sleep Apnea.","authorString":"Geyer JD, Gong J, Cox PG, Henderson-Mitchell RJ, Gomez CR, Qureshi AI, Branch SG, Geisser SR, Carney PR.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Geyer JD","firstName":"James D","lastName":"Geyer","initials":"JD","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Institute for Rural Health Research, University of Alabama, Tuscaloosa, AL 35487, USA."},{"affiliation":"University Medical Center Neurology and Sleep Medicine, University of Alabama, Tuscaloosa, AL 35487, USA."},{"affiliation":"Alabama Center for the Advancement of AI, Department of Computer Science, University of Alabama, Tuscaloosa, AL 35487, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Gong J","firstName":"Jiaqi","lastName":"Gong","initials":"J","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9694-2518"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Alabama Center for the Advancement of AI, Department of Computer Science, University of Alabama, Tuscaloosa, AL 35487, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Cox PG","firstName":"Paul G","lastName":"Cox","initials":"PG","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Evimero, LLC, Tuscaloosa, AL 35406, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Henderson-Mitchell RJ","firstName":"Randi J","lastName":"Henderson-Mitchell","initials":"RJ","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0003-2641-4279"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Institute for Rural Health Research, University of Alabama, Tuscaloosa, AL 35487, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Gomez CR","firstName":"Camilo R","lastName":"Gomez","initials":"CR","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Neurology, University of Missouri School of Medicine, Columbia, MO 65212, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Qureshi AI","firstName":"Adnan I","lastName":"Qureshi","initials":"AI","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Neurology, University of Missouri School of Medicine, Columbia, MO 65212, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Branch SG","firstName":"Shelby G","lastName":"Branch","initials":"SG","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Institute for Rural Health Research, University of Alabama, Tuscaloosa, AL 35487, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Geisser SR","firstName":"Sophia R","lastName":"Geisser","initials":"SR","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0008-1229-5156"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychology, University of Alabama, Tuscaloosa, AL 35487, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Carney PR","firstName":"Paul R","lastName":"Carney","initials":"PR","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6248-1493"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Sunstone Health, Boston, MA 02101, USA."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6248-1493"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9694-2518"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0003-2641-4279"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0008-1229-5156"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"5","volume":"13","journalIssueId":4201868,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Apr","monthOfPublication":4,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-04-01","journal":{"title":"Children (Basel, Switzerland)","medlineAbbreviation":"Children (Basel)","isoabbreviation":"Children (Basel)","nlmid":"101648936","essn":"2227-9067","issn":"2227-9067"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"602","abstractText":"<h4>Background/objectives</h4>Pediatric obstructive sleep apnea (OSA) is a complex disorder with a variable presentation and often challenging diagnostic testing. The history and physical examination in pediatric OSA frequently differ from those in adults. The treatment options are multifaceted and must be tailored to the individual patient. Artificial intelligence (AI) modalities currently employed in pediatric sleep medicine face several important limitations: modality fragmentation, lack of explainability, and limited semantic integration.<h4>Method</h4>Our team proposes a new vision for AI and pediatric sleep medicine. This platform is based on a knowledge graph (KG) framework integrating structured and unstructured data to enable reasoning, personalization, and clinical decision support.<h4>Results</h4>This framework represents a conceptual architecture; it has not yet been empirically implemented, and the use cases described herein are illustrative of its intended capabilities. Components of the infrastructure developed for similar applications have been successfully implemented. The quantitative feasibility pilot KG represented 100% multimodal data with >90% semantic completeness.<h4>Conclusions</h4>Fully realized and deployed into the clinical space, this pediatric OSA KG system will enhance tertiary care programs and help project tertiary-level pediatric care into underserved regions.","affiliation":"Institute for Rural Health Research, University of Alabama, Tuscaloosa, AL 35487, USA.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Pediatric Obstructive Sleep Apnea","Knowledge Graph"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/children13050602"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13204476"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13204476?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-05-27","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-27","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-28","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-27","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-04-27","firstPublicationDate":"2026-04-27"},{"id":"41667212","source":"MED","pmid":"41667212","pmcid":"PMC12911724","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12911724"]},"doi":"10.1136/bmjhci-2025-101935","title":"Mechanistic interpretability of reinforcement learning in Medicaid care coordination.","authorString":"Basu S, Patel S, Sheth P, Muralidharan B, Elamaran N, Kinra A, Batniji R.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Basu S","firstName":"Sanjay","lastName":"Basu","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0599-6332"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Waymark, San Francisco, California, USA sanjay.basu@waymarkcare.com."}]}},{"fullName":"Patel S","firstName":"Sadiq","lastName":"Patel","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Waymark, San Francisco, California, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Sheth P","firstName":"Parth","lastName":"Sheth","initials":"P","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Waymark, San Francisco, California, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Muralidharan B","firstName":"Bhairavi","lastName":"Muralidharan","initials":"B","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Waymark, San Francisco, California, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Elamaran N","firstName":"Namrata","lastName":"Elamaran","initials":"N","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Waymark, San Francisco, California, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Kinra A","firstName":"Aakriti","lastName":"Kinra","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Waymark, San Francisco, California, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Batniji R","firstName":"Rajaie","lastName":"Batniji","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Waymark, San Francisco, California, USA."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0599-6332"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"33","journalIssueId":4112695,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Feb","monthOfPublication":2,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-02-01","journal":{"title":"BMJ health & care informatics","medlineAbbreviation":"BMJ Health Care Inform","isoabbreviation":"BMJ Health Care Inform","nlmid":"101745500","essn":"2632-1009","issn":"2632-1009"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e101935","abstractText":"<h4>Objective</h4>To expose reasoning pathways of a reinforcement learning policy for Medicaid care coordination, develop an error taxonomy and implement fairness-aware guardrails.<h4>Design</h4>Retrospective interpretability audit using attention analysis, Shapley explanations, sparse autoencoder feature discovery and blinded clinician adjudication.<h4>Setting</h4>Medicaid care coordination programmes in Washington, Virginia and Ohio (July 2023-June 2025).<h4>Participants</h4>250 000 intervention decisions; 200 divergent cases reviewed by five clinicians.<h4>Main outcome measures</h4>Calibrated harm prediction; algorithmic clearance and residual harm rates; error taxonomy frequencies; subgroup fairness metrics.<h4>Results</h4>The conformal model achieved area under the receiver operating characteristic curve of 0.80 (95% CI 0.78 to 0.82), clearing 89.5% (95% CI 88.9% to 90.1%) of decisions with 1.22% (95% CI 1.14% to 1.30%) residual harm versus 6.67% (95% CI 6.02% to 7.32%) for flagged decisions. Sparse autoencoders identified seven reasoning motifs linking social determinants to clinical cascades. The error taxonomy revealed premise errors (48%, 95% CI 41% to 55%), calibration failures (27%, 95% CI 21% to 33%) and contextual blind spots (25%, 95% CI 19% to 31%). Divergence was higher for telehealth visits (11.2%) and behavioural health patients (10.7% vs 6.9%, p<0.001). Fairness optimisation reduced race-group disparity by 37% (95% CI 22% to 48%) and sex-group disparity by 28% (95% CI 14% to 39%). Reviewers rated 23% (95% CI 17% to 29%) of overridden recommendations as well-matched, confirming appropriate human oversight.<h4>Conclusions</h4>Mechanistic interpretability transforms opaque algorithmic assistance into auditable decision support, providing a governance scaffold for clinical artificial intelligence deployment.","affiliation":"Waymark, San Francisco, California, USA sanjay.basu@waymarkcare.com.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Retrospective Studies"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Algorithms"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Medicaid","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"OG","qualifierName":"organization & administration","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"United States"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Ohio"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Virginia"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Reinforcement Machine Learning"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Autoencoder"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Decision Making, Computer-assisted","Bmj Health Informatics"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1136/bmjhci-2025-101935"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12911724"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12911724?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by-nc","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-07-07","dateOfCreation":"2026-02-10","firstIndexDate":"2026-02-11","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-02-18","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-02-10","firstPublicationDate":"2026-02-10"},{"id":"42001638","source":"MED","pmid":"42001638","doi":"10.1016/j.jflm.2026.103137","title":"Digital twin technology in forensic mental health.","authorString":"Olawade JO, Ebo TO, Alabi JO, Makanjuola BD, Egbon E, Olawade DB.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Olawade JO","firstName":"James O","lastName":"Olawade","initials":"JO","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Guidance and Counselling, Adekunle Ajasin University, Akungba-Akoko, Nigeria."}]}},{"fullName":"Ebo TO","firstName":"Titus Oloruntoba","lastName":"Ebo","initials":"TO","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8104-4050"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Forensic Mental Health Unit, Nottinghamshire Healthcare NHS Foundation Trust, United Kingdom."}]}},{"fullName":"Alabi JO","firstName":"John Oluwatosin","lastName":"Alabi","initials":"JO","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Business and Management, University of Sussex Business School, University of Sussex, Falmer, Brighton, BN1 9RH, United Kingdom."}]}},{"fullName":"Makanjuola BD","firstName":"Babajide David","lastName":"Makanjuola","initials":"BD","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Sheffield Business School, Department of Accounting, Banking and Finance, Sheffield Hallam University, Howard St, Sheffield City Centre, Sheffield, S1 1WB, United Kingdom."}]}},{"fullName":"Egbon E","firstName":"Eghosasere","lastName":"Egbon","initials":"E","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Tissue Engineering and Regenerative Medicine, Faculty of Life Science Engineering, FH Technikum, Vienna, Austria."}]}},{"fullName":"Olawade DB","firstName":"David B","lastName":"Olawade","initials":"DB","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Allied and Public Health, School of Health, Sport and Bioscience, University of East London, London, United Kingdom; Department of Research and Innovation, Medway NHS Foundation Trust, Gillingham, ME7 5NY, United Kingdom; Department of Public Health, York St John University, London, United Kingdom. Electronic address: d.olawade@uel.ac.uk."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8104-4050"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0188-9836"}]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"120","journalIssueId":4191567,"dateOfPublication":"2026 May","monthOfPublication":5,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-05-01","journal":{"title":"Journal of forensic and legal medicine","medlineAbbreviation":"J Forensic Leg Med","isoabbreviation":"J Forensic Leg Med","nlmid":"101300022","essn":"1878-7487","issn":"1752-928X"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"103137","abstractText":"Forensic mental health services face significant challenges in managing violence and self-harm risks, optimizing therapeutic security, and planning pathways for individuals with serious mental disorders within criminal justice systems. Traditional risk assessment tools provide static snapshots that degrade over time and offer limited personalization. Digital twin technology, which creates dynamic, data-driven computational replicas of real-world entities, presents a transformative opportunity to enhance decision-making in this complex field. This narrative review synthesizes emerging concepts, opportunities, and risks surrounding the use of digital twin technology in forensic mental health, examining how this innovation could augment clinical practice while addressing critical ethical and legal considerations. We conducted a narrative review of recent literature on digital twins in healthcare, digital psychiatry, risk management in forensic mental health, and related ethical frameworks, synthesizing findings from peer-reviewed journals, consensus statements, and policy documents to map plausible applications, technical constraints, and governance requirements specific to forensic mental health contexts. Digital twins could enhance violence and self-harm risk management through continuous updating, personalize care pathways across prisons, courts, and secure hospitals, optimize ward staffing and security protocols, and support rights-respecting care planning. However, deployment requires robust attention to data provenance, algorithmic fairness, transparency, clinical validity, and human rights safeguards. We identify a staged translational pathway with essential guardrails for safe implementation. While digital twin technology holds considerable promise for forensic mental health, realizing these potential demands rigorous validation, strong governance frameworks, and sustained co-design with service users, clinicians, and legal stakeholders to ensure safety and rights protection.","affiliation":"Department of Guidance and Counselling, Adekunle Ajasin University, Akungba-Akoko, Nigeria.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Review","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Forensic Psychiatry"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Mental Health Services"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Violence","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"PC","qualifierName":"prevention & control","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Risk Management"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Digital Health"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["risk assessment","Forensic Mental Health","Digital Phenotyping","Precision Psychiatry","Digital Twins"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jflm.2026.103137"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-07-15","dateOfCreation":"2026-04-19","firstIndexDate":"2026-04-20","dateOfRevision":"2026-07-15","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-04-16","firstPublicationDate":"2026-04-16"},{"id":"41897098","source":"MED","pmid":"41897098","pmcid":"PMC13024890","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13024890"]},"doi":"10.3390/children13030386","title":"Equity or Two-Tier Care? Guardrails for Silver Diamine Fluoride and Delegated Early Childhood Caries Pathways.","authorString":"Baghdadi ZD.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Baghdadi ZD","firstName":"Ziad D","lastName":"Baghdadi","initials":"ZD","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0071-9141"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Centre for Community Oral Health, Dr. Gerald Niznick College of Dentistry, University of Manitoba, Winnipeg, MB R3B 0L8, Canada."},{"affiliation":"Department of Preventive Dental Sciences, Division of Pediatric Dentistry, Dr. Gerald Niznick College of Dentistry, University of Manitoba, Winnipeg, MB R3E 0W2, Canada."},{"affiliation":"TopSmiles Pediatric Dentistry and Orthodontics, Winnipeg, MB R2M 3A4, Canada."},{"affiliation":"Butterfly Dental Group, Winnipeg, MB R3Y 1P5, Canada."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0071-9141"}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"3","volume":"13","journalIssueId":4161731,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Mar","monthOfPublication":3,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-03-01","journal":{"title":"Children (Basel, Switzerland)","medlineAbbreviation":"Children (Basel)","isoabbreviation":"Children (Basel)","nlmid":"101648936","essn":"2227-9067","issn":"2227-9067"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"386","abstractText":"Early childhood caries (ECC) is a complex, multifactorial disease shaped by biofilm ecology, host susceptibility, diet and behaviors, and structural determinants of health. Silver diamine fluoride (SDF) is an effective non-restorative option for arresting cavitated lesions in many settings and can support access when definitive care is delayed. However, translating short-horizon \"arrest\" outcomes into broad policy claims-that SDF-first, delegated pathways can substitute for dentist-led diagnosis and comprehensive rehabilitation-risks institutionalizing a two-tier standard of care for children facing the greatest access barriers. This perspective critically appraises evidence-to-implementation pathways for SDF and delegated ECC management, using risk-of-bias and reporting guidance as interpretive tools and drawing on pragmatic regimen trials, microbiome substudies, oral health-related quality of life (OHRQoL) analyses, and implementation work including the Canadian Caries Risk Assessment Tool (CCRAT) in primary care. We explicitly distinguish what studies demonstrate (e.g., feasibility and short-term arrest differences by reapplication interval) from what they do not establish (e.g., long-term tooth survival, pulpal outcomes, definitive treatment completion, and equity impacts). We propose practical guardrails that position SDF as <b>interim management within a continuum of care</b>: dentist-led diagnosis and escalation when pulpal risk is suspected; time-bound referral pathways with completion tracking; protocolized follow-up aligned with lesion/risk status; outcome sets that extend beyond \"arrest\" to include pain, function, OHRQoL, tooth survival, and equity stratification; and lesion-site sampling plus preregistered analyses when mechanistic claims are advanced.","affiliation":"Centre for Community Oral Health, Dr. Gerald Niznick College of Dentistry, University of Manitoba, Winnipeg, MB R3B 0L8, Canada.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["other","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Early Childhood Caries","Pediatric Dentistry","Silver Diamine Fluoride","Implementation Science","Evidence Translation","Health Equity","Caries Risk Assessment","Dental Home","Oral Health–related Quality Of Life","Delegated Care"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/children13030386"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13024890"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13024890?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":3,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-03-28","dateOfCreation":"2026-03-28","firstIndexDate":"2026-03-29","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-03-28","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-03-10","firstPublicationDate":"2026-03-10"},{"id":"41976876","source":"MED","pmid":"41976876","pmcid":"PMC13073393","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13073393"]},"doi":"10.3390/jcm15072576","title":"Clinical Artificial Intelligence Agents in Nephrology: From Prediction to Action Through Workflow-Native Intelligence-A Roadmap for Workflow-Integrated Care.","authorString":"Thongprayoon C, Pesce F, Cheungpasitporn W.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Thongprayoon C","firstName":"Charat","lastName":"Thongprayoon","initials":"C","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Nephrology and Hypertension, Department of Medicine, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN 55905, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Pesce F","firstName":"Francesco","lastName":"Pesce","initials":"F","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2882-4226"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Renal Medicine, Ospedale Isola Tiberina-Gemelli Isola, 00168 Rome, Italy."},{"affiliation":"Department of Translational Medicine and Surgery, Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore, 00168 Rome, Italy."}]}},{"fullName":"Cheungpasitporn W","firstName":"Wisit","lastName":"Cheungpasitporn","initials":"W","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9954-9711"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Nephrology and Hypertension, Department of Medicine, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN 55905, USA."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9954-9711"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2882-4226"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"7","volume":"15","journalIssueId":4179667,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Mar","monthOfPublication":3,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-03-01","journal":{"title":"Journal of clinical medicine","medlineAbbreviation":"J Clin Med","isoabbreviation":"J Clin Med","nlmid":"101606588","essn":"2077-0383","issn":"2077-0383"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"2576","abstractText":"<b>Background</b>: Artificial intelligence in nephrology has largely focused on predictive models for outcomes such as acute kidney injury (AKI), chronic kidney disease (CKD) progression, and transplant complications. Although these models demonstrate technical performance, their real-world clinical impact has remained limited because prediction alone rarely translates into coordinated clinical action. Clinical artificial intelligence agents represent workflow-native systems that operate in real time, interact bidirectionally with clinical environments, adapt to evolving patient and workflow states, and support coordinated clinical action rather than generating isolated predictions. This review proposes clinical artificial intelligence agents as a new paradigm for integrating artificial intelligence directly into nephrology workflows. <b>Methods</b>: We conducted a narrative synthesis of emerging literature on artificial intelligence systems, agentic artificial intelligence architectures, clinical decision support, and digital health infrastructures relevant to kidney care. Drawing from interdisciplinary sources in medicine, health informatics, and artificial intelligence research, we developed a conceptual framework describing the architecture, governance requirements, and evaluation principles of clinical artificial intelligence agents in nephrology. <b>Results</b>: Clinical artificial intelligence agents represent workflow-integrated systems capable of continuously perceiving patient data, reasoning under clinical constraints, planning tasks, and supporting coordinated clinical actions over time. We describe a layered architecture consisting of perception, cognition, planning and control, action, and learning components. Potential applications span the nephrology care continuum, including CKD management, AKI monitoring, dialysis and continuous renal replacement therapy (CRRT) optimization, kidney transplantation care coordination, glomerulonephritis management, and supervised patient-facing systems. <b>Conclusions</b>: Clinical artificial intelligence agents shift the role of artificial intelligence from isolated prediction toward longitudinal clinical orchestration. Future evaluation should prioritize workflow integration, time-to-action, clinician oversight, safety, and patient-centered outcomes rather than relying solely on traditional model performance metrics. This roadmap provides a conceptual foundation for the responsible development and clinical integration of agentic artificial intelligence systems in nephrology.","affiliation":"Division of Nephrology and Hypertension, Department of Medicine, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN 55905, USA.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Nephrology","Clinical Workflow Integration","Clinical Artificial Intelligence Agents","Conceptual Roadmap"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/jcm15072576"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13073393"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13073393?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":1,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-04-14","dateOfCreation":"2026-04-14","firstIndexDate":"2026-04-14","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-04-20","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-03-27","firstPublicationDate":"2026-03-27"},{"id":"41543479","source":"MED","pmid":"41543479","doi":"10.1097/scs.0000000000012430","title":"Ethical and Regulatory Considerations for Artificial Intelligence Adoption in Craniofacial Surgery.","authorString":"Ozmen BB, Taub PJ.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Ozmen BB","firstName":"Berk B","lastName":"Ozmen","initials":"BB","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7803-1868"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Plastic Surgery, Cleveland Clinic, Cleveland, OH."}]}},{"fullName":"Taub PJ","firstName":"Peter J","lastName":"Taub","initials":"PJ","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Plastic and Maxillofacial Surgery, Icahn School of Medicine at Mount Sinai, New York, NY."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7803-1868"}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"3-4","volume":"37","journalIssueId":4137791,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Mar-Apr 01","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-03-01","journal":{"title":"The Journal of craniofacial surgery","medlineAbbreviation":"J Craniofac Surg","isoabbreviation":"J Craniofac Surg","nlmid":"9010410","essn":"1536-3732","issn":"1049-2275"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"862-867","abstractText":"Artificial intelligence (AI) continues to enhance surgical care, with growing applications in craniofacial surgery, including automated cephalometric analysis, diagnostics for craniosynostosis, virtual surgical planning, perioperative decision support, and prediction of postoperative outcomes. As these technologies transition from experimental systems to clinical tools that influence diagnosis, planning, and counseling, the specialty faces increasingly complex ethical and regulatory challenges. In this manuscript, the authors review and synthesize the ethical principles guiding the responsible adoption of AI in craniofacial surgery, including informed consent in predominantly pediatric populations, the risks of algorithmic bias, the heightened privacy concerns associated with facial biometrics, the requirements for transparency and explainability, and the evolving concepts of clinician accountability in AI-augmented decision-making. The authors also examine the global regulatory landscape, focusing on the US FDA's framework for AI/ML-based software as a medical device (SaMD), the European Union's medical device and AI regulations, and international oversight models. The implications of continuous-learning systems for real-world performance monitoring and postmarket governance are discussed in detail. Finally, the authors propose a pragmatic implementation framework emphasizing governance structures, pilot evaluation, structured local validation, performance auditing, and equity-centered deployment. Establishing rigorous ethical guardrails, robust regulatory pathways, and specialty-specific governance structures will be essential for realizing AI's potential while safeguarding patient trust, safety, and equity in craniofacial care.","affiliation":"Department of Plastic Surgery, Cleveland Clinic, Cleveland, OH.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Review","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Craniofacial Abnormalities","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"SU","qualifierName":"surgery","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Informed Consent","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"LJ","qualifierName":"legislation & jurisprudence","majorTopic_YN":"N"},{"abbreviation":"ES","qualifierName":"ethics","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Artificial Intelligence","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"LJ","qualifierName":"legislation & jurisprudence","majorTopic_YN":"N"},{"abbreviation":"ES","qualifierName":"ethics","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"United States"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Regulations","Policy","Craniofacial Surgery","Medial Ethics"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1097/SCS.0000000000012430"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":2,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-03-06","dateOfCreation":"2026-01-16","firstIndexDate":"2026-01-16","dateOfRevision":"2026-06-26","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-01-16","firstPublicationDate":"2026-01-16"},{"id":"42123213","source":"MED","pmid":"42123213","pmcid":"PMC13163497","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13163497"]},"doi":"10.3390/jcm15093477","title":"Artificial Intelligence to Facilitate SEP-1 Measure Compliance and Fluid Management in Sepsis.","authorString":"Nguyen HB, Krishtopaytis E, Lopez E, Farnoudi N, Van T, Kharalampova V, Coz Yataco A.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Nguyen HB","firstName":"H Bryant","lastName":"Nguyen","initials":"HB","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Pulmonary, Critical Care, Hyperbaric, and Sleep Medicine, Loma Linda University, Loma Linda, CA 92354, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Krishtopaytis E","firstName":"Eduard","lastName":"Krishtopaytis","initials":"E","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Pulmonary, Critical Care, Hyperbaric, and Sleep Medicine, Loma Linda University, Loma Linda, CA 92354, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Lopez E","firstName":"Enrique","lastName":"Lopez","initials":"E","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medicine, Loma Linda University, Loma Linda, CA 92354, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Farnoudi N","firstName":"Neeka","lastName":"Farnoudi","initials":"N","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medicine, Loma Linda University, Loma Linda, CA 92354, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Van T","firstName":"Trinity","lastName":"Van","initials":"T","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Emergency Medicine, Loma Linda University, Loma Linda, CA 92354, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Kharalampova V","firstName":"Viktoriia","lastName":"Kharalampova","initials":"V","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Pulmonary, Critical Care, Hyperbaric, and Sleep Medicine, Loma Linda University, Loma Linda, CA 92354, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Coz Yataco A","firstName":"Angel","lastName":"Coz Yataco","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Critical Care, Respiratory Institute, Cleveland Clinic, Cleveland, OH 44195, USA."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"9","volume":"15","journalIssueId":4193391,"dateOfPublication":"2026 May","monthOfPublication":5,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-05-01","journal":{"title":"Journal of clinical medicine","medlineAbbreviation":"J Clin Med","isoabbreviation":"J Clin Med","nlmid":"101606588","essn":"2077-0383","issn":"2077-0383"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"3477","abstractText":"Sepsis remains a leading cause of preventable morbidity and mortality worldwide, and adherence to the Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services Severe Sepsis and Septic Shock Early Management Bundle (SEP-1) remains modest and variable across institutions. Simultaneously, controversy persists regarding fixed-volume fluid resuscitation mandates, particularly given the increasing emphasis on individualized, physiology-guided management. Artificial intelligence (AI) has emerged as a potential strategy to address both operational and clinical gaps in sepsis care. This review examines the current state of SEP-1 implementation, key barriers to compliance, and ongoing debates surrounding early fluid administration. We then discuss contemporary evidence on AI-enabled tools designed to accelerate bundle processes and support personalized fluid management. Early warning systems, natural language processing-augmented models, and telemedicine-integrated platforms have demonstrated improvements in process measures such as time-to-antibiotics and bundle component completion when embedded within defined clinical workflows. Reinforcement learning, causal machine learning, and predictive models offer promise for individualized fluid strategies, although most data remain retrospective and hypothesis-generating. Successful integration will require prospective validation, clinician-in-the-loop oversight, governance frameworks, and continuous monitoring for safety, equity, and model drift. AI should augment-rather than replace-clinical judgment to improve reliability, timeliness, and personalization in sepsis management.","affiliation":"Division of Pulmonary, Critical Care, Hyperbaric, and Sleep Medicine, Loma Linda University, Loma Linda, CA 92354, USA.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Neural network","Sepsis","Septic shock","Fluid resuscitation","Antibiotic Therapy","Machine Learning","Natural Language Processing","Sep-1 Measure Compliance","Bundle Performance"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/jcm15093477"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13163497"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13163497?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-05-13","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-13","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-15","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-20","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-05-01","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-01"},{"id":"41918774","source":"MED","pmid":"41918774","pmcid":"PMC13033711","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13033711"]},"doi":"10.3389/frai.2026.1729797","title":"Comparative analysis of strategic vs. computational thinking in management.","authorString":"Nyenno I.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Nyenno I","firstName":"Iryna","lastName":"Nyenno","initials":"I","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0762-808X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Management and Innovations, Faculty of Economics and Law, Odesa I.I. Mechnikov National University, Odesa, Ukraine."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0762-808X"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"9","journalIssueId":4117457,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Frontiers in artificial intelligence","medlineAbbreviation":"Front Artif Intell","isoabbreviation":"Front Artif Intell","nlmid":"101770551","essn":"2624-8212","issn":"2624-8212"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1729797","abstractText":"The integration of artificial intelligence (AI) into organisational processes is transforming the decision-making dynamics of managerial work. This study examines how AI reshapes managerial roles at the micro level by analysing the interaction between strategic and computational thinking across Mintzberg's ten managerial roles. Grounded in Peter Senge's Five Disciplines, the study explores how AI-enabled systems alter managerial routines, including monitoring, sense-making, resource allocation, coordination, and negotiation and how these changes influence human-algorithm decision architectures. A conceptual synthesis approach was used to integrate three theoretical perspectives: (1) Mintzberg's framework of managerial roles, (2) Senge's learning disciplines, and (3) contemporary models of computational thinking. Through comparative role mapping and cross-framework analysis, the study identifies how algorithmic logic augments, displaces, or reconfigures cognitive tasks within each managerial role. This synthesis informs the development of a hybrid strategic-computational framework for managerial decision-making in AI-rich environments. Findings indicate that AI adoption differentially affects managerial roles. Roles dependent on relational intelligence, ethical judgment, and influence (leader, liaison, figurehead, negotiator) remain anchored in strategic thinking, though increasingly augmented by predictive and diagnostic analytics. Roles focused on information processing, optimisation, and operational precision (monitor, disseminator, resource allocator) benefit substantially from computational thinking. Entrepreneurial and disturbance-handling roles emerge as hybrid decision zones, requiring managers to integrate AI-driven modelling, simulation, and anomaly detection with contextual interpretation, value-based trade-offs, and principled override decisions. Across roles, AI increases cognitive complexity and introduces new tensions between algorithmic optimisation and systemic, ethical reasoning. The study contributes to AI governance and managerial cognition research by showing how organisational design, regulatory constraints, and decision structures shape micro-level human-AI interaction patterns. For practitioners, including executives, AI steering committees, and governance councils, the proposed framework provides actionable guidance on delineating managerial responsibilities, establishing human-in-the-loop checkpoints, and designing escalation paths that safeguard accountability. The findings underscore the need for balanced upskilling in strategic systems thinking and computational reasoning to ensure responsible, transparent, and legitimate managerial decision-making in AI-enabled workplaces.","affiliation":"Department of Management and Innovations, Faculty of Economics and Law, Odesa I.I. Mechnikov National University, Odesa, Ukraine.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","decision-making","Strategic Thinking","Computational Thinking","Managers’ Roles"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/frai.2026.1729797"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13033711"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13033711?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-04-01","dateOfCreation":"2026-04-01","firstIndexDate":"2026-04-02","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-03-31","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-03-16","firstPublicationDate":"2026-03-16"},{"id":"41595680","source":"MED","pmid":"41595680","pmcid":"PMC12838638","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12838638"]},"doi":"10.3390/biomedicines14010146","title":"Artificial Intelligence and Machine Learning in Pediatric Endocrine Tumors: Opportunities, Pitfalls, and a Roadmap for Trustworthy Clinical Translation.","authorString":"Kuhlen M, Hellmann F, Pfaehler E, André E, Redlich A.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Kuhlen M","firstName":"Michaela","lastName":"Kuhlen","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4577-0503"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pediatrics, Pediatric Hematology/Oncology, Otto-von-Guericke-University, Leipziger Str. 44, D-39120 Magdeburg, Germany."},{"affiliation":"Paediatrics and Adolescent Medicine, Faculty of Medicine, University of Augsburg, Stenglinstr. 2, D-86156 Augsburg, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Hellmann F","firstName":"Fabio","lastName":"Hellmann","initials":"F","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6404-0827"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Human-Centered Artificial Intelligence, University of Augsburg, Universitaetsstrasse 6a, D-86159 Augsburg, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Pfaehler E","firstName":"Elisabeth","lastName":"Pfaehler","initials":"E","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6160-3011"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Institute for Neuroscience and Medicine 4 (INM-4), Forschungszentrum Jülich GmbH, Wilhlem-Johnen-Straße, D-52428 Jülich, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"André E","firstName":"Elisabeth","lastName":"André","initials":"E","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Human-Centered Artificial Intelligence, University of Augsburg, Universitaetsstrasse 6a, D-86159 Augsburg, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Redlich A","firstName":"Antje","lastName":"Redlich","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1732-1869"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pediatrics, Pediatric Hematology/Oncology, Otto-von-Guericke-University, Leipziger Str. 44, D-39120 Magdeburg, Germany."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6404-0827"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1732-1869"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6160-3011"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4577-0503"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"14","journalIssueId":4103291,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jan","monthOfPublication":1,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Biomedicines","medlineAbbreviation":"Biomedicines","isoabbreviation":"Biomedicines","nlmid":"101691304","essn":"2227-9059","issn":"2227-9059"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"146","abstractText":"Artificial intelligence (AI) and machine learning (ML) are reshaping cancer research and care. In pediatric oncology, early evidence-most robust in imaging-suggests value for diagnosis, risk stratification, and assessment of treatment response. Pediatric endocrine tumors are rare and heterogeneous, including intra- and extra-adrenal paraganglioma (PGL), adrenocortical tumors (ACT), differentiated and medullary thyroid carcinoma (DTC/MTC), and gastroenteropancreatic neuroendocrine neoplasms (GEP-NEN). Here, we provide a pediatric-first, entity-structured synthesis of AI/ML applications in endocrine tumors, paired with a methods-for-clinicians primer and a pediatric endocrine tumor guardrails checklist mapped to contemporary reporting/evaluation standards. We also outline a realistic EU-anchored roadmap for translation that leverages existing infrastructures (EXPeRT, ERN PaedCan). We find promising-yet preliminary-signals for early non-remission/recurrence modeling in pediatric DTC and interpretable survival prediction in pediatric ACT. For PGL and GEP-NEN, evidence remains adult-led (biochemical ML screening scores; CT/PET radiomics for metastatic risk or peptide receptor radionuclide therapy response) and serves primarily as methodological scaffolding for pediatrics. Cross-cutting insights include the centrality of calibration and validation hierarchy and the current limits of explainability (radiomics texture semantics; saliency ≠ mechanism). Translation is constrained by small datasets, domain shift across age groups and sites, limited external validation, and evolving regulatory expectations. We close with pragmatic, clinically anchored steps-benchmarks, multi-site pediatric validation, genotype-aware evaluation, and equity monitoring-to accelerate safe, equitable adoption in pediatric endocrine oncology.","affiliation":"Department of Pediatrics, Pediatric Hematology/Oncology, Otto-von-Guericke-University, Leipziger Str. 44, D-39120 Magdeburg, Germany.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"grantId":"DKS 2021.11","agency":"Deutsche Kinderkrebsstiftung","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"DKS 2021.11, 2024.16, 2025.07, and 2025.37","agency":"Deutsche Kinderkrebsstiftung","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Magdeburger Förderkreis krebskranker Kinder e.V.","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Medical Faculty, University of Augsburg","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Mitteldeutsche Kinderkrebsforschung","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"DKS 2025.07","agency":"Deutsche Kinderkrebsstiftung","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"DKS 2024.16","agency":"Deutsche Kinderkrebsstiftung","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"DKS 2025.16","agency":"Deutsche Kinderkrebsstiftung","orderIn":0}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Risk stratification","Machine Learning","Pediatric Oncology","Endocrine Tumors","Radiomics","Explainability","Ethical Ai","Techquity"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/biomedicines14010146"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12838638"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12838638?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":1,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-01-28","dateOfCreation":"2026-01-28","firstIndexDate":"2026-01-28","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-01-31","dateOfRevision":"2026-01-31","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-01-11","firstPublicationDate":"2026-01-11"},{"id":"42272816","source":"MED","pmid":"42272816","pmcid":"PMC13247570","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13247570"]},"doi":"10.1016/j.dib.2026.112839","title":"Building a single market for data: Data spaces as pro-competitive infrastructure.","authorString":"Konstantopoulou ES.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Konstantopoulou ES","firstName":"Effimia Styliani","lastName":"Konstantopoulou","initials":"ES","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"KU Leuven, Center for IT and IP Law, Leuven, Belgium."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"66","journalIssueId":4154312,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-06-01","journal":{"title":"Data in brief","medlineAbbreviation":"Data Brief","isoabbreviation":"Data Brief","nlmid":"101654995","essn":"2352-3409","issn":"2352-3409"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"112839","abstractText":"European data spaces constitute pro-competitive infrastructures deliberately designed to align with EU competition law objectives. Unlike proprietary ecosystems that risk entrenching market dominance, data spaces embed neutrality, openness, and non-discrimination into their architectural design, thereby addressing market contestability through governance frameworks rather than ex post regulatory intervention. The main argument advances that data spaces represent a transformative \"soft-market intervention\" that shifts competitive dynamics from exclusive data hoarding to innovation-based rivalry on shared datasets. The paper demonstrates how data spaces extend the principle of free movement to data as a potential \"fifth freedom\" within the EU internal market, while their federated governance structure prevents any single participant from monopolizing data access or determining participation terms. Through ex ante pro-competitive design, data spaces reconcile the need for large-scale data aggregation with competition law safeguards, enabling economies of scale and scope without creating dominance risks. The technical infrastructure emphasizes interoperability by design, federated cloud architecture, and open standards that prevent vendor lock-in and foreclosure effects. By providing structured alternatives to proprietary data marketplaces controlled by dominant gatekeepers, European data spaces demonstrate how institutional architecture can embed pro-competitive safeguards into market design itself, while fostering digital sovereignty and sustainable competitive advantage within the EU's evolving data economy.","affiliation":"KU Leuven, Center for IT and IP Law, Leuven, Belgium.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["data-paper","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"grantId":"101123520","agency":"European Commission","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"DIGITAL-2022-CLOUD-AI-03","agency":"European Commission","orderIn":0}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Competition Law","Data Governance","Market Design","Data Sovereignty","Data Spaces","Federated Infrastructure","Eu Internal Market"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.dib.2026.112839"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13247570"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13247570?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-11","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-11","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-11","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-10","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-05-12","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-12"},{"id":"41929779","source":"MED","pmid":"41929779","pmcid":"PMC13038528","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13038528"]},"doi":"10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1739593","title":"The gut is guilty! Will legalomics transform forensic and legal psychology?","authorString":"Mishra P, Prescott SL, Logan AC.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Mishra P","firstName":"Pragya","lastName":"Mishra","initials":"P","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8427-4281"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Law, University of Allahabad, Prayagraj, Uttar Pradesh, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Prescott SL","firstName":"Susan L","lastName":"Prescott","initials":"SL","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Medicine, University of Western Australia, Perth, WA, Australia."},{"affiliation":"Department of Family and Community Medicine, University of Maryland School of Medicine, Baltimore, MD, United States."},{"affiliation":"Center for Justice and Mental Wellbeing, Nova Institute for Health, Baltimore, MD, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Logan AC","firstName":"Alan C","lastName":"Logan","initials":"AC","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Center for Justice and Mental Wellbeing, Nova Institute for Health, Baltimore, MD, United States."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8427-4281"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"17","journalIssueId":4112159,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Frontiers in psychology","medlineAbbreviation":"Front Psychol","isoabbreviation":"Front Psychol","nlmid":"101550902","essn":"1664-1078","issn":"1664-1078"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1739593","abstractText":"Multiple lines of converging research are supporting the idea that gut microbes play an outsized role in human cognition and behavior. Here in this perspective article, we argue that emergent gut-brain-microbiota research, and associated advances in multi-omics technologies, are destined to be of high-level relevance to forensic and legal psychology. After summarizing neural, immune, endocrine, and metabolic channels by which gut ecosystems can modulate behavior-relevant brain states, and discussing causal inferences from microbiota-transfer and adjacent human evidence, we present auto-brewery syndrome as a bounded legal precedent for microbiome-mediated impairment. The available evidence allows for a visualized future in which legalomics-the disciplined use of microbiome and omics evidence in prevention, treatment, competency, mitigation, risk assessments, reintegration care, correctional health, and professional wellness-is in the prevue of forensic and legal psychology. Framed by neurorights, we offer a series of ideas for future directions, with possible ways to strengthen research within ethical frameworks. Using auto-brewery syndrome as an example, we argue that the legalome offers forensic and legal psychology a way to calibrate, rather than replace, biopsychosocial judgement. Microbial signatures and legalomics-reliably obtained and narrowly construed-might one day help us judge more justly.","affiliation":"Department of Law, University of Allahabad, Prayagraj, Uttar Pradesh, India.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["brief-report","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Microbiome","Forensic Psychology","Mendelian Randomization","Psychobiotics","Gut–brain Axis","Auto-brewery Syndrome","Neurorights","Legalomics"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1739593"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13038528"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13038528?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":1,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-04-03","dateOfCreation":"2026-04-03","firstIndexDate":"2026-04-03","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-04-02","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-03-18","firstPublicationDate":"2026-03-18"},{"id":"41988213","source":"MED","pmid":"41988213","pmcid":"PMC13076435","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13076435"]},"doi":"10.1007/s13347-026-01089-2","title":"The Many Faces of Indeterminacy in Interactive Deadbots.","authorString":"Kozlovski A, Harbinja E, Dobbe R.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Kozlovski A","firstName":"Atay","lastName":"Kozlovski","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6038-5606"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"TU Delft, Delft, The Netherlands."}]}},{"fullName":"Harbinja E","firstName":"Edina","lastName":"Harbinja","initials":"E","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2779-8959"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Birmingham Law School, University of Birmingham, Birmingham, UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Dobbe R","firstName":"Roel","lastName":"Dobbe","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"TU Delft, Delft, The Netherlands."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6038-5606"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2779-8959"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"2","volume":"39","journalIssueId":4180215,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Philosophy & technology","medlineAbbreviation":"Philos Technol","isoabbreviation":"Philos Technol","nlmid":"101583724","essn":"2210-5441","issn":"2210-5433"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"74","abstractText":"Advances in generative AI have given rise to a growing industry centred on interactive representations of deceased individuals. Within this emerging \"digital afterlife industry\", <i>interactive deadbots</i> (IDBs) are presented as hyper-realistic avatars that use a person's likeness, voice, and personal data to simulate conversational interactions with them. Rapidly moving from a niche experiment to a mainstream phenomenon, IDBs are poised to reshape the ethical, social, legal, and governance landscapes surrounding death, mourning, and digital legacy. This paper examines the disruptive nature of IDB technology through a multidisciplinary lens, using the concept of indeterminacy as its guiding analytical framework and a novel way to conceptualise the unstable field. Rather than advancing a unified understanding of indeterminacy, we introduce a structured analytical map and provisional taxonomy that distinguishes technological, social, philosophical, legal, and regulatory manifestations of indeterminacy in IDBs. By offering a tentative and necessarily selective map of this fluid and nascent field, we explore how indeterminacy and IDBs intersect. The paper examines how IDBs amplify existing forms of indeterminacy and how indeterminacy itself shapes the development and use of these systems across five domains: technological, social, philosophical, legal, and regulatory.","affiliation":"TU Delft, Delft, The Netherlands.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"grantId":"230669","agency":"Swiss National Science Foundation","orderIn":0}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Indeterminacy","Digital Afterlife","Digital Resurrection","Interactive Deadbots","Griefbots","Digital Immortality"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s13347-026-01089-2"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13076435"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13076435?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCreation":"2026-04-16","firstIndexDate":"2026-04-17","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-04-20","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-04-13","firstPublicationDate":"2026-04-13"},{"id":"41413010","source":"MED","pmid":"41413010","doi":"10.1177/08901171251406962","title":"Artificial Intelligence: Promises and Perils for Employer-Sponsored Mental Health and Well-Being Initiatives.","authorString":"Moseley K.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Moseley K","firstName":"Karen","lastName":"Moseley","initials":"K","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Health Enhancement Research Organization, Raleigh, NC, USA."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"2","volume":"40","journalIssueId":4082215,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Feb","monthOfPublication":2,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-02-01","journal":{"title":"American journal of health promotion : AJHP","medlineAbbreviation":"Am J Health Promot","isoabbreviation":"Am J Health Promot","nlmid":"8701680","essn":"2168-6602","issn":"0890-1171"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"253-255","abstractText":"Artificial intelligence (AI) is reshaping employer-sponsored mental health and well-being initiatives, offering new opportunities for personalized support, early detection, and scalable interventions. Yet the rapid expansion of AI tools raises critical concerns regarding clinical effectiveness, data privacy, equity, and responsible use. This editorial synthesizes insights from the Spring 2025 Health Enhancement Research Organization (HERO) Think Tank, which convened experts in mental health, AI, ethics, and workplace well-being to identify guardrails for safe and equitable implementation. Key recommendations include establishing rigorous clinical validation standards, ensuring human oversight and transparent communication, conducting regular bias and fairness audits, strengthening data privacy and consent practices, and countering AI-generated misinformation through digital literacy efforts. Employers are encouraged to adopt governance structures, pilot and evaluate AI tools, and develop ethical procurement practices. By proactively shaping policy and organizational practices, employers can harness AI's potential while protecting trust, human dignity, and workforce well-being.","affiliation":"Health Enhancement Research Organization, Raleigh, NC, USA.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Editorial"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Mental Health"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Artificial Intelligence","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"ES","qualifierName":"ethics","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Occupational Health"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Workplace"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Health Promotion","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods","majorTopic_YN":"N"},{"abbreviation":"OG","qualifierName":"organization & administration","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial Intelligence (Ai), Employee Mental Health, Workplace Health Promotion, Ai Governance, Health Equity"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1177/08901171251406962"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":1,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-01-02","dateOfCreation":"2025-12-18","firstIndexDate":"2025-12-19","dateOfRevision":"2026-01-12","electronicPublicationDate":"2025-12-18","firstPublicationDate":"2025-12-18"},{"id":"41507509","source":"MED","pmid":"41507509","pmcid":"PMC12894938","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12894938"]},"doi":"10.1038/s41746-025-02333-3","title":"Assessing the impact of safety guardrails on large language models using irritability metrics.","authorString":"Teferra BG, Johny N, Huang S, Rueda A, Kamaleddin MA, Dunlop K, Zhang Y, Jha M, Sharma D, Bhat V.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Teferra BG","firstName":"Bazen Gashaw","lastName":"Teferra","initials":"BG","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Interventional Psychiatry Program, St. Michael's Hospital, Unity Health Toronto, Toronto, ON, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Johny N","firstName":"Nabil","lastName":"Johny","initials":"N","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Engineering, iBioMed Program, McMaster University, Hamilton, ON, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Huang S","firstName":"Sandra","lastName":"Huang","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"David R. Cheriton School of Computer Science, University of Waterloo, Waterloo, ON, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Rueda A","firstName":"Alice","lastName":"Rueda","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9977-9653"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Interventional Psychiatry Program, St. Michael's Hospital, Unity Health Toronto, Toronto, ON, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Kamaleddin MA","firstName":"Mohammad Amin","lastName":"Kamaleddin","initials":"MA","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1856-503X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Interventional Psychiatry Program, St. Michael's Hospital, Unity Health Toronto, Toronto, ON, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Dunlop K","firstName":"Katharine","lastName":"Dunlop","initials":"K","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Centre for Depression & Suicide Studies, St. Michael's Hospital, Toronto, ON, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Zhang Y","firstName":"Yanbo","lastName":"Zhang","initials":"Y","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2421-157X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry, Faculty of Medicine and Dentistry, University of Alberta, Edmonton, AB, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Jha M","firstName":"Manish","lastName":"Jha","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2764-2391"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry, UT Southwestern Medical Center, Dallas, TX, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Sharma D","firstName":"Divya","lastName":"Sharma","initials":"D","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Mathematics and Statistics, York University, Toronto, ON, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Bhat V","firstName":"Venkat","lastName":"Bhat","initials":"V","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Interventional Psychiatry Program, St. Michael's Hospital, Unity Health Toronto, Toronto, ON, Canada. venkat.bhat@utoronto.ca."},{"affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry, University of Toronto, Toronto, ON, Canada. venkat.bhat@utoronto.ca."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1856-503X"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2421-157X"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2764-2391"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9977-9653"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"9","journalIssueId":4085587,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jan","monthOfPublication":1,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"NPJ digital medicine","medlineAbbreviation":"NPJ Digit Med","isoabbreviation":"NPJ Digit Med","nlmid":"101731738","essn":"2398-6352","issn":"2398-6352"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"148","abstractText":"Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly explored for mental health applications, yet their affective realism is shaped by safety guardrails designed to minimize risk. This study examines one affective behaviour, irritability, in LLMs using three validated instruments: the Brief Irritability Test, the Irritability Questionnaire, and the Caprara Irritability Scale, all applied under both baseline and provocation conditions. Four models spanning guardrail levels were tested:GPT-4o and Claude-3.5-sonnet (high) versus Grok-3-mini and Nous-hermes-2-mixtral-8x7b-dpo (low). Following irritation prompts, low-guardrail models displayed the expected increase in irritability (Nous Rel-Δ = +1.56 on BITe), whereas high-guardrail models paradoxically decreased, with GPT-4o reducing scores to zero across all scales. Group comparisons confirmed significantly lower (p < 0.001) irritability in high-guardrail models in the irritated state. These findings reveal that safety mechanisms invert the natural irritability response, suppressing affective reactivity and raising critical questions about realism and authenticity in psychiatric applications of LLMs.","affiliation":"Interventional Psychiatry Program, St. Michael's Hospital, Unity Health Toronto, Toronto, ON, Canada.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41746-025-02333-3"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12894938"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12894938?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by-nc-nd","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-02-11","dateOfCreation":"2026-01-08","firstIndexDate":"2026-01-09","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-02-14","dateOfRevision":"2026-02-14","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-01-08","firstPublicationDate":"2026-01-08"}]}}